    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189416": {
      "title": "General Internal Medicine Clinician-Educator – Assistant/Associate Professor of Medicine at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - General Medicine Location: 02 The Department of Medicine, Division of General Medicine at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center in New York City seeks a general internist in the clinical-educator track in ambulatory care at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. Successful applicants must have clinical teaching experience in a comparable setting and a substantive interest in general internal medicine. Learn more at https://www.genmed.columbia.edu/patient-care/associates-internal-medicine-vanderbilt-practice Minimum Degree: M.D. or equivalent medical professional degree Minimum Qualifications: Must be board certified or board eligible in Internal Medicine and must be eligible to practice in the State of New York. Preferred: Spanish language proficiency Assistant Professor of Medicine at CUMC: $234,000-$250,000 Associate Professor of Medicine at CUMC: $236,000-$250,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189439": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist in Applied Mathematics / Scientific Computing",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics Location: 01 We are inviting applications for an associate research scientist position in applied mathematics and scientific computing, with an emphasis on inverse problems in seismology and Bayesian analysis. The position is associated with the Modeling Earth from Atomic to Global Scale (MINERALS) project funded by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation. The project aims to advance and integrate three branches of geophysics – mineral physics, seismology, and geodynamics – to analyze and interpret the deep-Earth seismological structure. The successful candidate will work in a multidisciplinary team of senior and junior scientists to develop machine learning and Bayesian methods to interpret seismic tomographic models of the elastic structure of the deep Earth in terms of temperature and mineralogy. Using thermodynamic properties databases, these analytical methods will also be applied to predict equilibrium mineral assemblages in collaboration with an international team of geochemists and petrologists. The position is initially for one year, possibly extending to three years. The position is available immediately and will remain open until filled. Applicants should send a CV, three letters of recommendation, and a cover letter that includes a research statement summarizing their past experience and future goals. Applications should be submitted through the Columbia Interfolio website. Candidates must have a PhD in scientific computing, applied math, mineral physics, geophysics, or materials science with a minimum of 2 years of relevant post-PhD experience.  Salary starting at $79,327. Applicants should send a CV, three letters of recommendation, and a cover letter that includes a research statement summarizing their past experience and future goals. Applications should be submitted through the Columbia Interfolio website."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189489": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Adjunct Professor - Economics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Economics Location: 06 The Graduate School of Business at Columbia University invites applications for Adjunct Professors of Economics to teach electives during the 2027 Spring term. The position is part-time and non-tenure-track.  Please note, our part-time faculty positions are posted based on anticipated need and student enrollment. We are seeking three adjuncts — one for each of the following electives: Economics of High-performing Organizations — topics such as incentives, decision-making, information, organizational design, and firm performance, with emphasis on translating economic insights into actionable strategies relevant for managers and executives Economics of Platforms, AI and Tech — topics such as platforms, AI, and the business models employed by tech firms, bringing a rigorous yet practical perspective to real-world business challenges. Economics of Healthcare and/or Pharma — the economics of the healthcare and pharmaceutical sectors, connecting economic frameworks to real-world business challenges. For each position, candidates must have substantial industry experience applying economic frameworks to real-world business challenges. We are particularly interested in candidates who can bridge economic theory and business practice, and who bring firsthand insight into how organizations operate in complex, real-world environments. Adjunct faculty at CBS play an essential role in delivering rigorous, analytically grounded instruction that connects economic theory to real-world business, financial, and policy contexts. The successful candidate will contribute to CBS’s mission of advancing the understanding and practice of economics in the global economy. Responsibilities Teach at least one course in the MBA program Design and deliver high-quality, engaging class sessions Integrate economic frameworks with real-world business applications Draw on industry experience to enrich classroom discussion Prepare syllabi, assignments, and assessments in accordance with CBS academic standards Evaluate student performance and provide timely, constructive feedback Hold office hours and actively engage with students Coordinate with CBS faculty and comply with university and school policies Contribute to the intellectual life of the Economics Division Appointment Details Appointment type: Part-time, non-tenure-track Compensation: Competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, consistent with Columbia Business School guidelines Location: On-campus in New York City If you have any questions, please email econsearch@gsb.columbia.edu . Required : Terminal degree (PhD preferred) or master’s degree in economics or a closely related field Demonstrated industry experience in a relevant sector. Preferred: Prior teaching experience at the graduate or undergraduate level Commitment to inclusive, high-impact teaching Applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing teaching experience, research or professional background, and interest in the position, indicating which elective(s) you are applying to teach Teaching statement and/or sample syllabus ( if available, please add to the a dditional documents section) Contact information for 2 references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189529": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I - Viny Lab",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Hematology and Oncology Location: 02 Staff Associate I The Division of Hematology & Oncology is seeking a highly motivated Staff Associate I to join Dr. Aaron Viny's research laboratory. The successful candidate will support cutting-edge translational research focused on hematologic malignancies through laboratory automation, clinical sample processing, and molecular profiling. This position plays a key role in developing and optimizing automated laboratory workflows, processing clinical research specimens, and collaborating with investigators across Columbia University Irving Medical Center and external academic and industry partners. The Staff Associate will work both independently and collaboratively to advance research projects while maintaining high standards of scientific rigor and laboratory operations. Responsibilities Operate, maintain, and optimize automated liquid handling systems and laboratory robotics. Translate manual laboratory protocols into automated workflows. Develop, troubleshoot, and optimize laboratory automation scripts. Process clinical research specimens for genomic, chromatin, and epigenetic profiling. Perform laboratory experiments supporting translational and clinical research studies. Assist with sample tracking, quality control, and data documentation. Maintain laboratory equipment and coordinate routine maintenance. Ensure compliance with institutional biosafety, laboratory safety, and regulatory requirements. Maintain laboratory inventories and coordinate procurement of research supplies. Collaborate with principal investigators, laboratory staff, and external collaborators to support ongoing research initiatives. Participate in laboratory meetings and contribute to the continuous improvement of laboratory workflows. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications : Bachelor's Degree, Molecular Biology, Biochemistry, Biomedical Engineering, or a related scientific discipline. Four years of related laboratory experience preferred or 2 years with Masters Degree. Experience with laboratory automation and liquid handling systems. Experience processing clinical or biological specimens. Demonstrated ability to work independently while managing multiple projects. Preferred Qualifications Experience with Biomek liquid handling systems or comparable laboratory automation platforms. Experience writing or modifying automation scripts. Experience with chromatin, epigenetics, genomics, or next-generation sequencing workflows. Experience supporting translational or clinical research laboratories. Familiarity with laboratory information management systems (LIMS)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189530": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Scientist is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center is one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers. The Department of Pediatrics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is amongst the leading pediatric departments in the New York region and NIH research funding. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES As a Postdoctoral Research Scientist in our program, the candidate will conduct research on viral infection and pathogenesis, with a particular focus on the mechanisms of viral entry and membrane fusion mediated by coronaviruses, mumps virus, and measles virus. Investigate the mechanisms of action and structural determinants of antiviral peptides, small molecules, and monoclonal antibodies, with an emphasis on the structural characterization of viral and host proteins involved in membrane fusion and viral entry. Apply advanced biochemical, structural biology, and cryo–electron microscopy (cryo-EM) approaches to characterize viral glycoproteins, host receptors, and antiviral therapeutics. Perform mammalian cell culture and baculovirus-based protein expression, including transient transfection and viral expression systems. Conduct membrane protein purification, biochemical characterization, cryo-EM sample preparation, data collection, image processing, and structural analysis. Collaborate closely with investigators at Columbia University Irving Medical Center and the CUNY Advanced Science Research Center (ASRC) on multidisciplinary research projects. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS Must have a doctorate degree (M.D. or Ph.D.)in virology, structural biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, biophysics, or a related field. Extensive training and hands-on experience in biochemistry and cryo-electron microscopy techniques. Demonstrated experience with mammalian cell culture, baculovirus-based protein expression systems, membrane protein purification, and cryo-EM sample preparation. Experience with cryo-EM data collection, image processing, and structural analysis. Strong record of scientific productivity, as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with the ability to establish and maintain productive working relationships across disciplines. Ability to contribute to a dynamic, high-performing research environment that values scientific rigor, innovation, and teamwork. Commitment to mentoring, collaboration, and fostering a positive and inclusive laboratory culture. Ability to recognize, support, and celebrate the contributions and successes of colleagues and trainees. PREFERRED EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS Experience working with viral glycoproteins, membrane fusion proteins, or host-pathogen interactions. Experience with antibody characterization, protein engineering, or antiviral drug discovery."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189535": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor of Pathology and Cell Biology at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pathology Location: 02 The Department of Pathology and Cell Biology at Columbia University is seeking a Director of the Cancer Cytogenetics Laboratory. This role will be appointed either as a Associate Professor or Assistant Professor. The position will provide clinical, scientific, and operational leadership for a comprehensive academic cancer cytogenetics program while advancing integrated genomic diagnostics across oncology and constitutional testing. Working closely with the Director of Cytogenetics, the Vice Chair of the Division of Genomic Pathology, laboratory leadership, and multidisciplinary clinical teams, this individual will oversee complex test interpretation, lead assay development and implementation, ensure regulatory excellence, and expand precision medicine services. The successful candidate will be an accomplished clinical laboratory leader with expertise in cytogenetics and molecular genomics, a collaborative leadership style, and a strong commitment to innovation, education, quality, and translational research in an academic medical center. Job Function : Direct the clinical operations of the Cancer Cytogenetics Laboratory within a high-volume academic medical center. Provide authoritative interpretation and sign-out of complex cytogenetic, cytogenomic, and molecular genetic testing, including chromosome analysis, FISH, copy number analysis, genomic microarray, sequencing, and other genomic assays for both oncology and constitutional disorders. Partner with the Director of Cytogenetics and Director of the Personalized Genomics Medicine Laboratory to ensure excellence across the full spectrum of constitutional and cancer cytogenetic testing. Collaborate with the Vice Chair of the Division of Clinical Pathology/Genomic Pathology to develop and implement an integrated strategy for genomic diagnostics across the Department. Serve as a key consultant to oncologists, hematopathologists, medical geneticists, genetic counselors, and multidisciplinary clinical teams regarding appropriate test utilization and interpretation of genomic findings. Participate in tumor boards, multidisciplinary conferences, and other clinical consultative activities. Laboratory Leadership and Innovation Lead the development, validation, implementation, and optimization of new cytogenetic and molecular genomic assays, with particular emphasis on precision oncology. Evaluate and implement emerging genomic technologies and testing platforms to expand clinical capabilities. Provide scientific and technical leadership to faculty, laboratory managers, supervisors, and technologists while fostering a collaborative culture of quality and continuous improvement. Oversee laboratory quality systems and ensure compliance with CAP, CLIA, New York State, and other applicable regulatory and accreditation requirements. Represent the laboratory during inspections and accreditation activities. Education Teach and mentor pathology residents, Molecular Genetic Pathology fellows, Laboratory Genetics and Genomics trainees, medical students, and laboratory personnel. Contribute to the Department’s educational mission through lectures, case conferences, and curriculum development. Collaborate closely with the Surgical Pathology, Hematopathology, and Genomic Pathology training programs. Research and Academic Scholarship Develop collaborative clinical and translational research initiatives in cancer genomics and genomic pathology. Publish in peer-reviewed journals and present scholarly work at regional and national scientific meetings. Participate actively in departmental, institutional, and national professional organizations and committees. Lab Mission/Purpose: The Cytogenetics and Personalized Genomics Medicine Laboratories provide a comprehensive test menu supporting oncology, hematologic malignancies, pediatrics, reproductive genetics, prenatal diagnosis, transplant medicine, and other clinical specialties. Current methodologies include conventional cytogenetics, FISH, SNP microarray, next-generation sequencing (NGS), and other advanced genomic technologies. Minimum Education Requirements: M.D., Ph.D., or equivalent doctoral degree. ABMGG certification (or board eligibility) in Laboratory Genetics and Genomics (LGG), or certification/eligibility in Clinical Cytogenetics and Genomics with appropriate molecular genetics expertise. Candidates with an M.D. should have appropriate pathology training and board certification/eligibility consistent with the position. Minimum Skills Required: Demonstrated expertise in constitutional and cancer cytogenetics, including chromosome analysis, FISH, copy number analysis, and genomic interpretation. Demonstrated expertise in constitutional and cancer molecular genetics, including sequencing, molecular assays, and genomic interpretation. Experience developing, validating, and implementing molecular and genomic assays within a CAP/CLIA-accredited clinical laboratory. Knowledge of applicable regulatory and accreditation standards governing clinical genomic laboratories. Excellent communication skills and a demonstrated commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration, education, professionalism, and inclusive leadership. Preferred Qualifications : Prior experience directing or co-directing a clinical cytogenetics or molecular diagnostics laboratory in an academic medical center. Expertise in precision oncology, cancer genomics, and integrated genomic diagnostics. Record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications, national presentations, and successful clinical or translational research collaborations. Demonstrated success building collaborative relationships with clinicians, scientists, and institutional leadership. Hiring Salary Range: $260,000 to $320,000 Assistant Professor at CUMC: $260,000-$300,500 Associate Professor at CUMC: $270,000-$320,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189572": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Zuckerman Institute Location: 06 Columbia University's Mortimer B. Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute unites world-class scientists across diverse academic disciplines to conduct groundbreaking research that transforms our understanding of the brain and its influence on the mind and behavior. As a leading neuroscience institute, our mission is to decipher the mind and brain to better understand behavior. Our researchers are committed to foundational science, uncovering the principles that shape how the brain develops, functions, and recovers. With more than 50 labs, the Zuckerman Institute serves as a hub for collaboration, bringing together Columbia’s top scholars to drive research in bold new directions. Located in Manhattanville, our state-of-the-art Jerome L. Greene Science Center provides an inspiring home for discovery in one of the world’s most dynamic cities. We are seeking an Associate Research Scientist to lead and contribute to innovative research investigating the neural mechanisms underlying visual perception and visual information processing. The successful candidate must possess demonstrated expertise in computational neuroscience, cognitive neuroscience, visual perception, and experimental neuroscience research methodologies. Experience investigating the neural mechanisms underlying visual feature conjunctions and visual information processing is required. The candidate should have experience developing and implementing deep neural network models of visual processing, designing and conducting psychophysical and functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) experiments, and analyzing complex behavioral and neuroimaging datasets. Strong quantitative, computational, and statistical analysis skills, including programming and data modeling, are essential. Experience collaborating within multidisciplinary research teams, contributing to methodological innovations, preparing scientific publications, and assisting with grant proposal development is highly desirable. PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Neuroscience, Neurobiology, Biomedical Engineering, Computational Neuroscience, Psychology, Biology, Computer Science, Data Science, or a closely related scientific discipline."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189588": {
      "title": "Buell Center Research and Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Graduate School of Architecture, Planning and Preservation Location: 01 The Buell Center at Columbia University seeks a recent doctoral recipient to join its intellectual community for a 21-month fellowship (two academic years and an intervening summer) as a Buell Center Research and Teaching Fellow. Ideal candidates will be scholars of the built environment who are beginning an academic career of research and teaching, with a growing record of original writing intended for peer-reviewed publication. Complementary experience, such as design, curatorial, critical, or fourth-purpose organizing work, is welcome but not required. The 21-month fellowship will begin September 1, 2027, and is intended to give Fellows a chance to advance their own research, gain teaching experience, and take part in the ongoing intellectual life of the Buell Center; the Graduate School of Architecture, Planning, and Preservation (GSAPP); and Columbia University. The Fellow will be co-hosted by the Society of Fellows and Heyman Center for the Humanities . The Fellow is expected to be in residence at Columbia for two academic years and will remain a Fellow in the intervening summer, but is not required to be in residence. In addition to presenting their research once yearly in a formal setting, Fellows will teach at least one semester as faculty in GSAPP’s architectural history survey course, “Questions in Architectural History” (QAH). The Fellow may also propose to teach a separate course within GSAPP based on their own research. The Temple Hoyne Buell Center for the Study of American Architecture is a separately endowed entity within Columbia GSAPP , which sponsors interdisciplinary research through projects, workshops, public programming, publications, and awards. A recent PhD degree in Architecture or a related field is required by the start date of the appointment. PhDs granted within the last three years prior to the appointment start date are acceptable. Successful candidates: - must have a PhD conferred within three years of—and no later than—the start date of the appointment, i.e., the date of the degree should be between September 1, 2024, and September 1, 2027. - must demonstrate an interest in pushing the disciplinary or methodological norms of architecture and related fields of the built environment - should have a growing record of bridging across disciplines in the humanities or social sciences - may be focused on any historical period and geographic area in their research. Interested applicants should submit: a CV; a cover letter; a research statement (1,500 words), dissertation abstract (150 words), one-sentence project description, one to three images (on one page); a teaching statement (500 words); at least one writing sample; and the names of three references. Recommendation letters will be solicited for shortlisted applicants. For further information about the Buell Center and FAQ about the fellowship, please visit: buellcenter.columbia.edu, You may also reach the Buell Center at buellcenter@columbia.edu. Please visit our online application site at https://apply.interfolio.com/189588 for further information about this position and to submit your application. Review of applications will begin on October 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189617": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medical Psychology (in Psychiatry) - CUCARD-W",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 There are immediate openings for licensed clinical psychologists at the CUIMC Faculty Practice Organization (FPO) in Manhattan. The Department of Psychiatry is seeking psychologists assigned to work in the CUIMC FPO. These are full-time, clinical positions in the Columbia Day Program. Primary responsibilities involve conducting group therapy utilizing empirically supported treatments, diagnostic/intake evaluations of adults, and individual treatment planning and progress monitoring with patients. Candidates should have previous experience providing outpatient treatment and working with a multi-disciplinary team. Experience and training in Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) preferred. Candidates with additional experience in evidence-supported treatment delivery for trauma and/or substance use are desired. Ideal applicants will have experience, familiarity and comfort working with high-risk patients, as well as young adults and college students. Applicants should have strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work well across disciplines and function effectively as part of a multi disciplinary team. The CUIMC FPO sees a mix of private pay and managed care patients and has a particular focus on serving the Columbia medical center community, among others. We prioritize evidence-based interventions and have an array of specialized programs to best serve our patients. All positions currently allow for hybrid work, and for this position in-person work will take place on the main medical center campus in Washington Heights. Our faculty serve as part of multi-disciplinary teams with ample opportunities to learn from and consult with colleagues. There are also professional development funds available to faculty to support licensure renewals, as well as obtaining additional training, conference attendance, professional organization members, among other opportunities. The CUIMC FPO is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive faculty and clinical service. We recognize that different backgrounds and experiences help to strengthen our department and provide better care. As part of your cover letter, if you choose, we welcome you to share how your personal and professional backgrounds would contribute to our department and mission. 1. Candidate(s) will conduct intake evaluations/diagnostic assessments of patients with psychiatric disorders and psychological distress. 2. Candidate(s) will provide group psychotherapy. 3. Candidate(s) will work closely with a clinical team, including other psychologists/social workers, psychiatrists and psychiatric nurse-practitioners. 4. Candidate(s) will have opportunities to attend case conferences, peer supervision and Grand Rounds at CUMC. Opportunities may exist for clinical supervision and teaching. Minimum Educational Requirements: PsyD or PhD in Clinical Psychology New York State license required. Candidates will be required to obtain a New Jersey license. Graduation from an APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology, and completion of an APA-accredited pre doctoral internship program."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189618": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Reproductive Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: 02 The Suh Lab is seeking an Assistant Professor of Research with expertise in computational genomics, bioinformatics, and biomedical informatics/AI/ML to lead the analysis of large-scale genomic datasets. The candidate will direct the application of contemporary computational methods, including predictive artificial intelligence and machine learning frameworks, to address biological questions related to aging, including reproductive aging, and aging-related diseases. This role requires a leader who can utilize cutting-edge next-generation sequencing (NGS) technologies and integrate diverse data resources to generate and test novel biological hypotheses. The successful candidate will be responsible for the computational strategy across several NIH-funded grants and collaborative projects. Job Responsibilities: ​​ Computational Strategy: Lead the development and application of specialized methods for analyzing genomic and epigenomic data, including but not limited to ChIP-seq, RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, and other high-throughput sequencing method, as well as the implementation of custom AI/ML pipelines for pattern discovery. Data Integration & Analysis: Collect and interpret publicly available data, including but not limited to transcriptomic/proteomic databases, genomic annotation hubs, and GWAS datasets from relevant studies using advanced informatics approaches and multi-omics data integration tools. Hypothesis Generation: Generate novel hypotheses from the analysis and integration of internal and external data resources using predictive modeling to drive the lab's research strategies. Grant Writing & Application: Play a central role in the preparation, writing, and submission of grant proposals by providing critical data analyses, developing computational and informaticsresearch plans, and crafting the methodology and results sections for applications. Technical Authority: Maintain comprehensive knowledge of NGS data analysis flows, including but not limited to WGS/WES, scRNA-seq, 4C, and HiC, as well as modern machine learning libraries and AI tools, to ensure the lab remains at the forefront of the field. Independent Leadership & Mentorship: Exhibit independent thinking in a highly dynamic environment and provide guidance to junior lab members on computational and informatics/AI/ML methodologies. Minimum Qualifications: PhD in bioinformatics, computational biology, biology, biomedical informatics, computer science, or another appropriate field. Extensive experience with NGS data analysis and proficiency in R , Python, and one or more other programming languages commonly used in data science and machine learning applications. A distinguished record of high-impact publications and a proven track record of independent scientific thinking and creative problem-solving. Deep knowledge of the human genome, gene expression, cell biology and machine learning architectures. Preferred qualifications: Prior experience or a strong interest in aging-related research and biology. Demonstrated experience applying deep learning or machine learning models to large-scale biological datasets (e.g., single-cell multi-omics or predictive clinical genetics. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is dedicated to the goal of building a multicultural faculty and staff committed to teaching, working and serving in a diverse community, and strongly encourages applications from candidates of traditionally underrepresented backgrounds. We are continuously seeking to recruit individuals who will enhance the diversity of our workplace and the effectiveness of our organization."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189620": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medical Psychology (in Psychiatry) - Gender and Sexuality",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Department of Psychiatry is seeking a psychologist to join the Gender and Sexuality Program in the Faculty Practice Organization at CUIMC. The Columbia Gender and Sexuality Program is comprised of a diverse and multidisciplinary team of experts devoted to serving the needs of transgender, nonbinary, and gender expansive children, adolescents, adults, and family members. Additionally, our program serves the needs of the LGB community. This is a full time, clinical position that will involve seeing children, adolescents/adults, and their families. As the position will include direct clinical care, candidates should have demonstrated interest and expertise in working with sexual and gender minorities, including children, adolescents, and families. The Columbia Gender and Sexuality Program works closely with the Program for the Study of LGBTQ+ Health, allowing for several opportunities for scholarship and research collaboration. Candidates with experience in Dialectical Behavior Therapy are especially encouraged to apply. Applicants should have strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work well across disciplines and function within a multi-disciplinary team. The CUIMC FPO sees a mix of private pay and managed care patients and has a particular focus on serving the Columbia Irving Medical Center community, among others. We prioritize evidence-based interventions and have an array of specialized programs to best serve our patients. All positions currently allow for hybrid work, with on-site time spent in our Columbus Circle offices in Midtown Manhattan or on the medical campus in Washington Heights. Our faculty serves as part of multi-disciplinary teams with ample opportunities to learn from and consult with colleagues. There are also professional development funds available to faculty to support licensure renewals, as well as obtaining additional training, conference attendance, and professional organization members, among other opportunities. As a department, we are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive faculty and clinical service. We recognize that different backgrounds and experiences help to strengthen our department and provide better care. As part of your cover letter, if you choose, we welcome you to share how your personal and professional backgrounds would contribute to our department and mission. Candidate(s) will conduct comprehensive intake evaluations of gender diverse individuals using a model of gender affirmation; candidate will also have skill in evaluating psychiatric disorders and psychological distress. Candidate(s) will provide group and individual psychotherapy. Candidate(s) will work closely with a multidisciplinary clinical team, including other psychologists/social workers, psychiatrists, and psychiatric nurse practitioners. Candidate(s) will have opportunities to attend case conferences, peer supervision and Grand Rounds at CUIMC. Opportunities may exist for clinical supervision and teaching. Minimum Educational Requirements: PsyD or PhD in Clinical Psychology New York State license required. Candidates will be required to obtain a New Jersey license. Graduation from an APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology, and completion of an APA-accredited pre doctoral internship program."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189638": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medical Psychology (in Psychiatry) - Pediatric Neuropsych",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 Assistant Professor level positions in the Department of Psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC). There are immediate openings for pediatric neuropsychologists at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center Faculty Practice Organization offices in NYC. The Department of Psychiatry is seeking neuropsychologists assigned to work in the Faculty Practice Organization at CUIMC. These are full-time, clinical positions in an outpatient setting. Primary responsibilities involve conducting comprehensive neuropsychological evaluations and providing associated recommendations for children, adolescents and young adults. Ideal applicants should have previous experience conducting neuropsychological assessment, including completion of a two-year postdoctoral fellowship in neuropsychology, strong interpersonal skills, and the ability to work well across disciplines and function effectively as part of a multi-disciplinary team. Additional preference for candidates with experience and training in adult-focused neuropsychology. Candidates will have opportunities to attend case conferences, peer supervision, and Grand Rounds at CUIMC. Candidates will work closely with a clinical team, including other neuropsychologists, psychologists/social workers, psychiatrists and psychiatric nurse-practitioners. Opportunities may exist for clinical supervision and teaching. As a department, we are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive faculty and clinical service. We recognize that different backgrounds and experiences help to strengthen our department and provide better care. As part of your cover letter, if you choose, we welcome you to share how your personal and professional backgrounds would contribute to our department and mission. Minimum Educational Requirements: PsyD or PhD in Clinical or School Psychology Completion of a two-year Neuropsychology Postdoctoral Fellowship. NYS license required. Candidates will be required to obtain a New Jersey license Instructor level requires graduation from an APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology, and completion of an APA accredited pre-doctoral internship program. Assistant Professor level requires 2-3 years clinical experience in a hospital or private practice setting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189656": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Institute for Human Nutrition Location: 02 The Staff Associate I will support research on the neural and molecular mechanisms underlying feeding behavior, energy balance, glucose homeostasis, and obesity-related metabolic disorders. The role will contribute to studies using inducible neuron-specific genetic mouse models to investigate lactate metabolism and cellular redox regulation in hypothalamic neurons. Responsibilities include mouse colony management, genotyping, tamoxifen induction, metabolic phenotyping, tissue collection & other procedures. Duties and responsibilities: Knowledge of the research subject and scientific methods of analysis (30%) Utilizes analytical skills to solve moderately complex problems based on guidelines, protocols and procedures (20%) Works within guidelines to carry out assigned duties in the design of apparatus, the adaptation of relevant technology to the needs of the project, the conduct of specific experiments and the analysis of the data (10%) Ability to operate and maintain equipment, computers, software programs and research (20%) Shares information and confirmation of data (20%) Bachelor's degree and 4 years of professional work experience preferred. Knowledge of handling mice and conducting metabolism-related experiments"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189664": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate / Professor of Neurological Surgery at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurological Surgery Location: 02 Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons (CUVCPS) has commenced a search for a nationally recognized physician/scientist to serve at the rank of Assistant / Associate / Professor in the Department of Neurological Surgery. This position is a full-time faculty appointment as an Assistant Professor in Neurological Surgery at Columbia University Medical Center with admitting privileges at one or more of Columbia’s affiliated/partner institutions. Candidate should be proficient in resident training and have strong research interests. Responsibilities will include direct patient care, the supervision and teaching of house staff (residents and fellows) and medical students. Candidate will participate in Departmental activities. This full-time faculty position in the Department of Neurological Surgery reports to the Chairman of Neurological Surgery. Board certified or board eligible in Neurological Surgery specialty MD required Must be eligible for NYS Medical License and DEA"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189690": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Scientist - Pediatric Cornea",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Ophthalmology Location: 02 At Columbia University Irving Medical Centers Department of Ophthalmology, advances in patient care are driven by scientific discovery. Our unique program is positioned with access to a large and diverse population of patients affected by rare and complex corneal diseases. For certain diseases such as keratoconus, we have built a database of about 1,000 patients and can study risk factors, timing of interventions, and genetics to better guide treatment and surveillance. This distinctive clinical volume enables high-impact research aimed at improving long-term outcomes. As our research continues to expand, we are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to contribute to and help lead these efforts. Responsibilities: ·       Participate in clinical research activities, including support during pediatric ophthalmology clinics (with a focus on Friday clinics) ·       Manage and maintain clinical research databases; ensure data integrity and compliance. ·       Assist with Institutional Review Board (IRB) submissions, amendments, and regulatory documentation. ·       Conduct patient outreach and recruitment for ongoing and new research studies. ·       Oversee and expand clinical imaging and data repositories, including development of image libraries. ·       Perform and coordinate research related testing and imaging under the direction of principal investigators. ·       Analyze, interpret, and summarize clinical and research data for presentations, manuscripts, and reports. Minimum Qualifications: ·       MD or PhD in biology, genetics, molecular biology, ophthalmology, or a related scientific discipline ·       Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong attention to detail ·       Proficiency in Microsoft Office (Word, Excel); experience with research databases preferred. ·       Demonstrated ability to work independently and manage multiple priorities effectively. ·       Excellent interpersonal skills and ability to collaborate in a multidisciplinary team environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189697": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor - Management",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Management Location: 06 The Management Division of Columbia Business School has opened the search for qualified applicants for a tenure-track position in Micro-Organizational Behavior at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. Applications that are received by October 1 will receive full consideration. However, we encourage applicants to submit their material as soon as possible as we are evaluating applications on a rolling basis. Applicants for an Assistant Professor level appointment should have completed, or be close to completing, a PhD from an accredited institution, demonstrate promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training in Management or related fields with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an Associate Professor level appointment (non-tenured) should have a PhD from an accredited institution, a record of being an excellent scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the School and show great promise of attaining distinction in the field of Management. We will have a particular interest in Organizational Behavior and other related areas of Management. Please visit our online application site at https://academic.careers.columbia.edu for further information about this position and to submit your application or copy and paste the URL below: https://apply.interfolio.com/189697."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189820": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor / Associate Professor / Professor of OBGYN - Gen OB",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: 02 The Department of Obstetrics & Gynecology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center has long been a leader in advancing women’s reproductive health care. We are the birthplace of the Apgar score for assessing newborn health, the first institution in the country to perform an amniocentesis test, and the center that created RhoGAM, which has virtually eradicated Rh-incompatibility reactions that once accounted for hundreds of fetal and neonatal deaths and illnesses each year. Located at the heart of CUIMC's vibrant medical community, more than 100 physicians and over 40 post-graduate residents and fellows work together alongside researchers and clinicians across CUIMC to meet women’s health care needs. We remain committed to our mission to provide the best patient care, to train future Ob/Gyn leaders, to support innovative research, and to bring those innovations into clinical practice. JOB RESPONSIBILITIES: The Columbia University Irving Medical Center is seeking a faculty in Obstetrics and Gynecology to join the General OB/GYN Division. We seek full-time board certified or board eligible individuals to participate in all the clinical and academic affairs of our dynamic Department. Responsibilities include providing comprehensive inpatient and outpatient obstetric and gynecologic care, including the management of general obstetrics and gynecology patients and the performance of gynecologic surgery. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to delivering high-quality clinical care, advancing academic scholarship, and educating residents and medical students. Clinical responsibilities will include providing care at NewYork-Presbyterian Morgan Stanley Children's Hospital and Columbia University outpatient practices located throughout Midtown and Washington Heights. This position offers significant opportunities for leadership, professional growth, and career development within an academic medical center. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is dedicated to the goal of building a multicultural faculty and staff committed to teaching, working and serving in a diverse community, and strongly encourages applications from candidates of traditionally underrepresented backgrounds. We are continuously seeking to recruit individuals who will enhance the diversity of our workplace and the effectiveness of our organization. Minimum Qualifications: • Medical Degree from an Accredited University • New York Medical License • Board Eligible or Board Certified Preferred Qualifications: • Commitment to Columbia University Irving Medical Center and the communities we serve • Desire to participate in a community-based healthcare practice • Commitment to evidence-based practice and quality improvement • Excellent communication, bedside manners, and organizational skills • Strong interest in medical education (residents, medical students, nursing students) • Collaborates well with other disciplines (RN, CNM, NP) and specialties • Fluency in Spanish a plus"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189826": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Microbiology Location: 02 The candidate will assist with on-going projects in the lab, and overtime will also play a lead role in pursuing projects. The main responsibilities include designing experiments, conducting experiments (e.g., tissue culture, molecular biology, cell biology, biochemistry etc.), data analysis, presenting at lab meetings, and manuscript writing. As needed, the candidate will also help with lab management tasks, including preparing reagents, lab organization, and equipment maintenance etc. Responsibilities include but are not limited to the following: Share knowledge of cell biology and molecular biology and participate in scientific discussions and meetings. Under the supervision of the principal investigator, candidate will design and conduct experiments and also analyze results to address key questions in immunology. Follow defined protocols from the lab and also adapt protocols from the literature to fit each experimental design. Will conduct some experiments independently, and some experiments in collaboration with others. Conduct tissue culture, molecular biology, and biochemistry experiments. Also requires ability to operate and maintain equipment. Organize experimental results, present data at meetings, and participate in scientific discussions. Minimum Educational Requirements Bachelors degree in Biology or related field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189872": {
      "title": "Reference Librarian",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: University Libraries Location: 01 The Li Lu Law Library at Columbia University is seeking a Reference Librarian to provide in-person and virtual reference service in all areas of law to faculty, students, and others. Reporting to the Head of Public Services, the successful candidate will participate in the Law Library’s Administrative Rotation System, an experience that provides librarians with a new set of administrative responsibilities on an annual basis, including assisting with collection development, coordinating faculty and student outreach, and organizing the robust legal research instruction undertaken by the reference librarians. Responsibilities also include training law students and others through extensive classroom teaching, workshops, and one-on-one instruction; providing in-depth research assistance for faculty members; creating and maintaining LibGuides, and other related duties. Reference Librarians share regular weekend hours during the academic year. This position offers the opportunity to work in a supportive team dedicated to delivering quality services to world-class professors and bright students, amidst one of the country’s finest collections of legal materials, in a beautiful, newly-renovated library. As one of the world's leading research universities, Columbia University in the City of New York provides outstanding opportunities to work and grow in a dynamic, multicultural, intellectual community. The Columbia University Libraries comprises a diverse and engaged staff committed to furthering the University's teaching and research mission through innovation, collaboration, and a commitment to excellence. Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and strongly encourages individuals of all backgrounds and cultures to consider this position. We offer a salary commensurate with qualifications and experience and excellent benefits, including the possibility of below-market-rate housing in one of several Columbia-owned apartment buildings. Minimum Qualifications JD from accredited institution (completed by the start date); Extensive experience with the use of legal and non-legal research tools; Excellent communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills; Strong service and teamwork orientation; and Technological competency and a willingness to learn new technology as needed. Preferred Qualifications MLS or equivalent from accredited institution or the equivalent combination of education and experience; Experience with teaching or instruction; Experience with or interest in foreign, comparative, and international law research; and Reading knowledge of a foreign language. Join us in shaping the future of library services and advancing open data initiatives at Columbia University! For serious consideration, please apply online with your current CV and a letter of interest at: https://academic.careers.columbia.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189972": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science Location: 01 Columbia Engineering, the Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist (ARS) in the field of global atmospheric modeling and machine learning, under the supervision of Prof. Pierre Gentine at Columbia University and Prof. Viviana Acquaviva at CUNY. The position is part of the National Science Foundation-funded Learning the Earth with Artificial intelligence and Physics (LEAP) Science and Technology Center (STC), https://leap.columbia.edu/, a multi-institutional center effort meant to improve climate projections using novel artificial intelligence for better climate adaptation. This position will be based at LEAP’s offices in New York City. The goal of this project is to develop a new multi-scale representation for Earth system modeling that can be used in fully differentiable generative modeling frameworks (e.g., flow matching, diffusion, and stochastic interpolants). The first objective will be to expand currently available multi-scale representations in two directions: 1) Considering wavelet-based representations that are optimized for the geometry of weather and climate maps, including e.g. spherical wavelets; 2) Considering other approaches to multi-scale modeling. These will be tested on well known partial differential equations (PDE) systems that regulate fluid dynamics (for example, turbulent radiative layer and Rayleigh-Benard convection), and compared to pixel-space representations in both deterministic and generative approaches. Next, the ARS will use the new tools and apply them to case studies in weather and climate modeling, considering 1) emulations of ocean and atmospheric systems, focusing on global scales; 2) generative downscaling. This ARS will work with LEAP scientists to build the workflows that allow for rapid production, analysis, and emulation of data products and to disseminate findings to research group leaders in LEAP and the wider research community. The ARS will closely collaborate with graduate students, postdocs, and other staff within LEAP, as well as the TerraD2I Lab at CUNY led by Dr. Viviana Acquaviva. We are committed to building a community of scientists with a range of academic and professional backgrounds, and believe that a variety of perspectives and experiences is essential to advancing our research and mission. Minimum Qualifications: - A Ph.D. in Data Science, Computer Science, Physics, Earth System Science or a directly related discipline is required by the start of the appointment. - Strong programming skills are a requirement. Preferred Qualifications: - Post-doctoral experience and demonstrated experience in Earth System Science, Data Science, or similar. - Fluency in Python, including deep learning frameworks such as PyTorch. - Advanced experience in generative AI modeling, including flow matching and diffusion models. - Experience with climate data formats (netcdf, zarr, xarray) and with designing and running data analysis pipelines for global climate simulations. - Experience with signal processing tools is desirable. - Experience with distributed training frameworks on high performance computing and cloud-based systems. - Excellent command of the English language (verbal and written) and strong communication skills are desired. Applications must include: (a) curriculum vitae (b) statement of research (optional) (c) names of at least three references who may be asked to provide letters."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189974": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatrics at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Assistant Professor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Columbia University College of Physicians and Surgeons and New York-Presbyterian Hospital are recruiting for a full-time academic pediatric faculty member in the Division of Pediatric Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation at the rank of Assistant Professor (commensurate with credentials) on the non-tenure track to serve as Faculty Leader of the division’s Quality/Safety program. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to collaborate with the outstanding clinical and research faculty within the division and at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC), the NCI-designated Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, and the Mailman School of Public Health. Founded in 1959, The Division of Pediatric Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplant at Columbia Children’s Health, New York-Presbyterian Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital (MSCH) is one of the oldest and most comprehensive centers in the country focused on treating children with cancer and blood disorders. MSCH has 202 inpatient pediatric beds, including a 54-bed Pediatric Intensive Care Unit and 10 operating rooms. Our division is currently composed of 3 sections (Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation) with 24 full-time faculty, 4 research RN/NP’s, 8 clinical research associates, and 2 regulatory specialists and includes an ACGME-accredited Fellowship Program and a FACT-accredited stem cell transplant program. The primary responsibility of this position is to provide oversight of the Quality/Safety program for the Division of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation at CUIMC/MSCH.  The program leader will be expected to develop, implement and oversee initiatives to improve quality and safety of care, operational efficiency and patient/family experience in the inpatient and outpatient settings.  They will be expected to promote a culture of safety through mentorship and teaching of trainees and other faculty regarding quality improvement processes and best practices.  The program leader will work closely with the NYP Quality and Safety Program on key hospital- and enterprise-wide initiatives related to the pediatric hematology, oncology and stem cell transplant patient population, including chemotherapy safety and CLABSI reduction. Clinical responsibilities will include serving as attending physician on the inpatient oncology service and providing outpatient care to children, adolescents and young adults for pre-HSCT with oncologic diagnoses as part of a multidisciplinary team. Administrative responsibilities will include leadership of division’s Quality/Safety Program and participation in hospital- and enterprise-wide Quality/Safety projects, committees and councils. Educational responsibilities include training of fellows and residents, including clinical oversight, mentorship, training and didactic teaching sessions related to quality/safety in pediatric hematology-oncology. Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License Must be Board certification in Pediatric Hematology/Oncology or an equivalent board certification with experience in Stem Cell Transplantation Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189973": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center Location: 02 The Human Immune Monitoring Core at Columbia University Irving Medical Center offers cutting-edge single-cell services, including multimodal single-cell genomics, multiplexed tissue imaging, and mass cytometry (CyTOF), supporting translational, clinical, and basic research across a diverse scientific and clinical research community. Responsibilities : Collaborate with scientists and clinicians to design and execute complex research studies involving patient-derived specimens and preclinical models. Plan, oversee, and perform high-quality quantitative multiplex imaging experiments using automated multispectral microscopy platforms. Develop, optimize, benchmark, validate, and implement novel single-cell and imaging methodologies. Ensure adherence to quality control standards and best practices for experimental design, data generation, and sample processing. Provide technical expertise, consultation, and training to investigators utilizing core facility services. Assist with data review, interpretation, and communication of results to researchers and collaborators. Contribute to the maintenance of laboratory protocols, standard operating procedures, and instrumentation. Perform other related duties and core facility activities as assigned. Minimum Degree Required Bachelor's degree in specific research field Minimum Qualifications Strong experimental and analytical skills especially in IHC, Immunofluorescence, and imaging microscopy Strong experimental and analytical skills Strong organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills Exceptional attention to detail Work independently and collaborate with leadership to implement novel concepts, methods, and broad impact of the core."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190032": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor - Management",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Management Location: 06 The Management Division of Columbia Business School has opened the search for qualified applicants for a tenure-track position in Strategy at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. Applications that are received by October 1 will receive full consideration. However, we encourage applicants to submit their material as soon as possible as we are evaluating applications on a rolling basis. Applicants for an Assistant Professor level appointment should have completed, or be close to completing, a PhD from an accredited institution, demonstrate promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training in Management or related fields with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an Associate Professor level appointment (non-tenured) should have a PhD from an accredited institution, a record of being an excellent scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school and show great promise of attaining distinction in the field of Management. We have a particular interest in Strategy and other related areas of Management. Please visit our online application site at https://academic.careers.columbia.edu to submit your application or copy and paste the URL below: https://apply.interfolio.com/190032."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190039": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Management",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Management Location: 06 The Management Division of Columbia Business School has opened the search for qualified applicants for a tenure-track position in Organizational Theory at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank.  Applications that are received by October 1 will receive full consideration. However, we encourage applicants to submit their materials as soon as possible as we are evaluating applications on a rolling basis. Applicants for an Assistant Professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a PhD from an accredited institution, demonstrate promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training in Management with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an Associate Professor level appointment (non-tenured) should have a PhD from an accredited institution, a record of being an excellent scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school and show great promise of attaining distinction in the field of Management. We will have a particular interest in Organizational Theory, Entrepreneurship, and other related areas of Management. Please visit our online application site at https://academic.careers.columbia.edu to submit your application or copy and paste the URL below: https://apply.interfolio.com/190039. ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190051": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Yuste Lab in the Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist position. The successful candidate will investigate the effects of neuropeptides on cortical microcircuits using advanced calcium and voltage imaging techniques, combined with experimental manipulations to characterize neural circuit function and signaling. Application screening will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Ph.D. in Neuroscience or a related field Preferred qualification: At least 5 years of experience using calcium imaging to monitor neuronal populations. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR) . On the site, please upload cover letter, CV, and a list of 3 reference providers (who may be asked to provide a letter at a later date)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190062": {
      "title": "Instructor in Rehabilitation and Regenerative Medicine (Physical Therapy) at CUIMC and Clinical Physical Therapist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Rehabilitation and Regenerative Medicine Location: 02 The Columbia University Program in Physical Therapy is seeking a collaborative individual with a strong work ethic for a staff physical therapist position in a vibrant Academic Clinical Faculty Physical Therapy Practice.  This individual strives for clinical excellence and patient-centered compassionate care. The mission of this faculty practice is to provide the best clinical practice, facilitate professional development among the physical therapy clinical and academic faculty of the future, and foster a collegial learning environment for clinicians, residents, and physical therapy students. This experienced, energetic and team-oriented individual will: Provide clinical care to patients with a broad range of orthopedic diagnoses. Efficiently execute all professional duties Remain current with emerging evidence. and skills in the pursuit of life-long learning. Invest in collaborative clinical practice. Communicate with respect and compassion. Opportunities include: Professional growth through co-treatments and collaborations with other health professions. Involvement in the Columbia University academic community including teaching within the entry-level DPT program, mentoring an Orthopedic Physical Therapy Resident, and providing clinical instruction to physical therapy program students. Participation in clinical research. Columbia University provides an excellent benefit package. Faculty Practice Locations: Columbia Physical Therapy Faculty Practice, Tarrytown 155 White Plains Road East Building, Suite 102 Tarrytown, NY 10951 Columbia Physical Therapy Faculty Practice, CUIMC 51 Audubon Avenue Suite 100 New York, NY 10032 Minimum Degree: Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) Minimum Qualifications: Must currently have or be eligible for PT licensure in New York State Clinical specialty preferred (ie, Board-certified Orthopedic Specialist) 5+ years of clinical experience in musculoskeletal physical therapy Preferred Qualifications: Graduate of an accredited physical therapy program Experience in Orthopedic PT Residency clinical mentorship or didactic educaiton BCLS Certified Member of the American Physical Therapy Association"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190095": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Discipline or Senior Lecturer in Discipline in Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Mechanical Engineering Location: 01 The Mechanical Engineering Department at Columbia University School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS) welcomes applications for a faculty position at the rank of Lecturer in Discipline or Senior Lecturer in Discipline, to start January 1, 2027. This is a full-time, non-tenure-track, renewable appointment with multi-year renewal contingent on successful reviews. Lecturers in Discipline are officers in the University who meet a programmatic need for instruction in specialized fields. Responsibilities of this position will include teaching three lecture courses per semester on topics in Robotics and Artificial Intelligence, including areas such as Machine Learning, Digital Twins, and Industrial Automation. Benefits available to Engineering lecturers may include leave, research, the potential for summer salary, and opportunities for additional compensation. Candidates must have a PhD in Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Computer Science, or a related field by the date of appointment, and a commitment to high-quality teaching at the undergraduate level. Candidates at the rank of Lecturer in Discipline are expected to have exemplary teaching experience, documented evidence of pedagogical excellence, and an interest in the science of teaching and learning at all levels. Candidates at the rank of Senior Lecturer in Discipline are expected to have substantial experience and accomplishments, a superlative record of teaching as a lecturer, and documented evidence of pedagogical excellence in carrying out administrative or other department responsibilities. For additional information and to apply, please see the following link: https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-resources/faculty-positions . Applications should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/190095 and include the following: a curriculum vitae including a publication list, a statement of teaching interests and plans, contact information for three experts who can provide letters of recommendation, and three selected pre/reprints of scholarly work. Applications received by September 1, 2026 will receive full consideration, and the position will remain open until filled. Hiring Salary Range: Lecturer in Discipline: 106K - 129K; Senior Lecturer in Discipline: 125K - 162K"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190108": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Chemistry Location: 01 The Cornish Research Group in the Department of Chemistry at Columbia University invites applications for the position of Associate Research Scientist. The candidate will have extensive experience in synthetic and chemical biology, with preference given to applicants with experience in protein engineering, directed evolution, and/or molecular biology. In this role the candidate will lead a project area in either biosensor engineering, genetic code expansion, or therapeutic delivery to the cytoplasm of cells and supervise junior students on the project. The candidate will assist the Principal Investigator with all aspects of running the laboratory ranging from setting the overall goals of the laboratory and day-to-day management. Responsibilities will include assisting in the design, direction, oversight, and execution of independent projects in the laboratory; drafting and editing grant proposals, progress reports, and manuscripts; mentoring postdoctoral-, graduate-, and undergraduate researchers. Required: -Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry, Molecular Biology, Biological Sciences, or similar; expertise in molecular biology, synthetic biology, and/or protein engineering -2-5 years of research experience after completing a Ph.D. -Demonstrated ability to work in a collaborative multi-disciplinary team environment -Strong problem-solving and troubleshooting skills. -Excellent written and oral communication skills, including scientific writing, reporting, and external presentation development. Preferred: -Background in synthetic biology, protein engineering, and/or genetic code expansion. -Experience engineering one of the following: amino acid/peptide modifying enzymes, G-protein coupled receptor proteins, -Experience with multiple strategies to create variant libraries, multiple host organisms (E. coli and Yeast), and/or multiple high-throughput screening/selection methods. Applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a CV and cover letter."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190112": {
      "title": "Open Rank Faculty Position: Assistant Professor / Associate Professor / Professor of Population and Family Health",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Heilbrunn Department of Population and Family Health Location: 02 The Heilbrunn Department of Population and Family Health (HDPFH) at Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health is a vibrant and interdisciplinary department comprising of faculty, scientists, thought leaders, and practitioners from the fields of public health, epidemiology, demography, social work, medicine, economics, and law. We apply multidisciplinary population science and public health research methods to address critical domestic and global challenges, including sexual and reproductive health rights, forced migration, child and adolescent health, maternal and newborn health, mental health, environmental justice, human rights, and complex health systems. The Department’s current research portfolio totals $21 million in multi-year committed awards from private foundations and federal funding sources, internal grants, service agreements and gifts to support both international and U.S. based research, service, and program. The Department works to shift global health policy and practice to improve health systems and health outcomes for populations in low-income, unstable, or inequitable environments worldwide. Faculty in this largely grant-funded department engage in a unique combination of research, teaching and service activities. Graduates of the Department pursue careers as research scientists, non-profit leaders, and public health practitioners and evaluators who actively contribute to scientific developments and assume important academic posts and public health leadership globally. The HDPFH is recruiting for a candidate with expertise in global and population health. The successful applicant will build an independent research portfolio with strong scholarly contributions, secure external research funding, and gain national and international recognition at the intersection of global public health, population dynamics, and major global trends. We seek candidates who will help to further develop the Department and School's research, educational, and policy agenda. The individual will be encouraged to collaborate across disciplines and departments within the School and the University. We seek candidates who will conceptualize research, identify funding sources, prepare proposals, establish research collaborations, and carry out funded projects. The incumbent will add to the global public health expertise of the Department and serve in several key roles in research, coursework and student programming. It is expected that the successful candidate will teach one or more departmental courses. The incumbent will also mentor students and actively engage in the academic life of the Department, including student advising and committee membership. A demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, students, community organizations and other external partners is critical to this position. The position includes the opportunity to lead one of the Department’s six advanced certificate programs. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The selected candidate will receive an academic appointment, as non-tenured or tenure track, commensurate with credentials and qualifications. Minimum Degree Required: Requires a PhD, MD, JD or professional equivalent. A doctoral degree in public health, law, psychology, social science, social work, education, nursing, medicine, or a related field is required. Minimum Qualifications: Academic experience commensurate with Assistant/Associate/Professor rank. Strong teaching, presentation and writing skills. Successful applicants have a well-developed independent research portfolio, including scholarly contributions to academic literature, a track record of successful externally funded research, and recognition in their area of expertise in population science and health. Ability and willingness to contribute to the development of the Department’s and School’s research, education, and policy agenda related to diversity, equity and inclusion. This includes conceptualizing research, identifying funding sources, preparing proposals, establishing research collaborations and carrying out funded projects. A strong research background and a robust record of peer-reviewed scholarship addressing the intersections of global public health, population dynamics, and major global trends. Preferred Qualifications: Proven experience in developing or managing government or privately funded grants. Demonstrated experience of independently attracting NIH grants is advantageous. Demonstrated research experience in adolescent health and well-being, global public health, and population research. Previous experience coordinating or holding a leadership role within an academic institution. Experience of leading and conducting research projects across scientific disciplines and translating findings into actionable programmatic and policy recommendations. Field experience in organizations that contribute to advancing and improving population health is highly desirable. All candidates must submit a current CV, cover letter, and a list of three (3) references with contact information. ***** For any inquiries, you can email the HR Director, Annika Recinos, at ar2851@cumc.columbia.edu . ***** Hiring Salary Range: $130,000 to $210,000 Assistant Professor: $130,000 to $160,000 Associate Professor: $150,000 to $180,000 Professor: $180,000 to $210,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190202": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology Location: 01 The Delmore Lab (delmorelab.com) at Columbia University is seeking a motivated postdoctoral researcher to contribute to an NIH-funded project on the molecular neurobiology of seasonal migration. The successful applicant will have the opportunity to contribute to multiple projects, but their primary focus will be a long-term study of a hybrid zone between two subspecies of Swainson's thrushes that exhibit dramatic differences in migratory behavior (orientation, timing, and distance). Our research combines work with both free-flying and captive birds to investigate the genetic and neural mechanisms that underlie migration. Initial projects could expand earlier bulk RNA-seq analyses (Louder et al. 2024 Nat Comm) using single-nucleus RNA sequencing and spatial transcriptomics with existing tissues. Pharmacological experiments could also be used to probe candidate receptors underlying migration. The postdoc will be encouraged to develop independent projects within the broad framework of neuroethology. Location: The Delmore lab is housed in the E3B department at Columbia. We are affiliated with the Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute, Neurobiology and Behavior Program at Columbia, and American Natural History Museum. Required: Ph.D. and expertise in neurobiology, molecular biology, animal behavior, or similar. Preferred: Evidence of past productivity (e.g., first-author pubs and/or grants) and strong written and oral communication skills. Applicants should have experience with tools commonly used in molecular and evolutionary neurobiology. For example, it would be great if they had experience with at least two of the following: molecular biology (e.g., library preparation), histology and neuroanatomy (e.g., brain sectioning, HCR), imaging (e.g., light-sheet microscopy), or computational approaches (e.g., image analysis, bioinformatics). Experience with birds is not necessary. We are particularly interested in candidates who are excited by the opportunities and challenges of working in a non-traditional study system. Specifically, the ideal applicant will be enthusiastic about applying and adapting modern molecular neurobiology approaches in an emerging avian model, helping to advance our understanding of the neural mechanisms underlying complex behaviors. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please send the following required materials i) a complete CV; ii) a one-page statement that includes research accomplishments, research interests, and motivation to apply for the position; and iii) the name and contact details for 3 academic referees who may be contacted at a later stage in the search process . In addition, you may submit one or two representative publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190207": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II/IT Support",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Biological Sciences invites applications for a Desktop and Server support position at the rank of Staff Associate II. Initial appointment is for six months, with the possibility of annual renewals contingent on satisfactory performance. Reporting to the Director of IT Resources, the incumbent will provide the following services to the Department of Biological Sciences: Participate in providing end-user support for all employees in the department Install software as needed; troubleshoot hardware and software issues when necessary Maintain and trouble-shoot departmental computer systems (workstations, file servers, web servers) Help the department’s research groups with backups and data restoration Advise faculty, research staff, and graduate students on purchase and use of computer and peripheral equipment Assist faculty with computer replacement program Work with university-level IT support team to resolve network, email, and other issues Although infrequent, some evening and weekend hours will be required. Minimum Degree Required: Bachelor’s degree in related field (Computer Science, IT Support or similar field) Minimum Qualifications: 4 years of relevant work experience. Knowledge of Windows servers, Linux servers, various desktop software packages (Adobe, Microsoft, Apple, etc.), and basic coding and programming skills for batch file creation. Preferred Qualifications: Level 2 or higher Help Desk support with hands-on experience. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials:  CV and a list of 3 references who may be contacted later in the process; at least one recommender should be a recent supervisor or professional contact."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190363": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Epidemiology Location: 02 The Department of Epidemiology at Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health in New York City is hiring a Postdoctoral Research Scientist with interest on the role of nutrition and the gut microbiome in infectious diseases and/or maternal-child health outcomes. The Postdoctoral Research Scientist will work with the faculty mentor, the research team, and various internal and external collaborators on projects such as 1) investigating the impact of nutritional interventions on the gut microbiome and metabolome in children who are HIV-exposed uninfected, 2) determine how maternal HIV infection and its treatment affects diet, appetite and the gut microbiome in mother-infant populations, and 3) study the relationship of gut microbiome with alcohol intake and TB treatment outcomes in adults. The Postdoctoral research scientist will have the opportunity to learn and gain experience in the design and conduct of epidemiological studies related to nutrition, microbiome and health outcomes. They will also have opportunities to lead and co-author manuscripts, and the postdoctoral research scientist will also be supported to develop and pursue their own research projects and grant proposals. There is access to samples and data from multiple well-characterized cohorts and clinical trials to develop future projects (e.g. K grants). The postdoctoral research scientist is expected to have relevant expertise in microbiome and metabolomics data processing and statistical analysis, and will be expected to prepare manuscripts, present at scientific meetings, and work on relevant grant proposals. Required Applicant Documents: CV, cover letter, 2 references, 2 writing samples/publications, and 1-page research statement. Please contact Dr. Rupak Shivakoti at rs3895@cumc.columbia.edu for any questions or clarifications prior to applying. Requirements: • PhD or equivalent degree in Epidemiology/Biostatistics, Nutrition/Metabolism, Computational Biology or related disciplines (e.g. Microbiology with strong analytical skills) • Proficiency in R or related programming language • Experience and publications in microbiome bioinformatics and data analysis; experience in metabolomics and multi-omics data analysis preferred • Knowledge of nutrition/metabolism/biology for data interpretation preferred • Strong motivation to develop new epidemiologic research projects related to nutrition and microbiome • Excellent communication and quantitative skills • Ability to conduct self-directed and collaborative research Required Applicant Documents: CV, cover letter, 2 references, 2 writing samples/publications, and 1-page research statement. Please contact Dr. Rupak Shivakoti at rs3895@cumc.columbia.edu for any questions or clarifications prior to applying."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190375": {
      "title": "Open Rank: David M. Posner Professor of International and Public Affairs",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of International and Public Affairs Location: 01 The School of International and Public Affairs (SIPA) and the Institute of Israel and Jewish Studies (IIJS) at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications from scholars and practitioners whose work engages with public policy, politics, foreign policy, economics, security, or related interdisciplinary issues, including but not limited to work relevant to Israel and the broader Middle East for a position as the Rabbi David M. Posner Professorship in Israel and Jewish Studies, at the rank of assistant, associate, or full Professor or at the rank of assistant, associate, or full Professor of Professional Practice, to begin July 1, 2027. Disciplinary specialization is open, and includes political science, history, economics, business, journalism, diplomacy or international relations, though other areas of expertise may be considered. The successful candidate will hold a primary appointment in SIPA along with an appointment in IIJS and will teach both undergraduate and graduate courses. The incumbent will additionally provide intellectual leadership in the field of Israel and the Middle East, especially as they relate to SIPA’s Global Policy Challenges: Geopolitical Stability, Democratic Resilience, Climate and Sustainable Development, Inclusive Prosperity and Macroeconomic Performance, Technology and Innovation. Ph.D. or other terminal degree in a related field. Demonstrated successful experience in teaching both undergraduate and graduate students. Candidates will be expected to sustain a strong commitment to research, publication, and teaching. Applicants should upload a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, statement of current and future plans, a writing sample, and a list of references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For further information, please contact Hazel May, Senior Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, at hm2135@columbia.edu Hiring Salary Range: $120,000 - $250,000 · Tenure Track Assistant Professor or Assistant Professor of Practice: $120,000-$130,000 · Tenure Track Associate Professor or Associate Professor of Practicer: $130,000-$180,000 · Professor with tenure or Professor of Practice: $180,000-$250,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190477": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Political Science Location: 01 Columbia University Arts & Sciences, as part of a hiring cluster, seeks to fill two positions in African Studies, to begin July 1, 2027, at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor without tenure, Associate Professor with tenure, or Full Professor. One of these appointments will be located within the Department of Political Science, with a possible joint appointment in the Department of Middle Eastern, South Asian, and African Studies; History; or Anthropology. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication profile and teach in the relevant undergraduate and graduate programs. Ph.D. in Political Science required. We seek candidates with a distinguished record of scholarly inquiry who will contribute to the broader intellectual community and mission for the study of Africa in and across disciplines. We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute to teaching and research on African Politics, Comparative Politics, or other subfields of Political Science, depending on specific expertise. All applications must be made through Columbia University's Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and an article-length writing sample. Within your cover letter, please discuss your teaching philosophy. Non-tenured applicants should provide three reference letters; tenured applicants should provide the names and contact information of three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190466": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Genetics and Development Location: 02 The Ciccia Laboratory in the Department of Genetics and Development at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) is seeking a highly motivated scientist for a Research-Track Assistant Professor (Non-Tenure Track) position. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr. Alberto Ciccia to help lead the laboratory's research program, mentor trainees, and contribute to the strategic and administrative direction of the lab. Key Responsibilities Supervise and mentor graduate students and postdoctoral fellows on a day-to-day basis, including experimental design, data interpretation, and career development. Play a central role in the preparation, writing, and submission of grant proposals (e.g., NIH R01/R21, foundation, and fellowship applications) to sustain and grow lab funding. Organize and coordinate the research activities of the laboratory, including project planning, resource allocation, and lab management. Contribute new ideas and help shape future research directions for the laboratory, in areas spanning genome stability, replication stress, and genome editing. Design, execute, and interpret experiments related to the laboratory's core research programs, and represent the laboratory's work through publications and presentations at scientific meetings. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in genetics, molecular biology, or cell biology. Demonstrated expertise in genome stability and genome editing technologies. Background in the study of the mechanisms by which cells respond to replication stress is strongly preferred. Solid record of first-author publications in peer-reviewed journals, commensurate with career stage. Prior experience mentoring junior researchers and/or contributing to grant writing is highly desirable. Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in a team-oriented research environment. To apply, please submit your application and attach an updated copy of your CV."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190485": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Political Science Location: 01 Columbia University’s Department of Political Science seeks to fill a position in the Politics of the Middle East, with a joint appointment in the Institute for Israel and Jewish Studies, to begin July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will hold a primary appointment in Political Science along with an appointment in IIJS, and will teach in the undergraduate program, including a course for undergraduates in the politics of the Middle East. The successful candidate will also train and mentor graduate students. Ph.D. in Political Science required. We seek scholars in any area of Political Science (e.g., comparative politics, international politics, political theory) with an outstanding publication record and who have ongoing research, including but not limited to work relevant to Israel and the broader Middle East and North Africa region. Preference is for a senior scholar, but the search is open-rank. Applications must be made through Columbia University's Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and an article-length writing sample. Within your cover letter, please discuss your teaching philosophy. Non-tenured applicants should provide three reference letters; tenured applicants should provide the names and contact information of three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190500": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of International and Public Affairs",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of International and Public Affairs Location: 01 The School of International and Public Affairs at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications from scholars working in the field of environmental, resource, and/or energy economics. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching and intellectual leadership in the SIPA Climate, Environment, and Energy curriculum at the master’s and undergraduate levels. This position is at the rank of Assistant Professor, tenure track, and will begin July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Ph.D. in Economics or a field closely related to environmental, resource, and/or energy economics is required at the time of appointment. Candidates will be expected to sustain a strong commitment to research, publication, and teaching. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, job market paper, and three letters of recommendation. The deadline for submissions is November 15th, 2026. For further information, please contact Hazel May, Senior Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, at hm2135@columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190511": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University seeks an Associate Research Scientist to join the Stockwell Laboratory, which investigates ferroptosis—an iron-dependent, non-apoptotic form of regulated cell death—and its roles in cancer, neurodegeneration, and other disease states. The successful candidate will lead the rational design, chemical synthesis, and biological evaluation of small-molecule modulators of ferroptosis, encompassing both ferroptosis-inducing and ferroptosis-inhibiting agents, as chemical probes and as candidate therapeutics. The position sits at the interface of synthetic organic chemistry, medicinal chemistry, and chemical biology, and demands an unusual combination of expertise across these disciplines. Research Duties The research duties and the specialized, complex nature of this position are as follows: •     Design and synthesize libraries of novel small molecules intended to induce or inhibit ferroptosis, applying advanced synthetic organic and medicinal chemistry strategies including asymmetric catalysis and heterocycle construction. •     Carry out structure-guided, iterative optimization of lead compounds to improve potency, target selectivity, metabolic stability, and other drug-like properties. •     Evaluate synthesized compounds in biochemical and cell-based assays measuring cell viability, lipid peroxidation, and engagement of ferroptosis regulators such as GPX4 and associated targets. •     Integrate computational and structure-based design with experimental structure–activity relationship (SAR) data to guide successive rounds of compound development. •     Interpret co-crystal structures and biophysical data to define compound binding modes and elucidate mechanisms of action. •     Collaborate closely with biologists, structural biologists, and computational scientists within the laboratory and across collaborating groups, and provide scientific guidance and mentorship to graduate and undergraduate researchers. •     Prepare manuscripts for publication, present findings at scientific meetings, and contribute to grant reporting and the development of new research directions. Because the position requires the independent execution of multidisciplinary research spanning synthetic chemistry, chemical biology, and structure-based drug design, it can be performed only by an individual holding an advanced doctoral degree together with a rare, specialized combination of experimental skills. Minimum Qualifications •     Ph.D. in Chemistry, Chemical Biology, or a closely related field. •     Demonstrated expertise in synthetic organic and/or medicinal chemistry, including the design and multistep synthesis of novel small molecules. •     Record of peer-reviewed scientific publication. Preferred Qualifications •     Experience with chemical-biology approaches to cell death, redox biology, or lipid peroxidation, and familiarity with ferroptosis. •     Proficiency in mammalian cell culture and cell-based assays of compound activity and target engagement. •     Experience with structure-based drug design and the interpretation of structural and biophysical data. •     Ability to work independently, manage parallel projects, and collaborate across chemistry and biology teams. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR) . On the site, please upload cover letter, CV, and a list of 3 reference providers (who may be asked to provide a letter at a later date)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190515": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Political Science Location: 01 Columbia University’s Department of Political Science invites applications for an Assistant Professor position in Political Methodology, to begin July 1, 2027, pending final budgetary approval. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication agenda and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate programs. A Ph.D. in Political Science, Economics, or Statistics is required at the time of appointment. All candidates must demonstrate outstanding scholarship and excellent promise for future development as scholars, instructors, and mentors. Applications must be made through Columbia University's Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, three letters of reference, and at least two article-length writing samples. The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026, and the search will remain open until filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190517": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Political Science Location: 01 Columbia University’s Department of Political Science invites applications for a position in Comparative Politics at the Assistant Professor level to begin July 1, 2027, pending final budgetary approval. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication agenda and teach in undergraduate and graduate programs. Preference will be given to scholars of the Middle East and North Africa, South Asia, and Southeast Asia and those working on democratic erosion. Ph.D. required. All candidates must have excellent scholarship and strong promise for future development as scholars and teachers. Preference will be given to scholars of the Middle East and North Africa, South Asia, and Southeast Asia and those working on democratic erosion. Applications must be made through Columbia University's Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, three letters of reference, and at least two article-length writing samples. The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications on October 15, 2026, and the search will remain open until filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190628": {
      "title": "Staff Associate III",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of Social Work Location: 01 The purpose of this position is to conduct research and analysis across projects for the Center on Poverty and Social Policy. The incumbent of this position will be responsible for leading reports and analyses using data from the Poverty Tracker in order to meet the project’s contract goals. Additionally, the incumbent of this position will lead other data analysis projects associated with the Center on Poverty and Social Policy’s federal and state policy research portfolio, including original academic research, policy simulations, and joint reports with external partners. The incumbent will also lead project teams around individual deliverables and supervise hourly student research assistants. · Proactively develop and execute research projects (including question development, data analysis plans, analysis, and writing) that are relevant to policy discussions in New York City or at the national and state levels; independently lead and execute these projects (25%) · Independently propose, execute, and draft research projects in areas of subject matter expertise related to social and public policy, as well as poverty measurement (25%) · Supervise student research assistants in the execution on research projects (10%) · Actively develop further subject matter expertise and knowledge of latest technologies and statistical programming techniques by engaging with the broader policy and research community and leading literature reviews of relevant research produced by academic institutions and policy organizations (10%) · Assist PI with completion of academic peer-review manuscripts and related documents and deliverables (10%) · Communicate research findings in an accessible manner to press and advocacy organizations, and developing and maintain partnerships with external policy organizations (10%) · Present research findings to academic and policy audiences in the context of conferences and/or testimonies to public bodies. (5%) Other responsibilities as needed (5%) Bachelor’s degree in social science field (e.g., economics, sociology, political science, social work, psychology, or other social science field), Master’s degree preferred. Four to six years of experience conducting social science research required. Computer skills including proficiency with and broad understanding of Microsoft Office and software for data management and/or data analysis (Stata/R); demonstrated organizational skills; demonstrated initiative; attention to detail."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190656": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 THE KUO LAB is seeking an outstanding Associate Research Scientist with expertise in human electroencephalography (EEG) to join the Initiative for Columbia Ataxia and Tremor (ICAT) Laboratory. The successful candidate will lead and support translational research focused on cerebellar ataxia, tremor, Parkinson's disease, and other movement disorders through advanced EEG acquisition, analysis, and integration with multimodal clinical and neurophysiological data. The Associate Research Scientist will work closely with the Principal Investigator, multidisciplinary clinical teams, engineers, and data scientists to develop innovative EEG biomarkers of disease progression, neural circuit dysfunction, and therapeutic response. This position offers opportunities to lead independent research projects, publish in high-impact journals, mentor trainees, and contribute to grant development. JOB RESPONSIBILITIES: Design, implement, and oversee human EEG research studies involving patients with movement disorders and healthy controls. Develop and optimize EEG acquisition protocols, including resting-state, task-based, and event-related potential (ERP) paradigms. Perform advanced EEG preprocessing, artifact removal, source localization, time-frequency analyses, connectivity analyses, and machine learning approaches. Integrate EEG findings with clinical, neuroimaging, behavioral, and genetic datasets. Develop novel neurophysiological biomarkers for cerebellar ataxia, essential tremor, Parkinson's disease, dystonia, and related disorders. Maintain quality assurance procedures for EEG data collection and processing. Supervise research coordinators, graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, and research assistants involved in EEG studies. Prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed publications and present research findings at national and international scientific meetings. Assist in writing NIH and foundation grant applications. Collaborate with investigators across Columbia University and external research institutions. Ensure compliance with IRB regulations, HIPAA requirements, and Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines. Participate in laboratory meetings, journal clubs, and collaborative research initiatives. Other duties as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Biomedical Engineering, Psychology, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field. Minimum of three years of postdoctoral research experience or equivalent. Demonstrated expertise in human EEG acquisition and advanced EEG signal processing. Strong publication record in peer-reviewed scientific journals. Experience analyzing electrophysiological data using MATLAB, Python, EEGLAB, FieldTrip, MNE-Python, or similar software. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a multidisciplinary research environment. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience conducting EEG research in neurological or movement disorders. Knowledge of cerebellar physiology and motor control. Experience with event-related potentials (ERP), source localization, functional connectivity, and neural oscillation analyses. Familiarity with statistical analysis software (R, SPSS, Python). Experience with machine learning or artificial intelligence approaches applied to EEG. Experience integrating EEG with MRI, motion capture, EMG, wearable sensors, or other neurophysiological modalities. Prior experience mentoring trainees and managing collaborative research projects. Successful history of obtaining research funding or contributing to NIH grant applications. Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV and Cover Letter in Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Preferred CV formatting guidelines: https://www.vagelos.columbia.edu/file/41228/download?token=JJuDEmY6"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190693": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor in Film (Creative Producing) or Assistant Professor of Professional Practice/Associate Professor of Professional Practice in Film (Creative Producing)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of the Arts Location: 01 Columbia University School of the Arts invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor/Associate Professor in Film (tenure-track/tenured) in the Faculty of the Arts, or Assistant Professor/Associate Professor of Professional Practice in Film in the Faculty of the Arts, effective July 1, 2027. The MFA Creative Producing program in the Film division is seeking a dynamic and active producer with deep industry experience and relationships who is also a dedicated educator, preferably with experience teaching at the graduate level. The program emphasizes the role of the producer as the holistic creative and business force behind a filmed project. This requires the producer to have both storytelling and business skills, the ability to build collaborative teams, and an understanding of rapidly changing technologies and market conditions. Candidates must have a vibrant professional practice in producing, teaching experience, and the ability to teach and mentor students at the graduate level. The successful candidate will have a breadth of experience in narrative film, television, and digital media in both the independent and studio worlds; the ability to think imaginatively about the future of the industry; and a broad professional network in New York and Los Angeles that can be utilized for the benefit of students and alumni. Experience with international co-productions and familiarity with AI tools, the creator economy, and emerging storytelling technologies, formats, and platforms are preferred. The position will participate in the selection of MFA applicants and mentor students through the MFA thesis and postgraduate planning. A fully engaged presence on campus is required, as well as a willingness to participate in various cross-disciplinary initiatives. The successful candidate will have an active hand in shaping the future direction of the Creative Producing program and the Film program as a whole alongside colleagues in directing and screenwriting. Candidates for Assistant Professor or Associate Professor (tenure-track position) must have demonstrated and shown promise of the capacity for creative work and the accompanying pedagogy qualifying them for future nomination to tenure. Candidates with the rank of Associate Professor (tenured) must have a demonstrated record of professional and teaching achievement and show great promise of attaining distinction in their fields of specialization. Candidates for Assistant Professor of Professional Practice must have demonstrated capacity for creative work and exhibit the potential for continuing to make significant contributions to their area of professional practice and accompanying pedagogy. Candidates for Associate Professor of Professional Practice must have a demonstrated track record of professional success and substantial experience teaching and mentoring students. Columbia University School of the Arts offers Masters of Fine Arts Degrees in four disciplines: Film, Theatre, Visual Arts and Writing; a Masters of Arts degree in Film Studies; and undergraduate majors in Creative Writing, Film Studies and Visual Arts. Review of applications will begin September 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Minimum Degree Required: Bachelor’s Degree or Equivalent required. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must have a vibrant professional practice in producing, a broad industry network, teaching experience, and the ability to teach and mentor students at the graduate level. The successful candidate will have a breadth of experience in narrative film, television, and digital media in both the independent and studio worlds; strong storytelling and business skills; a track record of building collaborative teams; and an understanding of rapidly changing technologies and market conditions. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree or equivalent experience preferred. Experience with international co-productions, familiarity with AI tools, the creator economy, emerging storytelling technologies, formats, and platforms, and experience with line producing and post production are preferred. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) contact information for three references, who may be asked to provide a letter at a later stage in the process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190695": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor - Decision, Risk, and Operations",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Decision, Risk and Operations Location: 06 The Decision, Risk, and Operations Division at Columbia Business School is seeking to hire qualified faculty members for tenure-track appointments at the assistant or associate professor level . The Division has teaching responsibilities for business analytics, statistics, and operations management courses at the MBA and Ph.D. levels. Priority will be given to applications that are submitted before November 1, 2026. For applicants that will be attending the INFORMS Annual Conference in San Francisco, it is highly recommended that they submit their initial materials (CV and statements) by October 19, 2026. Anticipated start date: July 1, 2027 Applicants for an assistant professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, demonstrate promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an associate professor level appointment should have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, a record of scholastic achievement in both research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. To begin application, applicants should click 'apply now' on this page."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190699": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Orthopedic Surgery Location: 02 Position Summary The Department of Orthopedic Surgery at Columbia University Irving Medical Center seeks an Associate Research Scientist with expertise in basic and translational research on spine and intervertebral disc disease, with particular emphasis on aging-related degeneration, immunological mechanisms of disease, mechanobiology and omics-driven discovery. The successful candidate will play a key role in advancing mechanistic understanding of age-associated spinal disorders, identifying novel biological targets for therapeutic intervention, including evaluating novel therapeutics. This is a research position emphasizing scientific leadership, independent investigation, and collaboration within a multidisciplinary research program. Key Responsibilities ·         Lead and conduct basic and translational research focused on biology and mechanobiology of aging and injury related spine and intervertebral disc degeneration. ·         Investigate innate and adaptive immune mechanisms, inflammation, cellular senescence, and immune–tissue interactions in spine and disc disease. ·         Design and implement omics-based approaches, including transcriptomics, epigenomics, proteomics, single-cell, and spatial omics methodologies. ·         Develop and utilize in vitro, ex vivo, and in vivo models of spinal aging and disc degeneration including use of genetic models of disease ·         Collect, validate and integrate omics datasets with functional assays, histology, imaging, and mechanobiological analyses. ·         Analyze and interpret high-dimensional datasets using rigorous and reproducible analytical approaches. ·         Prepare manuscripts for publication in high impact peer-reviewed journals and present findings at scientific meetings. ·         Contribute to the preparation of extramural funding applications (e.g., NIH, foundations, institutional, and industry partners). ·         Mentor and supervise students, postdoctoral fellows, and research staff. ·         Collaborate with clinicians, scientists, engineers, and administration across Columbia University. Minimum Qualifications ·         PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in biomedical engineering, immunology, systems biology, aging biology, musculoskeletal biology, physiology or a related field. ·         Demonstrated expertise in basic research relevant to spine and/or intervertebral disc disease, aging biology, or immunological mechanisms of disease. ·         Strong record of peer-reviewed publications. ·         Experience with omics technologies and interpretation of complex biological datasets. ·         Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. ·         Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Preferred Qualifications ·         Postdoctoral or equivalent research experience in spine aging, disc degeneration, immunology, or systems biology using genetic small animal models ·         Experience with single-cell and/or spatial omics platforms and associated bioinformatics pipelines. ·         Familiarity with inflammaging, immune modulation, cellular senescence, or age-related tissue degeneration. ·         Prior involvement in successful grant submissions or funded research projects. ·         Demonstrated experience mentoring trainees in an academic research setting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190715": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of International and Public Affairs",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of International and Public Affairs Location: 01 The School of International and Public Affairs at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications from scholars working in the field of Artificial Intelligence and Data Science for Policy. Candidates should have deep expertise in AI/ML/data science methods and the drive to see applications through to real world impact in public policy settings. Questions of interest could include, for example: the regulatory, political, or economic questions arising from increasing reliance on AI in the public and/or private sectors; the social and political consequences of reliance on algorithmic approaches to public policy implementation; methodological development at the intersection of social science and AI/machine learning. It is advantageous to have expertise in at least one applied domain, which could relate to SIPA’s global policy challenges: democratic resilience; technology and innovation; geopolitical stability; inclusive prosperity and macroeconomic performance; climate and sustainable development. In addition, this search has the following priorities: - A research agenda that bridges a public policy domain and a technical area of AI, machine learning, data science, or related computational methods - A desire to have real-world impact through both technical innovation and its application to practical problems - Experience teaching computational or numerical skills in public policy-related domains - Interest in contributing to cross-sector collaborations that connect academia with government, industry, and communities, with the aim of informing evidence-based policy around AI The successful candidate will contribute to teaching and intellectual leadership in the SIPA Data Science for Policy curriculum at the master’s and undergraduate levels. This position is at the rank of Assistant Professor, tenure track, and will begin July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Ph.D. in a field related to Artificial Intelligence and Data Science for Policy – including Data Science, Machine Learning, Computer Science, Political Science, Economics, Statistics, or another policy-related discipline – is required at the time of appointment. Candidates will be expected to sustain a strong commitment to research, publication, and teaching. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, writing sample, and three letters of recommendation. For full consideration, applications should be submitted by September 30, 2026. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For further information, please contact Hazel May, Senior Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, at hm2135@columbia.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190766": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Civil Engineering and Engineering Mechanics Location: 01 Immediate opening for an Associate Research Scientist with Andrew Smyth at The Fu Foundation School of Engineering & Applied Science, Department of Civil Engineering & Engineering Mechanics and Principal Investigator for the NSF Center for Smart Streetscapes and Vibration Monitoring Projects for the Metropolitan Museum of Art as well as EMS Optimization for the FDNY. The successful candidate will conduct leading-edge research in infrastructure sensing, data-driven simulation and modeling, with the following responsibilities: · Integration of technologies and research products to build applications; · Recommending technologies to support projects; · Executing software engineering tasks; · Developing and managing detailed project schedules and work plans; · Mentoring; · Collaboration with an interdisciplinary team including engineers, urban planners, emergency response service managers and art conservators. PhD in Civil Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or related field. Work experience in academia and/or industry. This appointment is funded by the NSF Center for Smart Streetscapes and Vibration Monitoring Projects for the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The appointment period is subject to satisfactory performance and funding availability. Provide a CV and Research Statement."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190825": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist position. The research group studies the evolution, plasticity and regeneration of vertebrate brains using the Iberian ribbed newt Pleurodeles waltl and other non-conventional vertebrate models. The successful candidate will focus on understanding the molecular and neural mechanisms underlying the plasticity of the nervous system and of behavior in newts exposed to different environments. Responsibilities: Supervising research projects and conducting research concerning the plasticity of sensory systems in Pleurodeles waltl . Research goals: o   Assessing whether environmental plasticity causes changes in sensory organs at the transcriptomic level. o   Establishing new behavioral assays to evaluate the environmental plasticity of sensory systems, including automated behavioral monitoring with multiple cameras. o   Investigating the neural correlates of the environmental plasticity of olfaction by establishing and conducting calcium imaging in the olfactory bulb. Working closely with collaborators on modeling the plasticity of behavior. Analyzing and interpret data. Managing preparation of progress reports and manuscripts describing study results and conclusions. Minimum Qualifications: PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Neuroscience, Neurobiology, Physiology, Biology, or a closely related scientific discipline. Strong background in systems neuroscience and at least four years of experience in neuroscience research on vertebrate models. Skills required: Performing survival brain surgeries in vertebrates. Performing single-cell RNA sequencing experiments Designing and implementing new behavioral paradigms. Animal handling for behavioral experiments. In vivo two-photon calcium imaging. Confocal microscopy. Strong analytical and quantitative skills, including advanced data analysis in python and R Familiarity with pose estimation analysis of behavioral data. Familiarity with the analysis of single-cell RNA sequencing data. Efficient handling of large datasets Scientific writing (manuscript preparation) All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR) . On the site, please upload cover letter, CV, and a list of 3 reference providers (who may be asked to provide a letter at a later date)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190830": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Surgery Location: 02 The Department of Surgery at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons is accepting applications for an Associate Research Scientist in the Division of Surgical Sciences. The focus of the research projects will be cellular and molecular mechanism underlying neurodegeneration and mitochondrial dysfunction relevant to basic and translational study. Responsibility: Performing laboratory research in the area of neurodegeneration and mitochondrial function relevant to amyloid pathology and mitochondrial degeneration. Evaluating the effect of small molecule compounds or targeted gene changes on amyloid pathology and mitochondrial function, oxidative stress, synaptic pathology, mouse behavior and stereo brain injection and cell culture and images. Functionally characterize candidate genes that influence mitochondrial and synaptic function, amyloid pathology in transgenic animal models. Initiates and conducts individual and interdisciplinary research in neuronal function. Analyze and summarize research data sets utilizing statistical methods. Present research results and prepare manuscripts for peer review and publication in professional journals. Participate to the development of an extramurally funded research program by assisting PIs with NIH grants by developing and generating data and figures. Active participation in the academic and research missions of the University and the Department of Surgery. Training and development of junior lab members. Add Additional responsibilities include participation in the academic and research missions of the University, the Department of Surgery. Minimum Requirements: MD or PhD in Chemistry. Experience in Pathophysiology and Neurobiology research. Research experience using mice. Preferred Qualifications: Experience performing research in synaptic biology, cell biology techniques,neuronal culture, protein evaluation (immunoblotting), and/or microscopy., and mouse behavior. Articles published in peer reviewed journals as first or co-author in the areas of neuroscience, amyloid and synaptic biology. Experience writing scientific papers and assisting with grant application."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190885": {
      "title": "Staff Attorney (Associate Research Scholar)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Law School Location: 01 Columbia Law School seeks a lawyer with experience in immigration law to join Morningside Heights Legal Services, Inc., beginning on October 1, 2026. Morningside Heights Legal Services, Inc. is the nonprofit law firm that houses Columbia Law School’s clinical program. This is an initial one-year appointment, with the possibility of renewal through June 20, 2028. The staff attorney will support members of the Columbia community whose immigration status is at risk. The staff attorney will be responsible for conducting individualized legal consultations and representation, as well as outreach and Know-Your-Rights (KYR) presentations, for Columbia community members who have concerns about their immigration status. The staff attorney will provide rapid response representation, including deportation defense and the filing of federal lawsuits as needed. As a staff attorney, responsibilities will include but are not limited to: Conducting intake interviews for Columbia community members (10%); Representing Columbia community members before administrative agencies, tribunals and federal courts, on a range of immigration-related issues, including but not limited to (60%): Advocacy with CBP concerning international travel; Waivers filed with the State Department and U.S. consulates; Applications for bond and representation in removal proceedings; Stays of removal and parole requests with ICE; Habeas and mandamus petitions, temporary restraining orders, among other district court filings; Petitions and applications before USCIS and EOIR, including adjustment of status and asylum and other humanitarian protections; Liaising with Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) and Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and other government agencies, as necessary; Drafting and keeping FAQ and KYR documents up-to-date for Columbia community members (10%); Hosting KYR trainings and presenting at community events (10%); Covering an on-call emergency line (5%); and Attending meetings in the community, including with local immigration coalitions (5%). The staff attorney will hold the University title of Associate Research Scholar. This is a non-tenure track position and does not teach. Minimum Degree Required: J.D. or equivalent legal degree Minimum Qualifications: Applicants for the Staff Attorney position should have a minimum of three years of relevant professional experience. This experience may include but is not limited to working with asylum seekers; individuals seeking Special Immigrant Juvenile Status, Temporary Protected Status, or Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals; family, student, or employment visas; naturalization applications; and/or related direct legal services or advocacy efforts on behalf of immigrant communities. Admission to a state bar and a demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with others are necessary. Preferred Qualifications: Excellent interpersonal, administrative, and organization skills. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated success in a collaborative, fast-paced work environment. Experience engaging with CBP, ICE, USCIS, EOIR, and in district court litigation is an asset. Comfortable with and interested in conducting Know Your Rights trainings and community presentations. Demonstrated ability to work independently. Prior experience working in an interdisciplinary team preferred. Applications will be considered on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Applicants should submit: (a) a letter of interest, describing your qualifications and interest in the position (Cover Letter); and (b) a curriculum vitae."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190893": {
      "title": "All Ranks: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor (Non-tenure/Tenure Track) (SLP)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Otolaryngology Location: 02 The Department of Otolaryngology Head & Neck Surgery at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons is seeking a full-time Speech Language Pathologist-PhD with experience at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery. We are looking for an individual with leadership and grant writing experience in research, in the areas of voice, swallowing, or upper airway disorders. We are a multidisciplinary center that provides care in an outpatient clinical setting in the heart of New York City. Our patient population includes the pediatric population and professional voice users, as well as adults across the lifespan. Responsibilities: ·  Build an independent NIH supported research program ·  Develop and oversee research infrastructure ·  Mentor and train learners across all levels, medical students, PhD candidates, otolaryngology residents ·  Lead and coordinate division research activities ·  Foster interdisciplinary collaboration ·  Advance the division’s academic reputation ·  Demonstrate leadership that supports future growth Qualifications: Minimum Degree Required: A Masters in Speech Language Pathology, NY State Licensure. PhD and Research Experience in Speech Language Pathology or a related field. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must have the ASHA CCC and be eligible for New York licensure. Applicants must have experience in the research of voice, swallowing, and upper airway disorders. Preferred Qualifications: ·  Strong record of peer reviewed publications ·  Demonstrated success with grant funding ·  Experience leading research teams or complex projects ·  Expertise in research methodology and analytics ·  Teaching and mentoring experience ·  Strong collaborative and communication skills ·  Evidence of national engagement and academic leadership potential ·  Commitment to building a long term, externally funded research program"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190939": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist (Auerbach Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 This is an opportunity to join the Department of Psychiatry at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Associate Research Scientist. The Columbia University Department of Psychiatry is one of the largest in the country in terms of faculty size as well as state, federal, and foundation research support. We are currently among the top-ranked in the nation for Psychiatry in the US News & World Report Best Hospital rankings, as well as in psychiatric research funding from the National Institutes of Health. We have extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. Our faculty includes over 400 psychiatrists, psychologists, social workers, nurses, and neurobehavioral scientists. Clinical facilities and laboratories of the Psychiatry Department are located in a large number of institutions and healthcare systems. These include NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, the New York State Psychiatric Institute, the New York State Office of Mental Health, and the Washington Heights Community Mental Health Center. The Department of Psychiatry also houses the Center for Neurobiology and Behavior, the Mind/Brain Institute, a Howard Hughes Research Institute and the Stanley Center for Applied Neuroscience of Bipolar Disorders. The position is available within the Translational Research on Affective Disorders and Suicide Laboratory at Co lumbia University (auerbachlab.com), directed by Dr. Randy P. Auerbach. The Associate Research Assistant will support NIH/NIMH- and foundation-funded projects that aim to identify neural (structural and functional MRI) and real-time (e.g., experience sampling, passive sensor) markers of depression and suicide in adolescents. Associate Research Scientists will have opportunities to pursue independent research, grants (e.g., R-awards), and interdisciplinary collaborations across Columbia University Key Responsibilites ·        Provide training and supervision to undergraduate, post-baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral level trainees regarding neural bases of depression and suicide. ·        Manage study protocols and IRB-related tasks, ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements. ·        Support federal grant requirements through data-related tasks such as progress reports, study ramp-up activities, and study close-out tasks. ·        Develop and manage study-related and lab-related RedCap surveys. ·        Provide support to study PI to develop and maintain Human Research Protection Program compliant documentation, training, and research. ·        Conduct initial data quality assurance checks and collaborate with internal and external data teams to ensure accurate and complete data collection. ·        Other related responsibilities that emerge in order to complete the lab’s work (e.g., data analysis, processing). · PhD or MD in psychology, psychiatry, or related field. · Candidates must have a track record of extramural funding and a strong publication history. · 3+ years of electrophysiology experience, particularly as it relates to a track record of developing automated processing pipelines for EEG, ERPs, and oscillations · Experience with matlab, EEGLab, and ERPLab is required."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190998": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Surgery Location: 02 Position Summary: The Senior Staff Associate I for the Department of Surgery will work in the lab of Dr. Frank D’Ovidio to facilitate translational cardiothoracic research studies. The individual will work closely with Principal Investigators and Post-Doctoral Researchers on a variety of research projects focusing on lung physiology, pulmonary tissue pathophysiology, pulmonary cell physiology, and tissue biochemistry, clinical, basic and translational research in lung transplantation. Will also collaborate with clinical thoracic and animal research teams. Responsibilities : Collection of surgical specimens from the operation room Establish, verify, and document laboratory protocols. Coordination of research projects, material supplies and equipment maintenance. Facilitate benchtop research and manage clinical information pertaining to research specimens (databases, consent forms, etc.). Perform basic and translational research experiments in the area of pulmonary pathophysiology and lung transplantation Prepare clinical samples for storage (including blood bronchial and broncho-alveolar washings and tissue specimens for analysis) and database logging. Installing and testing new laboratory equipment. Assisting with the design and execution of various experiments. Compile and present results in a brief and efficient approach during weekly lab meetings or as requested. Assist with the preparation of manuscripts and presentations. Communicate amongst both researchers and clinicians and administrative/financial staff . Support other functions as assigned and/or required. Minimum Requirements: Bachelors degree in specific research field and 6-10 years’ experience. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with laboratory techniques: processing clinical samples, histology, cell culture, immunoassays, biomechanics, imaging, and data analysis. Background in pulmonary anatomy and physiology. Ability to present research and scientific data in a professional setting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191014": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor of Ophthalmology at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Ophthalmology Location: 02 Columbia University Irving Medical Center is conducting a search for a Board-Certified or Board-Eligible Ophthalmologist to join the Ophthalmic Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery Division. We are seeking a dynamic and motivated colleague who is inspired by the tripartite mission of academia: clinical care, research, and education. Responsibilities include participation in the faculty practice of the Department of Ophthalmology and teaching through our affiliated sites (NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital and Harlem Hospital).  A track record of proven positive interactions with residents and fellows is highly desirable The successful candidate will be appointed to the full-time faculty at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons’ Department of Ophthalmology at the Edward S. Harkness Eye Institute with privileges at NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital and Harlem Hospital. Academic rank and base salary commensurate with experience. Requirements: MD Degree, License (or eligible) to practice Medicine in New York; Board Certification or Eligibility (ABO); ASOPRS fellowship training in ophthalmic plastic and reconstructive surgery. Please submit a letter of intent, a curriculum vitae, and a list of 3 professional references addressed to: Leejee H. Suh, M.D. Search Committee Chair Miranda Wong Tang Associate Professor of Ophthalmology Department of Ophthalmology/Edward S. Harkness Eye Institute Columbia University Irving Medical Center lhs2118@columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191083": {
      "title": "Director of Digital Library and Scholarly Technologies",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: University Libraries Location: 01 Columbia University Libraries seeks a dynamic and engaged leader to serve as the Director of Digital Library and Scholarly Technologies (DLST). The Director of DLST leads development of strategic vision, prioritization and assessment of system-wide Libraries digital library, scholarly technology services, and online web presence. The Director of DLST leads a creative and innovative team that advances development and implementation of digital library and scholarly technology systems for Columbia affiliates and the global scholarly community, in support of the University mission to advance knowledge and learning at the highest level and convey the products of its efforts to the world. Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Technology and Preservation, the Director of DLST is a critical member of the Technology and Preservation leadership team and the Libraries' Leadership Council. Responsibilities Works closely with Collections divisions to advance Digital Collections programs and services, provides leadership, vision and innovative approaches to enhance and integrate Libraries’ digital technologies and tools in support of faculty and student teaching, learning and research, and oversees development and management of digitization, preservation, born-digital and archival technologies and processes to support growth and sustainability of digital collections and archival collections. Works closely with library and campus partners to deliver programs and services in support of research and scholarship, including the Academic Commons institutional repository, journal publishing, faculty-driven projects, research data and research services. Coordinates and participates in planning for new and enhanced technology and AI-based scholarly communications solutions and services . Partners with Digital Project Management, Library Information Technology, Preservation, Digital Scholarship, special collections units, other parts of the Libraries and other campus technology organizations as appropriate. Serves on Leadership Council, library committees and project teams within the Libraries, with other Columbia entities, and with other institutions as needed to further the programmatic and strategic goals of the Libraries. Pursues external funding, including grants, foundation and donor-based support, to develop and/or enhance digital libraries and scholarly technologies that advance teaching, learning and research. Communicates broadly with Libraries’ staff about the division’s systems, services and accomplishments. Advocates for digital library and scholarly technologies at Columbia and beyond. Represents the Libraries on campus, regional and national discussions of digital library and scholarly system development, contributing to national efforts, standards and practices. MLS or other advanced degree in a related field and experience in libraries, museums, archives, publishing, or other cultural heritage institutions. Minimum Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of library digital technology systems and services Demonstrated knowledge of digital library standards, practices and technologies Demonstrated ability to plan and implement new end-user systems Demonstrated experience in staff management and development Significant technology implementation management experience Significant project management experience Preferred Qualifications An advanced degree relevant to research and scholarship Experience with project and program planning and development Technology-related grant writing Experience with development RFPs and contracts for outsourced services For serious consideration, please apply online with your current CV and a letter of interest at: https://academic.careers.columbia.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191084": {
      "title": "Director, Library Technology Services",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: University Libraries Location: 01 Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Technology and Preservation, the Director of Library Technology Services (LTS) leads the planning, implementation, and daily operations of all library technology functions. This role supports the Libraries’ mission by ensuring effective access to information, advancing teaching and research, and enabling the long-term preservation of collections and significant cultural heritage objects. The Director oversees staff, technology infrastructure, and applications, develops and executes technology strategy and provides budget recommendations. The position collaborates closely with the Leadership Council, campus partners including CUIT, external vendors, and peer academic libraries to deliver innovative, reliable, and sustainable technology services. Key Responsibilities The Director of Library Technology Services (LTS) leads a team of approximately 15 LTS staff in service of Columbia University’s mission for Libraries staff, Columbia affiliates and the global scholarly community, and leads development of strategic vision, assessment and budgeting of Library Technology Services. The Director will collaborate and work closely with other Leadership Council directors to plan and coordinate services for Libraries staff, engage with CUIT and other campus partners, vendors and external partners to procure and support infrastructure, applications and services. Oversees planning, implementation, configuration and ongoing support for enterprise Library systems and services, including CLIO, FOLIO and related applications, CUIT infrastructure and external vendor and partner infrastructure and systems. Provides technology infrastructure support for DLST-managed digital collections, digital workflows, and related applications. Plays lead role in vendor assessment and coordination, including collaborating and supporting the changing nature of library applications, databases and electronic resources. Oversees essential technical infrastructure in support of staff operations and workflows, including but not limited to acquisitions, cataloging, metadata, technical services, electronic resources management, circulation, inter-library loan, shared collections, digital repositories, discovery systems, preservation, reporting, and budgeting. Oversees Libraries staff technology services and support, including library-specific applications, desktop and administrative technology services, data management and records loads, networking, peripherals and staff support. Administers staff technology budget, upgrades, replacement cycles and tracks library and related technology trends. Plays lead role in coordinated technology strategic planning, analysis, assessment and project management, working closely with other Libraries divisions, CUIT and campus partners, external commercial vendors, library technology providers, and library community partners such as ReCAP, BorrowDirect and IvyPlus institutions. Communicate and collaborate broadly with Libraries staff about the division’s systems, services and support. Advocate for Library technology services at Columbia and beyond.  Manages the development, implementation and maintenance of IT policies and procedures.  Actively participate in professional organizations related to library technology services and represents the Libraries in campus, regional and national meetings. Master’s degree in Library Science, Computer Science, or a related field with a minimum of seven (7) years’ experience in library technology, library systems, or a related area; or an equivalent combination of education, training, and experience. Significant experience managing technology implementation projects. Demonstrated knowledge of library technology systems, standards, metadata, and services. Proven experience planning and implementing end-user systems and services. Supervisory experience managing technical teams. Strong understanding of technology systems, workflow analysis, and infrastructure in library or research environments. Preferred Qualifications Advanced academic degree in a relevant scholarly discipline. Experience with project and program planning and development. Experience developing RFPs, writing grants, and engaging with internal and external partners. Demonstrated experience in vendor management, contract negotiation, and procurement. Experience working in an academic or research library or archive. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Strong collaboration and relationship-building abilities. Strategic thinking skills with the ability to envision and implement complex knowledge systems. For serious consideration, please apply online with your current CV and a letter of interest at: https://academic.careers.columbia.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191541": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Institute for Cancer Genetics Location: 02 The Palomero Laboratory, led by Dr. Teresa Palomero (Professor of Pathology and Cell Biology, Institute for Cancer Genetics, Columbia University), is a basic/translational research group investigating the molecular genetics and epigenetic mechanisms mediating malignant transformation in T-cell malignancies, including T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) and peripheral T-cell lymphomas (PTCL), using human and murine models. Our small group is dynamic and collaborative, integrated by researchers with diverse backgrounds and expertise. For additional information and a list of publications, please visit our website: Palomero Lab . Current open areas of research include genomic, transcriptomic and single-cell approaches towards identification of mechanisms of lymphoma and leukemia transformation; interactions between tumor cells and the tumor microenvironment; mechanistic (genetic and biochemical) analyses of lymphoma oncogenes and tumor suppressors; and the identification and evaluation of experimental therapeutics targeting vulnerabilities in human mature T-cell malignancies. The lab is located in the Irving Cancer Research Center on the Columbia University Irving Medical Center campus in Washington Heights, New York City, and has full access to Columbia's shared core facilities, including flow cytometry, single-cell genomics, molecular pathology and animal husbandry cores. Position Overview We are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Scientist to join our team studying the genetics and mechanisms of T-cell leukemia and lymphoma. The successful candidate will design and execute experiments using mouse models of T-cell malignancy, human patient sample analysis, and flow cytometry- and single-cell-based immune profiling, with a focus on tumor microenvironment interactions and the evaluation of experimental therapeutics, working closely with lab members and collaborators across the Institute for Cancer Genetics and the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center. As a Postdoctoral Research Scientist, you will oversee the design and performance of experiments and directly contribute to the conceptual framework of the study. In addition, you will be encouraged to develop your own research ideas along the lines of the research interests of the lab. You will be expected to present your data at departmental seminars and scientific meetings. In addition, you will be requested to collaborate on other ongoing projects within the lab. This position offers a very competitive salary with benefits, supported by the Columbia University postdoctoral union. Responsibilities Design, plan, and execute experiments using genetically engineered and/or xenograft mouse models of T-ALL and PTCL Process and analyze primary human patient samples (e.g., peripheral blood, bone marrow, tumor biopsies) Perform multi-parameter flow cytometry for immunophenotyping, cell sorting, and functional assays Design and analyze single-cell experiments (e.g., scRNA-seq, CITE-seq) to characterize tumor and immune cell populations Investigate interactions between tumor cells and the surrounding tumor microenvironment Evaluate experimental therapeutics in vitro and in vivo, including preclinical efficacy and mechanism-of-action studies Analyze, interpret, and present experimental data clearly to the PI and lab group Maintain detailed, accurate, and up-to-date laboratory and animal records Contribute to manuscript preparation, grant applications, and conference presentations Collaborate productively with other lab members, core facility staff, and outside collaborators Help train and mentor junior lab members (graduate students, technicians) as needed Minimum Qualifications Required: PhD (or equivalent) in a biomedical field, completed or near completion Hands-on expertise in flow cytometry (multi-color panel design, acquisition, and analysis) Direct experience working with mouse models (handling, husbandry, and in vivo experimentation) Experience working with human patient samples and single-cell approaches (e.g., scRNA-seq, CITE-seq) Excellent written and spoken English communication skills Strong organizational skills and meticulous record-keeping An enthusiastic, self-motivated, and collaborative approach to research Highly Preferred Qualifications: Background in immunology Experience with hematologic malignancies or cancer biology Prior first-author publication(s) in a peer-reviewed journal Along with applying for the role, all interested candidates should please email Dr. Teresa Palomero at tp2151@cumc.columbia.edu and include a cover letter detailing relevant work experience, a CV, and the contact information of three references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191580": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Climate School Location: 01 The Center for Climate Systems Research (CCSR) in the Columbia Climate School invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist (ARS) in cloud microphysics modeling. We seek a highly motivated scientist with a strong background in atmospheric science and a demonstrated record of research contributions to cloud and precipitation processes. This position is closely aligned with CCSR’s strategic focus on Earth System Intelligence: using AI-driven climate modeling, NASA satellite observations, and Earth system models to reduce uncertainty in the representation of key climate processes. Warm clouds play a central role in Earth’s radiation budget and water cycle, yet they remain poorly understood and challenging to simulate. Clouds are also a central source of uncertainty in climate projections, making improved cloud microphysics essential to CCSR’s broader goal of advancing next-generation models of clouds and atmospheric processes, from process-scale understanding to global Earth system applications. A key reason is that cloud and precipitation processes span a vast range of scales, from the microphysics of individual particles to the planetary-scale dynamics of Earth’s general circulation. The ARS will address two fundamental challenges that arise from this complexity: how to tractably represent the population statistics of cloud and precipitation particles in models and how microphysical processes couple to macroscale cloud properties and evolution, including precipitation initiation. The successful candidate will develop and evaluate microphysics parameterizations using a hierarchy of model frameworks, from high-resolution process models to global ESM simulations, guided by observational data from field campaigns and satellite platforms. This work will connect directly to CCSR’s dual advantage at the intersection of NASA’s Goddard Institute for Space Studies and the Columbia Climate School by integrating space-based Earth observations, process-level modeling, the NASA GISS ModelE Earth system model, and real-world validation. Among other methods, the ARS will apply machine learning and Bayesian statistics to develop improved parameterizations and systematically distinguish structural from parametric uncertainty in model representations of cloud processes. The ARS will collaborate with partners at NASA GISS and contribute to CCSR’s mission to advance climate modeling and actionable Earth system understanding. Responsibilities Design and execute research using a hierarchy of atmospheric and microphysical models to evaluate and improve warm cloud microphysics parameterizations across scales. Use remote sensing, field campaign, and satellite observations to constrain and evaluate model structural assumptions. Contribute to CCSR’s Earth System Intelligence research area by improving the representation of cloud and precipitation processes in next-generation climate and Earth system models, including connections to NASA satellite observations and the NASA GISS ModelE framework. Publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and present at national and international scientific conferences. Contribute to the preparation of competitive grant proposals to federal funding agencies and applications for HPC allocations. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science, Earth Science, Applied Mathematics, Physics, or a closely related field. Proficiency in Fortran and at least one scientific analysis language, e.g., Python, Julia, MATLAB. Demonstrated experience with numerical modeling. Preferred Qualifications Postdoctoral research experience in atmospheric or Earth science. Experience with atmospheric models such as Large Eddy Simulations (LES) and/or Earth System Models (ESMs). Experience modeling microphysical processes, especially with Lagrangian, particle-based approaches, and/or experience working with cloud observational data, especially radar spectra. Experience running and analyzing simulations on high-performance computing (HPC) platforms. Familiarity with Bayesian statistical methods or parameter estimation applied to complex models. Experience with scientific machine learning. A track record of contributing to successful federal grant proposals. Interest in contributing to CCSR’s broader research mission at the intersection of AI-driven climate modeling, NASA Earth observations, and Earth system model development. The search will remain open for at least 30 days after the ad appears and will continue until the position is filled. Appointments are made on a fiscal year basis and are eligible for renewal each July 1, contingent upon performance and funding. Columbia University benefits accompany this Officer of Research appointment. We accept online applications only"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191686": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center Location: 02 Columbia University is a leader in biomedical research and is a National Cancer Institute-designated Comprehensive Cancer Center. Columbia’s Vagelos School of Physicians and Surgeons is amongst the top research universities and provides an outstanding intellectual environment. The Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center (HICCC) at Columbia University Irving Medical Center provides outstanding Biomedical Research Cores. The HICCC houses 12 core facilities or shared resources that provide services essential to basic and translational cancer research, to which the applicant will have continued full access on a 24/7 basis. The Confocal and Specialized Microscopy Shared Resource (CSMSR) at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) is seeking an Imaging Scientist at the rank of Associate Research Scientist. The CSMSR is a highly rated, well-funded imaging core serving hundreds of research labs at the CUIMC campus as part of the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, and has provided confocal and other advanced optical microscopy technologies and expertise for over 25 years. Capabilities include confocal, 2-photon (ex vivo and intravital), light sheet, high-content plate imaging, super-resolution, wide-field, laser capture microdissection, and real-time metabolic analysis. Details are available at the core website: https://www.cancer.columbia.edu/research/shared-resources/confocal-and-specialized-microscopy. The Imaging Scientist’s primary responsibility will be to work with our diverse user base of basic and clinical scientists to meet their needs for high-performance imaging. This may include training on existing equipment, developing new workflows, or adapting existing methods and analytical approaches, as needed. The Imaging Scientist will also maintain and troubleshoot imaging and computer systems, and assist with purchasing, scheduling, and billing. Opportunities are available for community outreach, workshops, and grant preparation. Collaborative studies and publication are possible for motivated individuals. The Imaging Scientist will report to the Technical Director and Faculty Director. Please contact Technical Director Theresa Swayne ( tcs6@cumc.columbia.edu ) or Faculty Director Liza Pon ( lap5@columbia.edu ) for further details. Minimum Degree Required Ph.D. degree in a relevant scientific field Minimum Qualifications Expertise in advanced microscopy, including image acquisition and analysis, and knowledge of imaging technologies and sample preparation methods Excellent interpersonal and communication skills Interest in state-of-the-art imaging technology, and in applying this technology to meet the scientific objectives of Shared Resource users Experience with light-sheet imaging, intravital imaging, and/or real-time metabolic analysis is desirable, but not required"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191827": {
      "title": "Faculty - All Ranks - Instructor /Assistant Professor / Associate Professor/ Professor  (All Surgical Specialities)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Surgery Location: 02 The Department of Surgery at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons of Columbia University is accepting applications for surgeons on to qualify for the rank of the Assistant Professor. Responsibilities include seeing patients in an outpatient office at one or more of our office locations and on call hospital responsibilities. The scope of practice will include on-call responsibilities and building an outpatient practice. In addition to clinical duties, the incumbent will teach medical students, residents and fellows. Responsibilities include participation in the academic and research missions of the University and the Department of Surgery. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with the candidate's experience and credentials. A board-eligible Surgeon would be appointed at the Instructor rank until board certiﬁed. MD or DO. Completion of General Surgery Residency and Specialitty Fellowship if applicable. Completion of Cardthorracic Residency and Specialitty Fellowship if applicable. Board certified or eligible by the American Board of Surgery, American Board of Thoracic Surgery or it's equivalent."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191897": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medical Psychology (in Psychiatry) at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: CUMC The candidate at the rank of Assistant Professor will support a focused research program under the direction of Dr. Katherine Burdick and the Mitochondrial Psychobiology Group at Columbia University. Reporting relationships are outlined and clarified below. The candidate will assist and collaborate on exploring how applied physical energy inputs (light, electromagnetic fields) influence mitochondrial biology, cellular and physiological states, and human experiences. Applied energy-based technologies are increasingly used in clinical and broad health care settings—including neuroimaging and neuromodulatory techniques—yet their biological mechanisms and integration into biomedical science remain underrecognized and underdeveloped. Examined with an adequate level of scientific exploration and translation efforts, these energy-based technologies have the potential to enhance medical and psychiatric care. The Mitochondrial Psychobiology Group provides established platforms for quantitative bioenergetic assessment across cellular and human models. Key Job Responsibilities: There is a critical need to rigorously study these effects, and to translate and communicate findings in a way that enables adoption within psychiatry and across the biomedical sciences. -       Co-develop a shared experimental and conceptual framework linking energy dynamics to health and disease. -       Disseminate findings through peer-reviewed publications and scientific conferences. -       Develop workshops and interdisciplinary forums to introduce biophysical approaches to the field of psychiatry and other audiences. -       Expand collaborative networks to position the biophysics of energy as an emerging pillar within biomedical research. -       Validated experimental protocols for electromagnetic and photonic modulation of mitochondrial bioenergetics in established cultured human cells. -       Datasets linking applied energy inputs to mitochondrial, biochemical, and physiological outcomes. -       Initial reference dataset of optical signatures correlated with mitochondrial functional metrics. -       Human study design and implementation for energy sensing—mitoception-focused longitudinal protocols. -       Co-authored peer-reviewed publications. -       Mentorship and training of junior researchers across all project areas. -       Must have a MD, Ph.D. or equivalent degree -       Must have at least 1 – 3 years of postdoc experience"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191951": {
      "title": "2026 Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Climate School, Columbia University",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Climate School Location: New York, NY The Climate School Postdoctoral Research program is the premier program in the world for those dedicated to a better understanding of critical scientific and social issues in global sustainable development. Columbia Climate School Postdoctoral Researchers will join multidisciplinary teams of outstanding, committed scientists from across the Climate School and Columbia University. Candidates must have received their doctoral degrees (Ph.D., M.D., or J.D.) within three years prior to the start of the appointment. All doctoral requirements must be fulfilled and the degree awarded before the start of the program. Gaps in employment or studies will be considered on a case-by-case basis. The program is open to U.S. and non-U.S. citizens. Columbia University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer. Minorities and women are encouraged to apply. Columbia University policy only allows a cumulative maximum of five years in a postdoctoral position, whether held at or outside of the university. If you have questions about the timing of your doctoral degree or prior postdoc experience, please contact postdocs@climate.columbia.edu to discuss your application. Required materials: Online Application Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter (Brief Personal History) Format: Two (2) page maximum; double-spaced; one-inch margins; minimum 11-point standard font. The personal statement should include the following elements based on their relevance to your situation: Brief personal introduction and research statement 1 paragraph addressing the Climate School’s mission Summary of any discussions you have had with potential mentors Explanation of gaps in employment or studies (these will be considered on a case-by-case basis). Research Proposal Format: Five (5) page maximum; double-spaced; one-inch margins; minimum 11-point standard font. Bibliographic references are required but not counted toward the 5-page maximum. They should be included in the same document. The research proposal should address how the proposed work will contribute to the goal of global sustainable development. Proposals may suggest participating in, contributing to, or extending existing multidisciplinary Columbia Climate School projects. Candidates are also encouraged to develop new, innovative, projects that connect Climate School expertise. Three (3) Recommendation Letters Three (3) recommendation letters are required by October 30, 2026. In addition to submitting the above-required materials, applicants are strongly encouraged to identify and contact their desired multidisciplinary mentoring team, i.e., two or more senior faculty members or research scientists/scholars at Columbia University (who hold the rank of Assistant Professor / Research Scientist or higher) with whom they would like to work during their appointment. We encourage scholars who have a specific research project in mind to contact the appropriate faculty member. An endorsement from a faculty member or researcher does not guarantee acceptance into the program."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191966": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Cell Engineering and Therapy (Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine  - Cell Engineering & Therapy Location: 02 The Columbia Institute for Cell Engineering and Therapy (CICET) at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons (VP&S) invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor or Associate Professor. We seek outstanding scientists pursuing basic and/or translational research that advances cell and gene therapies. VP&S is part of Columbia University Irving Medical Center, which comprises 30 basic and clinical departments complemented by over 20 interdisciplinary centers. Clinical investigators work in close partnership with NewYork-Presbyterian (NYP), one of the nation’s leading academic health systems. The faculty member will hold an interdisciplinary appointment in CICET and in the VP&S department that best aligns with their expertise. About CICET CICET is a university-wide academic initiative dedicated to discovering and developing transformative cell- and gene-based therapies through interdisciplinary research and translational innovation. This recruitment is part of CICET’s continued investment in building a collaborative faculty group focused on pioneering therapeutic approaches with strong translational potential. CICET provides a highly collaborative academic environment that enables interdisciplinary research spanning fundamental discovery, translational development, and clinical application. Faculty have access to shared institutional resources, robust translational infrastructure, and CICET’s core laboratories, which will be located in the medical center’s new Vagelos Innovation Laboratories (VIL) building. This environment is further strengthened by a state-of-the-art cell and gene therapy GMP facility, regulatory support, cell and gene therapy clinical trials management, and a broad network of investigators and physician-scientists across Columbia University. This network includes leaders in all fields of medicine, bioengineering, computational biology, immunology, stem cell biology, systems biology, biochemistry, artificial intelligence, chemical engineering, and more. Together, these strengths create an open and dynamic ecosystem that fosters productive cross-disciplinary collaboration in translational cell engineering and in the development and implementation of cell and gene therapies. Position Overview and Responsibilities The successful candidate will establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program aligned with CICET’s mission. This position offers the opportunity to pursue high-impact scholarship in a collaborative academic environment that values scientific excellence, innovation, and translational relevance. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: innovative cell-based therapies, cell and gene delivery platforms, genetic engineering technologies, and translational research across a broad range of diseases, including cancer, autoimmunity, infectious diseases, transplantation, neurodegeneration, and monogenic disorders. Faculty responsibilities include research, teaching, mentorship, and service consistent with the expectations for tenure-track faculty at Columbia University. The faculty member will be expected to: Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program Conduct original, high-impact cell engineering and gene therapy research aligned with CICET’s mission and priorities Engage in interdisciplinary collaboration with investigators across Columbia University Contribute to the teaching and mentoring of graduate students, medical trainees, and postdoctoral fellows Participate in academic service activities that support the mission of CICET and the University Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D., M.D., M.D./Ph.D., or equivalent degree Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of research excellence and scholarly productivity Clear potential or track record to establish an independent, externally funded research program Commitment to teaching, mentorship, and collaborative scholarship Preferred Qualifications for Associate Level Applications: Evidence of a productive publication record and national reputation in their field Review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Required documents include: Cover letter: Applicants must clearly indicate whether they are applying for the Assistant Professor or Associate Professor position. Curriculum vitae Research statement describing past accomplishments and future research directions Assistant Professor: 2–3 pages Associate Professor: 3–4 pages Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy Letters of Reference Applicants should upload three letters of reference, if applying for this position. Letters can be uploaded by the candidate via the other document section OR the candidate can request letters via interfolio under the letter tab directly from their recommender, which is completely confidential. If letters are still pending, applicants should include the name and contact information for the referees. Please contact Ms. Courtney Mead at cm4753@cumc.columbia .edu if you have any questions. The annual salary range for these positions: Assistant Professor: $160,000 - $180,000 Associate Professor: $200,000 - $230,000 For questions, please contact Ms. Eden Bechard: eb3712@cumc.columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191996": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Instructor, or Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pathology Location: 02 Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) is seeking a highly motivated Genetic Counselor to join the Interdepartmental Genetic Counseling Program, administered through the Department of Pathology and Cell Biology. The successful candidate will provide comprehensive clinical genetic counseling services while collaborating closely with multidisciplinary clinical teams, molecular diagnostic laboratories, and research investigators to advance precision medicine across Columbia University. While the position is administratively based in the Department of Pathology and Cell Biology, approximately 80% of the incumbent's effort will be dedicated to clinical, research, and educational activities within the Department of Medicine, with the remaining effort supporting the Interdepartmental Genetic Counseling Program. This position offers a unique opportunity to integrate patient care, genomic medicine, research, and education within one of the nation's leading academic medical centers. Clinical Responsibilities Provide genetic counseling services to patients and families under limited supervision. Obtain detailed medical and family histories, perform risk assessment, and coordinate appropriate genetic testing. Explain the benefits, limitations, and potential outcomes of genetic testing to patients and families. Interpret genomic test results and communicate findings to patients, families, and referring providers. Prepare clinical documentation and summary reports. Serve as a liaison between clinical providers and the Precision Genomics Laboratory (PGL), the Laboratory of Personalized Genomic Medicine (PGM), and other molecular diagnostic laboratories. Participate in multidisciplinary specialty clinics and genomic medicine initiatives across CUIMC. Research Responsibilities Support genomic research studies through patient identification, recruitment, informed consent, and coordination of study procedures. Review medical records and assist with phenotype collection and data management. Maintain study documentation and ensure compliance with IRB requirements and institutional policies. Participate in variant interpretation and contribute to genotype-phenotype correlation efforts. Collaborate with investigators on research projects, abstracts, presentations, and scientific manuscripts. Educational Responsibilities Provide education to physicians, trainees, nurses, genetic counselors, and other healthcare professionals regarding genomic medicine and genetic testing. Participate in educational conferences, professional meetings, and continuing education activities. Contribute to teaching and mentorship of students, residents, fellows, and other trainees as appropriate. Key Skills Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Excellent organizational and project management abilities. Ability to manage multiple clinical and research priorities simultaneously. Commitment to compassionate, patient-centered care. Interest in advancing precision medicine through clinical care, research, and education. Minimum Education Qualifications Master's degree in Genetic Counseling. Other Qualifications Board Certified or Board Eligible by the American Board of Genetic Counseling (ABGC). Eligibility for New York State licensure as a Genetic Counselor. Preferred Qualifications Experience in clinical genetic counseling within an academic medical center. Experience coordinating genomic testing and interpreting genetic test results. Experience participating in clinical research, including patient enrollment and regulatory compliance. Interest in scholarly activities, including presentations and scientific publications. Spanish or other foreign language proficiency is desirable but not required. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within multidisciplinary clinical and research teams. Application Instructions All candidates must submit a CV Hiring Salary Range: $108,748.00- $108,748.00"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192065": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Music Location: 01 The Department of Music at Columbia University invites applications for a full-time appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Full Professor with a specialization in Music Composition and Music Technology to begin July 1, 2027.  The successful candidate will be a composer with a record of superior artistic work and sustained creative activity appropriate to their professional stage, complemented by technical expertise and technological research that advance the department’s legacy of innovation in music technology and interdisciplinary collaboration across the university and beyond. Responsibilities will include service in the Computer Music Center’s rotating Director role; teaching, curricular development, and advising at the undergraduate and graduate levels, including university-wide and non-major offerings; and active engagement in area, departmental, and university service. A doctoral degree in Composition or a related field is required by the time of appointment. PhD or DMA.  Candidates must demonstrate excellent promise for future development as scholars and teachers. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: -Cover letter that includes statements of teaching philosophy and research focus; -Curriculum vitae including teaching experience, detailed technological knowledge, and a record of creative activities; -Electronic links to three works completed within the past five years. Please do not submit older works or more than three works. Links must be directly accessible without requiring an account, permission request, or file download; -Contact information for three recommenders. Letters are not required at this stage. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192137": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medicine at the Columbia University Medical Center",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Preventive Medicine Location: 02 Columbia University’s Department of Medicine, Division of Preventive Medicine and Nutrition invites applications for a clinical faculty position. Candidates should have strong clinical and teaching qualifications. Responsibilities will include direct patient care, the supervision and teaching of housestaff (residents and fellows) and medical students and active participation in Departmental activities. Minimum Qualifications: MD or equivalent professional degree required.  Candidate must be board certified or board eligible and must be eligible to practice in the State of New York. Preferred Qualifications: Training and/or experience in Preventive Medicine and/or Nutrition Medicine. Hiring Salary Range: $120,000 - $120,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192190": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pharmacology Location: 02 The Abate-Shen laboratory in the Department of Molecular Pharmacology and Therapeutics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is seeking a Senior Staff Associate I to provide research support for the development and analyses of models of cancer. This new position will develop human models of cancer. This will entail coordinating with clinical faculty to obtain patient samples and to develop patient derived organoid and xenograft models. Functional Knowledge Must have extensive laboratory experience and functional knowledge of the development and analyses of human models of cancer. Experience with the collection of human patient data and samples is preferred - 30% Problem Solving Works with a team of lab members and clinicians to collect patient samples and generate models - 10% Decision Making/Autonomy Carries out research projects with limited PI supervision both collaboratively and independently - 10% Technical Expertise Must have extensive technical expertise in analyses of human models of cancer. Knowledge and/or experience in dealing with patients is strongly preferred - 30% Communication Must have excellent written communication skills, particularly the ability to clearly record data in written form or on a computer. Must be able to communicate with patients to obtain patient consents - 20% Bachelor's degree in a specific research field; Master's degree or equivalent combination of education and experience is preferred 6-10 years of experience"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192256": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor (Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Systems Biology Location: 02 The Department of Systems Biology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center in New York City invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor. We seek exceptional scientists developing and applying quantitative approaches to address fundamental questions in biology and biomedicine. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, experimental and computational systems biology, AI and machine learning for biology, systems immunology, synthetic biology and engineered therapeutics, functional genomics, single-cell and spatial biology, mechanistic and multiscale modeling, and innovative approaches for generating, analyzing, and interpreting large-scale biological data. The successful candidate will join a collegial and highly collaborative community of investigators within the Department of Systems Biology and across Columbia University. Columbia offers an exceptionally vibrant environment for interdisciplinary research, bringing together expertise in basic and translational biomedical sciences, computational and data sciences, engineering, and clinical medicine across its campuses and the broader New York City scientific community, with access to world-class research, computational, and clinical resources. Assistant Professor: $150,000-$160,000 Associate Professor: $190,000-$210,000 Columbia University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer. Applicants must hold a Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent doctoral degree and have a strong record of applying quantitative approaches to biological or biomedical problems. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a research statement (maximum three pages) describing current and proposed research. Three letters of reference are also required. Applications received by November 1, 2026, will receive full consideration; review will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192294": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Intramurals, Club Sports, and Camps, and Associate in the Department of Intercollegiate Athletics and Physical Education",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Intercollegiate Athletics Location: 01 Position Summary Columbia University's Department of Intercollegiate Athletics and Physical Education offers elite-level student-athletes the ability to compete for and win Ivy League titles and NCAA Championships. With 32 varsity sports, Columbia Athletics is the largest NCAA Division I athletics program in New York City and the only NCAA Division I sports program in Manhattan. A beautiful and peaceful campus in the heart of New York City, Columbia offers the most diverse student body in the Ivy League. Columbia Athletics has positioned itself with elite-level coaches and top-notch facilities in the Campbell Sports Complex, Baker Athletics Complex, the Milstein Tennis Center, and Dodge Fitness Center. The Assistant Director of Intramurals, Club Sports, and Camps is responsible for managing the intramural program and providing administrative assistance for the Director of Intramurals and Club Sports and Director of Camps. Responsibilities Manage the recruiting, hiring, training, scheduling, supervising, and evaluating of approximately 40 student intramural employees and 30 sports camp employees per year. Conduct day-to-day operations of all intramural activities including but not limited to: manage IMLeagues & Fusion set up, marketing, facility scheduling, review policies and rules, record game results, staff payroll, equipment orders/inventory, risk management, prepare annual and quarterly reports. Maintain excellent participant and member relations, effectively communicating with intramural Participants, club sport officers and camper parents/guardians to facilitate high levels of customer service. Assist in day-to-day management of the Club Sports program including but not limited to: budget management, travel coordination, officer training, facility scheduling, roster management. Assist in day-to-day operations of camp programming including but not limited to: budget management, marketing, risk management, planning camp activities, attendance, facility scheduling. Teach one course in the required physical education program per semester. Contributing to the maintenance of good working relationships with all members of the Department and assuming other duties as assigned by the Director of Intercollegiate Athletics and Physical Education Exhibiting the highest professional standards and ethical behavior with regard to adherence to NCAA, Ivy League, University, and Department of Intercollegiate Athletics code of ethics and rules and regulations. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree and/or equivalent related experience required 0 - 2 years related experience Excellent verbal and written communication, organization, management, leadership, and professional skills. Ability to collaborate effectively as a member of a goal-oriented team. A working computer knowledge using Microsoft Office and Adobe products. Knowledge and experience with safety and risk management, as it pertains to both intramural sports and camp settings. Preferred Qualifications Advanced degree in kinesiology, recreation management, sports administration or closely related field. One (1+) year of experience in a camp or youth program setting. Officiating experience in organized or recreational sports setting"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192400": {
      "title": "Staff Associate III",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: CUMC The Division of Pathophysiology & Treatment Research in Department of Psychiatry is looking for a full time staff associate lll to work within the Computational Psychiatry at Columbia University (https://www.columbiapsychiatry.org/research-labs/horga-lab), directed by Dr. Guillermo Horga. This job will consist of developing and conducting behavioral and neuroimaging studies in humans with or without psychiatric conditions. This position serves to support fMRI studies in the lab, providing guidance on the technical aspects of neuroimaging  6/04 HR Compensation, 615 W. 131st Street, Studebaker Building 4th Floor, New York, NY 10027 2 of 6 acquisition and implementing computational analyses on data. It is responsible for conducting neuroimaging experiments, performing analyses on data, and interpreting results. The position will ensure studies run smoothly and have sufficient scientific and analytical support. Applicants must have a bachelor's degree in a related field (e.g., neuroscience, psychology, biology, computer science, or a related discipline) and a master's degree in neuroscience, bioinformatics, biostatistics, computational neuroscience, or a closely related field. Additionally, applicants must have 4-6 years of relevant experience. Applicants should have 4–6 years of research experience in the design, preprocessing, and analysis of functional MRI (fMRI) datasets. Demonstrated expertise in quantitative data analysis, statistical modeling, and computational methods is required. Excellent programming skills, with proficiency in Python, MATLAB, and other scientific programming languages, are highly desirable. Applicant must complete application questions and upload their CV."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192485": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Associate/Professor of Dermatology (Tenure Track/Tenured)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Dermatology Location: 02 Columbia University’s Department of Dermatology invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor . This position will lead new research initiatives for the Irving Skin Cancer Center in collaboration with the Department Chair, Dr. Aimee Payne, aimed at advancing the department’s legacy of transformative research and clinical innovation. About Columbia Dermatology: Columbia Dermatology has over a century-long tradition of excellence in dermatologic research, education, and patient care. Our faculty have pioneered landmark discoveries including: The use of corticosteroids for autoimmune skin diseases Development of the antifungal agent nystatin Innovations in photopheresis and Janus kinase inhibitors Advances in precision medicine, tissue engineering, and diagnostics The Department is internationally recognized for its contributions to skin cancer, autoimmunity, bioengineering, wound healing, translational science, and more. Responsibilities: Lead and expand research initiatives within the Irving Skin Cancer Center Develop and maintain an independent, externally funded research program in basic, translational, or clinical dermatology relevant to skin cancer Collaborate across CUIMC and Columbia University to foster program project grants and interdisciplinary research in skin cancer and related fields Mentor trainees and junior faculty Contribute to teaching and service within the department and university M.D., or M.D./Ph.D. (or equivalent) in a relevant field Demonstrated excellence in research and scholarship in skin cancer and related fields Commitment to advancing dermatologic science and improving patient outcomes Eligibility for appointment at the Associate Professor level within CUIMC. Interested applicants should submit: A cover letter outlining research interests and career goals. Curriculum vitae."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192539": {
      "title": "Instructor in Dermatology - Cutaneous Oncology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Dermatology Location: 02 Position Summary The Department of Dermatology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center seeks a full-time Instructor in Dermatology with a focused academic and clinical interest in cutaneous oncology. This non-tenured junior faculty position is designed for a board-eligible or board-certified dermatologist pursuing an academic career in dermatology with advanced expertise in cutaneous oncology, medical education, and clinical and translational research. The initial appointment is anticipated to be for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on academic and clinical performance, programmatic needs, and availability of funding. The Instructor will maintain an independent dermatology practice, provide resident education through clinical supervision in outpatient clinic and inpatient dermatology consultation coverage, and receive advanced mentored clinical experience in the Comprehensive Cutaneous Oncology Center. 0.2 FTE will be allocated for research and academic development. The position has a particular emphasis on cutaneous lymphoma while providing broader experience in melanoma, Merkel cell carcinoma, advanced and high-risk non-melanoma skin cancers, and dermatologic complications of systemic cancer therapy. About Columbia Dermatology Columbia Dermatology has over a century-long tradition of excellence in dermatologic research, education, and patient care and is internationally recognized for its contributions to skin cancer, autoimmunity, bioengineering, wound healing, translational science, and more. Our faculty have pioneered landmark discoveries including: The use of corticosteroids for autoimmune skin diseases Development of the antifungal agent nystatin Innovations in photopheresis and Janus kinase inhibitors Cell and gene therapies in dermatology Advances in precision medicine, tissue engineering, and diagnostics The department has one of the largest academic dermatopathology practices in the country, including a national referral service for complex skin tumors, lymphoproliferative disorders, and inflammatory conditions, including immunohistochemistry, RNA in situ hybridization, next generation sequencing, and other advanced diagnostic panels. Clinical and Academic Responsibilities Independent Dermatology Practice – 0.2 FTE The Instructor will maintain an independent faculty dermatology practice within ColumbiaDoctors, providing evaluation and longitudinal management of patients with general and complex medical dermatologic conditions. Responsibilities will include diagnostic evaluation, dermatologic procedures and biopsies as appropriate, development and implementation of treatment plans, clinical documentation, coordination of care, and communication with referring physicians and other members of the healthcare team. Resident Clinical Teaching and Supervision – 0.1 FTE The Instructor will participate in the clinical education and supervision of Columbia dermatology residents one half-day per week in outpatient clinic and 2 weeks per year through on-call inpatient consult coverage at NewYork-Presbyterian/CUIMC facilities. Responsibilities will include direct resident preceptorship and supervision of patient care, including teaching of diagnostic and therapeutic decision-making, review of pathology and laboratory findings, procedural instruction, and case-based education. Advanced Cutaneous Oncology – 0.45 FTE The Instructor will participate in advanced, mentored clinical care under Dr. Geskin within the Comprehensive Cutaneous Oncology Center at CUIMC. The major clinical focus will be cutaneous lymphomas, including mycosis fungoides, Sézary syndrome, primary cutaneous B-cell lymphomas, and other rare cutaneous lymphoproliferative disorders. Clinical activities will include collaboration with dermatopathology, hematology-oncology, medical oncology, radiation oncology, surgical oncology, dermatologic surgery, and other relevant specialties, as well as infusion ordering. Additional experience will include the evaluation and management of patients with: Melanoma; Merkel cell carcinoma; Advanced and high-risk cutaneous squamous cell and basal cell carcinomas; Other uncommon cutaneous malignancies; and Dermatologic complications of systemic cancer therapy. The Instructor will develop advanced expertise in diagnosis, staging, longitudinal disease assessment, treatment selection, systemic and biologic therapies including cancer infusion therapy, treatment-related toxicity, and multidisciplinary management of patients with cutaneous malignancies. The Instructor will also participate in multidisciplinary cutaneous lymphoma tumor boards and other cutaneous oncology conferences. Research and Academic Development – 0.25 FTE The Instructor will have 25% effort for research and academic development. Academic development includes participation in Dermatology Grand Rounds and the opportunity to meet with visiting lecturers. The research program will emphasize cutaneous oncology and cutaneous lymphoma and may include clinical, translational, biomarker, epidemiologic, outcomes, artificial intelligence, and clinical-trial research. Activities may include, but are not limited to: development of independent research questions and protocols; prospective and retrospective clinical studies; investigator-initiated and industry-sponsored clinical trials (described further below); clinical databases, registries, and outcomes research; translational studies using patient-derived tissue and blood specimens; biomarker discovery and validation; molecular and immunologic characterization of cutaneous malignancies; development of diagnostic and prognostic tools; collaboration with dermatopathology, genomics, biostatistics, computational biology, and biomedical informatics; preparation of abstracts, manuscripts, and scientific presentations; and preparation of foundation, society, career-development, and other extramural grant applications. A major objective of the protected research time will be development of an increasingly independent area of investigation that can serve as the foundation for the Instructor’s future academic career and extramural funding. Clinical Research and Clinical Trials The Instructor will have the opportunity to participate as a sub-investigator in investigator-initiated and industry-sponsored clinical trials in cutaneous lymphoma and other cutaneous malignancies. Depending on the individual study, responsibilities may include protocol development, feasibility assessment, patient identification and screening, informed consent, study visits, standardized disease assessments, toxicity monitoring, data interpretation, and preparation of abstracts and manuscripts. The Instructor will gain experience in the regulatory and operational aspects of clinical investigation and will work with research coordinators, institutional research administration, regulatory personnel, sponsors, and collaborating investigators. Required qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree; successful completion of an ACGME-accredited dermatology residency prior to the start of the appointment; board eligibility or board certification in dermatology; eligibility for New York State medical licensure; eligibility for appointment to the faculty of Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons; demonstrated interest in academic dermatology and cutaneous oncology; and evidence of prior scholarly activity. Preferred qualifications: prior research experience in dermatology, oncology, cutaneous lymphoma, or a related field; peer-reviewed publications and/or scientific presentations; experience with clinical or translational research; experience with clinical trials; and demonstrated commitment to medical education and an academic career. Interested applicants should submit: A cover letter outlining research interests and career goals Curriculum vitae At least one letter of support for the candidate’s academic and research qualifications and goals from a prior research faculty mentor"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192605": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Earth Institute: Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory (LDEO) Location: 04 The Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory of Columbia University invites applications for the Postdoctoral Fellowships in the fields of Earth, Environmental, and Climate Sciences. Candidates should have recently completed their Ph.D. or should expect to complete their degree requirements by September 2027. Researchers at the Observatory work to understand the dynamics of the Earth's chemical, physical, and biological systems, from the core to the upper atmosphere, including Earth-human interactions. Our scientists lead research in the fields of solid Earth dynamics; ocean, atmospheric, and climate systems; cryospheric dynamics; paleoclimate; bio-geoscience; and solutions to air, water or soil contamination impacting communities. An opportunity also exists for a fellowship in forest ecology with a fieldwork component at Black Rock Forest. See www.blackrockforest.org/black-rock-forest-postdoctoral-fellowship-program/ to explore research opportunities and the Black Rock Forest-specific fellowship details. The principal selection criteria for Fellows are scientific excellent and a clearly expressed plan to investigate problems at the forefront of Earth Science. Applications from all related fields are welcom. Candidates with a machine learning and AI background are encouraged to apply. Interested candidates are strong encouraged to contact potential mentors as early as possible. To find prospective mentors, you may read about the research conducted by our Professors, Lamont Research Professors, and Research Scientists at https://lamont.columbia.edu/research-divisions . Fellowships are supported institutionally for 24 months, include a $12,000 research allowance, and carry an annual salary of $72,116. Successful candidates will be encouraged to apply for external funding and may be eligible for further internal awards and positions. Lamont is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive culture. We encourage applications from individuals of all backgrounds, including those who are traditionally underrepresented in the fields of earth, environmental, and climate sciences. Candidates should have recently completed their Ph.D. or should expect to complete their degree requirements by September 2027. Columbia University benefits accompany this Officer of Research appointment. For more information, and to apply for the fellowship, please visit: http://www.ldeo.columbia.edu/postdoc We accept online applications only."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192621": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scholar",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Interdisciplinary: Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race (CSER) Location: 01 The Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race at Columbia University invites applications for a two-year, non-renewable, residential post-doctoral fellowship in Asian American History, beginning on September 1, 2027. We welcome a variety of methodological approaches and embrace an expansive vision of Asia and its influence on the Americas. The Fellow will be appointed as a post-doctoral research scholar in the Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race (CSER) http://www.cser.columbia.edu/. The Fellow will pursue their own research and writing and teach one course per semester, while participating in programming at CSER and History and in the rich intellectual life at Columbia. The fellowship includes a salary plus benefits. Postdoctoral Research Scholar Salary Range: $72,116 Lecturer (part-time) salary range: $10,000 (per course) The Search Committee will begin to review applications on December 15, 2026. Questions about the position can be directed to Karl Jacoby kj2305@columbia.edu Minimum Degree Requirement: The fellowship is open to candidates who have received their Ph.D. degree in the humanities or social sciences after May 1, 2024. Candidates must have successfully completed all requirements for their doctoral degree by August 31, 2027. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting system (ASR/Interfolio). Please upload the following required materials: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover letter 3) Syllabus for a class in Asian American History 4) Names of three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192684": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Biochemistry and Molecular Biophysics Location: 02 Columbia University Medical Center's Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biophysics seeks to hire a successful candidate for the position of Associate Research Scientist, in the lab of Dr. Laura Landweber. Primary Responsibilities: Biochemical and genetic studies of Oxytricha genome rearrangement, including Oxytricha cell culture and genetic manipulation for experiments. Bioinformatic analyses of Oxytricha and genome rearrangement. Training other lab members in Oxytricha manipulation and Tetrahymena biology and tools for its manipulation. Presenting clear oral and written summaries of research progress and planned directions. Being an active and participatory member of the lab, and contributing to a positive and welcoming research environment for all lab members. Other activities related to the research program of Dr. Laura Landweber. This position requires prior, specialized experience with Oxytricha cell culture/maintenance and experimental studies of Oxytricha genome rearrangement using biochemical and genetic approaches. The role also requires proficiency in basic bioinformatic analyses of Oxytricha genomic/rearrangement data and sufficient expertise in Tetrahymena biology and manipulation tools to train other lab members. Incumbent must have a PhD in molecular biology or related field. At least 3 years postdoctoral research experience in ciliate molecular biology. Incumbent must also be capable of conducting research independently with minimum oversight and mentor graduate students and technicians. Submit an application and attach a copy of your updated CV."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192685": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Physiology (in the Institute of Human Nutrition) - Tenure Track",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Institute for Human Nutrition Location: 02 The Institute of Human Nutrition (IHN) in collaboration with the Department of Physiology and Cellular Biophysics at Columbia University in the City of New York, seeks candidates for Assistant Professor in the tenure track. The IHN fosters a network of researchers at the cutting edge of their disciplines, all working together to advance the fields of nutritional and metabolic biology. Applicants must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree, an outstanding record of research accomplishments showing high level of productivity in their field and the potential to secure external funding and excellent teaching credentials. The successful candidate will use cutting edge technologies in the field of cellular, tissue and organismal metabolism in physiological and pathological conditions. Responsibilities include establishing a rigorous and independently funded research program in metabolic biology, supervising and mentoring students with diverse background. Successful candidates will be expected to participate in the teaching and mentoring of Masters students and PhD students within the IHN as well as other graduate training programs. All recruited IHN faculty will be fully supported by a structured mentoring and career committee. Successful candidates will be appointed to the Department of Physiology and Cellular Biophysics within the Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons (VP&S) of Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) and Columbia University (CU). They will be an integral part of a highly collaborative group of faculty working in a broad range of areas related to metabolism. Faculty in IHN are associated with the Graduate School of Arts and Sciences, and may have membership in other Institutes and Centers, including the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, the Naomi Berrie Diabetes Center, Columbia Stem Cell Initiative, and the Taub Institute, and have the opportunity to interact with faculty at Columbia University’s Morningside and Manhattanville Campuses. Minimum Degree Required: · Ph.D. and/or MD degree Minimum Qualifications: · Must demonstrate the ability to develop an internationally recognized extramural funded research program. Required Documents: · Cover Letter · Statement of Research with a description of significant research accomplishments and future research plans (four pages maximum) · Minimum of three (maximum of five) letters of reference"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192728": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Immunology Location: 02 Columbia University is a leader in biomedical research, and Columbia's Vagelos School of Physicians and Surgeons is amongst the top research universities and provides an outstanding intellectual environment. The Columbia Center for Translational Immunology (CCTI) at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is a leading center for Translational Immunology. Dr. Megan Sykes is seeking a highly motivated Associate Research Scientist in the Department of Medicine's Columbia Center for Translational Immunology (CCTI) to contribute to cutting-edge research focused on immune tolerance and transplantation. The successful candidate will play a leading role in externally and internally funded research projects, conducting studies involving mouse models of the human immune system and advancing our understanding of T cell immunology and transplant tolerance. This position involves leading bench-based research, performing data analysis, and interpreting and presenting research findings. The Associate Research Scientist will also prepare progress reports and manuscripts and contribute to studies of transplant tolerance in both preclinical humanized mouse models and human transplant recipients, including patients participating in related clinical trials. The individual will devote 100% effort to research activities within the Sykes Laboratory. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in Immunology, Transplantation Biology, Biomedical Sciences, or a related field. In-depth knowledge of laboratory equipment, research methodologies, and experimental protocols related to immunology and transplantation. Demonstrated ability to analyze, interpret, and present complex research data, including large datasets. Extensive expertise in the generation, maintenance, and analysis of human immune system (HIS) mouse models. Experience performing laboratory procedures and surgical techniques in mice. Preferred Qualifications Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and work effectively both independently and as part of a collaborative team. Demonstrated initiative, attention to detail, and a commitment to continuous learning. Proficiency in Microsoft Office, Adobe Illustrator, GraphPad Prism, FlowJo, and statistical analysis software. Prior experience with the handling, care, and treatment of laboratory mice. Experience managing collaborative and multidisciplinary research projects."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192768": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Instructor/Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor_GEM",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Emergency Medicine Location: 02 The Department of Emergency Medicine at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons is seeking highly skilled, board certified/board eligbile Emergency Medicine Physicians to support our general emergency medicine divisions at our NewYork-Presbyterian affiliated hospitals in Upper Manhattan and Westchester, NY.  Academic rank will be commensurate with experience. Responsibilities include expanding our capacity in the clinical diagnosis, counseling, and treatment of patients, educating and supervising students, residents, fellows, and advanced practice providers and participating in administrative and scholarly activities. Candidates with academic interest and/or fellowship training in Emergency Medicine * Pediatric Emergency Medicine * Critical Care * Disaster and Emergency Preparedness * Operations/Administration * Research * Quality and Safety are encouraged to apply.  Ideal candidate will have a demonstrated record of research, publication and scholarship. Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NewYork-Presbyterian is a premier academic institution with world-class clinical facilities and programs committed to excellence in patient care, research, education, and community service. NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital is ranked #1 in the NY metropolitan area and repeatedly named to the Honor Roll of America’s Best Hospitals. Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons is a top ten medical school in the nation with a superb, collaborative research environment. Columbia University Medical Center is an internationally recognized leader in the creation of new knowledge and therapies to improve health in individuals and populations with sponsored research totaling more than $600 million annually. MD, MD/PhD, DO degree NY state medical license NY state registered DEA Board certified or board eligible in Emergency Medicine Salary Ranges: Instructor $125,000 - $150,000 Assistant Professor at CUMC $319,884 - $359,884 Associate Professor at CUMC $350,805 -390,805 Professor at CUMC $384,812 - $424,812 Please submit your application, a cover letter including a statement of academic objectives , curriculum vitae , and contact information of three references. For additional information, please contact emrecruiting@cumc.columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192872": {
      "title": "Instructor/Assistant Professor  at CUMC-CL",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 Full time position is available for a psychologist to work in transplant as part of the Consultation-Liaison Psychiatry division.   The clinician will be primarily assigned to work in renal transplantation and work alongside transplant nephrologists, nurse practitioners and social workers in a liaison-role.  Most of the work will be in the outpatient clinic setting with some inpatient consultation. 1. The psychologist will provide assessments of adult candidates for renal transplant 2. The psychologist will provide some follow up as needed in the evaluation phase and post renal transplant especially as a bridge to more chronic mental health care.  A stepped-care approach will be applied, with collaborative care with social workers, nurse practitioners and transplant psychiatrists. 3. The psychologist will be a dedicated liaison to the renal team and attend selection committee and quality improvement meetings to provide their insight and recommendations. 4. The psychologist may also evaluate a select group of kidney donor candidates for surgery 5. The psychologist may also cross cover for inpatient evaluations for the three psychiatrists dedicated to other transplant groups in heart, liver and lung. The faculty member will be part of the clinical, research and educational mission of transplant psychiatry through the CL Psychiatry Division at Columbia.  The applicant should have some experience with conducting diagnostic interviews and administering and reviewing scales.  Applicants should have knowledge of both cognitive behavioral and motivational interviewing techniques.  Applicants should have strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work well across disciplines and function effectively as part of a multi-disciplinary team. Our faculty serve as part of multi-disciplinary teams with ample opportunities to learn from and consult with colleagues. There are also professional development funds available to faculty to support licensure renewals, as well as obtaining additional training, conference attendance, professional organization membership, and potentially supervise/provide didactics to trainees. Faculty will have opportunities to attend case conferences, peer supervision, and Grand Rounds at CUIMC. The CUIMC Psychiatry FPO is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive faculty and clinical service. We recognize that different backgrounds and experiences help to strengthen our department and provide better care. As part of your cover letter, if you choose, we welcome you to share how your personal and professional backgrounds would contribute to our department and mission. This role includes opportunities for additional compensation aligned with the department's comprehensive compensation plan. Demonstrate interest in the psychological care of patients with medical illness Demonstrate strong interpersonal skills and ability to work well in a team setting Demonstrate interest in academic as well as clinical psychiatry Additional preference to applications with interest in research and quality improvement Additional preference to applicants who are bilingual in Spanish PsyD or PhD in Clinical Psychology New York State license required Candidates may be required to obtain a New Jersey license For psychologists, Instructor level requires graduation from an APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology, and completion of an APA-accredited pre-doctoral internship program"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192942": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Physics Location: 01 Columbia Physics Department invites applications for the 2027 EKA Postdoctoral program for outstanding young scientists with research interests in any area of physics pursued at Columbia. The positions are co-sponsored by the Ernest Kempton Adams fund and a Columbia faculty member and come with travel and research funds. The positions may begin at any time between April 1, 2027 and Sept 1, 2027 and are for an initial term of one year extendible for up to two additional years, contingent on satisfactory performance and funding. We anticipate making two offers in 2027, one in theoretical/computational physics and another in experimental physics. Preference will be given to candidates with research interests in the areas of Astrophysics (including cosmology), or Condensed Matter Physics. It is expected that additional positions will be available in 2028. We will give preference to candidates who have obtained their degree in 2026, but are open to all candidates who received their PhD as early as 2024. A Ph.D. in physics or a related field at the time of appointment is required. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR). Please upload the following required materials: a curriculum vitae, statement of research interests and experience (3 page maximum), three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until position is filled. All inquiries should be directed to Ashley.Delphia@columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193010": {
      "title": "Instructor in Pediatrics (Cardiac Catheterization) at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY: This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of instructor an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. The desired candidate must be board certified/eligible in Pediatric Cardiology and will have completed a Pediatric Cardiology fellowship. The Advanced Fellowship in Pediatric and Congenital Interventional Cardiology is specialized program is designed for graduates of accredited Pediatric Cardiology Fellowship programs who desire advanced, intensive training in diagnostic and therapeutic cardiac catheterization. Our curriculum covers the entire lifespan of patients with congenital heart disease, providing comprehensive training from premature neonates to adult congenital heart disease (ACHD) populations. As an international leader in congenital heart care, our high-volume program exposes the advanced fellow to complex structural and vascular interventions, hybrid surgical-catheter procedures, and mechanical circulatory support platforms. The fellow will transition under direct faculty supervision to build exceptional technical skills, sophisticated clinical judgment, and true procedural autonomy. The Program Offers Premier Case Volume & Variety: Extensive hands-on experience in balloon angioplasty, valvuloplasty, complex vascular stenting, and transcatheter valve replacement (TPVR). Advanced Structural Interventions: Leading-edge training in device closures of septal defects (ASD, VSD, PDA), including extremely low-birth-weight premature infants. Innovative Multidisciplinary Care: Participation in hybrid operating room procedures and complex case planning with our world-renowned pediatric cardiothoracic surgery and mechanical support (ECMO) teams. Lifespan Congenital Training: Integrated clinical exposure through the Schneeweiss Adult Congenital Heart Center. Academic Excellence & Research: Robust mentorship for clinical, translational, or basic science research, with the expectation of abstract submission and manuscript publication. Dedicated Heart Team Conferences: Collaboration during multidisciplinary management conferences joining medical, imaging, surgical, and intensive care faculties. Program Directors & Leadership Christopher J. Petit, MD – Division Academic Chief, Pediatric Cardiology Alejandro J. Torres, MD – Director, Pediatric Catheterization Laboratory KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: · Maintain timely and complete EPIC chart notes, reports, and communication with primary care/referring physicians and other relevant subspecialty physicians. Participate in multidisciplinary clinical management initiatives with other subsections within Pediatric Cardiology. · Participate in division and department quality improvement initiatives and curriculum development, including within the department. · Attend patient rounds and complete consultation and inpatient notes. Comply with all billing, coding, documentation, and regulatory requirements. · Participate in divisional conferences, Department of Pediatrics grand rounds, morbidity and mortality conferences, and faculty development education. COMMITMENT OF DIVERSITY: Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and Educator.  The University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment, and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Columbia University welcomes applications from individuals who may have had nontraditional career paths, or who may have taken time off for family reasons (e.g. children, caring for disabled or elderly family), or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of academia (e.g., in professional or industry service).  The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and is committed to supporting the work-life balance of its faculty.  We are interested in candidates who have a record of success advising and mentoring individuals from groups underrepresented in higher education and is particularly interested in candidates who have research interests in subjects that will contribute to the understanding of diversity and equal opportunity. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS: · Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License · Must be Board certification in Pediatric Cardiology. · Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; · The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. COMPENSATION: Compensation arrangements are competitive and commensurate with both experience and achievement."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193013": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medicine at CUMC - Endocrinology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Endocrinology Location: 02 The Naomi Berrie Diabetes Center at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor of Medicine. This faculty position offers an opportunity to contribute to a leading academic diabetes program through excellence in clinical care, clinical research, and medical education at NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital/Columbia University Irving Medical Center (NYP/CUIMC). Responsibilities Develop and maintain an active outpatient diabetes practice (approximately eight weekly sessions) at our CUIMC location. Participate in the teaching and mentorship of medical students, residents, and endocrinology fellows in both inpatient and outpatient settings. Provide inpatient coverage for the Diabetes Consult Service at NYP/CUIMC. Engage in scholarly activities, including clinical and translational research, quality improvement initiatives, academic presentations, and publication of research findings that advance the field of diabetes, metabolism, and endocrinology. MD degree (or equivalent). Board certification or eligibility in Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism. Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of New York. Additional expertise in insulin pump therapy and continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) management is desirable but not required."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193021": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of French Location: 01 The Department of French seeks to appoint an Assistant Professor in the field of Caribbean and/or African Francophone literature and culture. The successful candidate will have deep knowledge of the literature, thought, and culture associated with one or both regions. A research focus on the twentieth and/or twenty-first centuries is preferred. Expertise in subfields including ecocriticism, film, new media, digital humanities, theater and performance, critical theory/philosophy, or gender and sexuality studies is highly desirable. The search committee will consider candidates with a PhD in disciplines other than French literature, but fluency in French is required. The PhD must be conferred by July 1, 2027. The tenure-track professor will usually teach four courses a year at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, including courses required for Columbia’s Core Curriculum. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and a strong commitment to student advising and participation in departmental initiatives are crucial. PhD The search committee will consider candidates with a PhD in disciplines other than French literature, but fluency in French is required. The PhD must be conferred by July 1, 2027. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: 1. Cover letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Teaching statement (describing teaching philosophy, experience and practices) 4. Writing sample (in English or French; no more than 25 double-spaced pages) 5. Names and contact information of 3 referees, who will be asked by a system-generated email to upload a letter of recommendation once the candidate’s application has been submitted. Letters received before October 15th will receive full consideration. Contact information: Joanna Stalnaker, Chair of the Search Committee: jrs2052@columbia.edu Nicole Meily, Director of Academic Administration and Finance: nam2214@columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193016": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Evolutionary Biology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology Location: 01 The Department of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology (E3B) at Columbia University invites applications for a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level. Applicants can use any combination of field, experimental, computational, or theoretical approaches to study fundamental questions in evolutionary biology. The successful candidate is expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program that complements and augments existing strengths within E3B, and to participate in undergraduate and graduate teaching. E3B is an intellectually diverse and stimulating department consisting of a vibrant and close-knit community. It has strong links to other departments and disciplines at the University, including evolutionary biologists working in other departments ( evolutionatcolumbia.org ), neuroscientists (Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute), earth and environmental scientists (Lamont Doherty Earth Observatory, the Earth Institute, and other centers in the Climate School), and disease experts (Mailman School of Public Health). E3B also has close ties with New York partner institutions, including the New York Genome Center, the American Museum of Natural History, the New York Botanical Garden, and the Wildlife Conservation Society. These connections provide ample opportunities for interaction and collaboration. Applications received by October 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. Please submit applications via Interfolio. Minimum Degree Required: Ph.D. Preferred Qualifications: A record of excellence and originality in research and strong interest in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentorship. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, a statement of research, a teaching and mentoring statement, PDFs of up to 3 significant publications, and 3 reference letters."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193026": {
      "title": "Instructor in Pediatrics (Advanced Cardiac Care and Transplantation) at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY/POSITION DESCRIPTION This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of instructor an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. The desired candidate must be board certified/eligible in Pediatric Cardiology and will have completed a Pediatric Cardiology fellowship. The PACCT team consists of six heart failure/transplant cardiologists directed by Dr. Marc Richmond, four cardiac surgeons led by Dr. Emile Bacha, and a multi-disciplinary team that includes five nurse practitioners, two pharmacists, two social workers and nurse transplant and VAD coordinators. Begun in 1984, our transplant program has performed transplant in over 650 patients and currently averages approximately 20-25 transplants annually. In addition, we have an established MCS program led by Dr. Sabrina Law, offering the full complement of temporary and durable devices including Syncardia Total Artificial Heart. The program has recently provided MCS to approximately 15-20 patients per year, including bridge-to-transplant in complex CHD patients and destination VAD therapy.  A close relationship with our surgical colleagues and adult VAD specialists offers a unique experience for novel approaches to VAD use.  Our program consists of an established cardio-genetics clinic under the supervision of Dr. Teresa Lee, as well as multidisciplinary clinic for cardio-oncology, advanced Fontan care and Neuromuscular diseases; our center is an HCMA Center of Excellence for the care of patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.  We have strong collaboration with our colleagues in ACHD and adult cardiomyopathy, heart failure and transplantation, participating in joint clinical conference and other opportunities. Our team has two inpatient services, comanaging patients in the cardiac ICU and serve as the primary attending for our patients on the cardiac floor working with pediatric residents and nurse practitioners. We have several outpatient clinics throughout the week to manage patients with cardiomyopathy, heart failure, and transplant, located at MSCH and at a satellite clinic in Westchester County, NY KEY RESPONSIBILITIES · Maintain timely and complete EPIC chart notes, reports, and communication with primary care/referring physicians and other relevant subspecialty physicians. Participate in multidisciplinary clinical management initiatives with other subsections within Pediatric Cardiology. · Participate in division and department quality improvement initiatives and curriculum development, including within the department. · Attend patient rounds and complete consultation and inpatient notes. Comply with all billing, coding, documentation, and regulatory requirements. Participate in divisional conferences, Department of Pediatrics grand rounds, morbidity and mortality conferences, and faculty development education. COMMITMENT OF DIVERSITY: Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and Educator.  The University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment, and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Columbia University welcomes applications from individuals who may have had nontraditional career paths, or who may have taken time off for family reasons (e.g. children, caring for disabled or elderly family), or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of academia (e.g., in professional or industry service).  The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and is committed to supporting the work-life balance of its faculty.  We are interested in candidates who have a record of success advising and mentoring individuals from groups underrepresented in higher education and is particularly interested in candidates who have research interests in subjects that will contribute to the understanding of diversity and equal opportunity. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS: · Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License · Must be Board certification in Pediatric Cardiology. · Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; · The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. COMPENSATION: Compensation arrangements are competitive and commensurate with both experience and achievement."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193031": {
      "title": "Instructor in Pediatrics (Pulmonary Hypertension) at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY/POSITION DESCRIPTION This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of instructor an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. The desired candidate must be board certified/eligible in Pediatric Cardiology and will have completed a Pediatric Cardiology fellowship. The Robyn J. Barst, M.D Fellowship in Pulmonary Hypertension is a specialized program designed for graduates of Pediatric Cardiology, Pulmonology, Critical Care or Neonatology and wish to pursue a career in Pediatric Pulmonary Vascular disease. During the course of the one-year fellowship program, the trainee will learn to diagnose, assess and manage patients with Pulmonary Hypertension. Fellows will become proficient in the interpretation of diagnostic data including imaging modalities (Echo, VQ Scans, CTA, cardiac MRI), pulmonary function testing, cardiopulmonary exercise testing, and measurement and interpretation of cardiopulmonary hemodynamics. The training curriculum will include exposure to extensive inpatient and outpatient management of Pulmonary Hypertension patients including diagnostics, medical and surgical therapeutics, ECMO as well as related research opportunities. The fellow also will participate in the diagnosis and management strategies of our Pulmonary Vein Stenosis program, Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia program and Bronchopulmonary Dysplasia Program; as well as gain experience with use of pulmonary vasodilators in single ventricle patients which is a growing population. The trainee will also have an opportunity to attend our multidisciplinary CDH clinic and participate in our Bronchopulmonary Dysplasia service. The trainee will have opportunities to participate in teaching and training of categorical fellows and staff. Fellows will be involved in scholarly activity including case-conferences and journal clubs and will have dedicated mentorship in the execution of PH/PVD-related research. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES · Maintain timely and complete EPIC chart notes, reports, and communication with primary care/referring physicians and other relevant subspecialty physicians. Participate in multidisciplinary clinical management initiatives with other subsections within Pediatric Cardiology. · Participate in division and department quality improvement initiatives and curriculum development, including within the department. · Attend patient rounds and complete consultation and inpatient notes. Comply with all billing, coding, documentation, and regulatory requirements. · Participate in divisional conferences, Department of Pediatrics grand rounds, morbidity and mortality conferences, and faculty development education. COMMITMENT OF DIVERSITY: Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and Educator.  The University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment, and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Columbia University welcomes applications from individuals who may have had nontraditional career paths, or who may have taken time off for family reasons (e.g. children, caring for disabled or elderly family), or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of academia (e.g., in professional or industry service).  The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and is committed to supporting the work-life balance of its faculty.  We are interested in candidates who have a record of success advising and mentoring individuals from groups underrepresented in higher education and is particularly interested in candidates who have research interests in subjects that will contribute to the understanding of diversity and equal opportunity. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS: Must have a doctorate degree (M.D. or Ph.D.) Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with                    professionals in other disciplines. The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each  other’s contributions. The ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. COMPENSATION: Compensation arrangements are competitive and commensurate with both experience and achievement."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193035": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 A postdoctoral research scientist position is available in the laboratory of Dr. Catarina M. Quinzii, at the H. Houston Merritt Clinical Research Center for the study of Neuromuscolar Disorders, in the Department of Neurology, at Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY. Job Responsibilities: The postdoc will be involved in projects focused on understanding primary mitochondrial pathology and the contribution of mitochondrial abnormalities to synapsis dysfunction in neurodegeneration using in vitro and in vivo models. The postdoc will perform molecular and biochemical studies, including immunoistochemistry, RNA interference, transfections, spectrophotometric assessments of mitochondrial respiratory chain enzyme activities and Seahorse Analyzer assays, CoQ measurement, oxidative stress analysis, western blots and qPCR. Under the supervision of Dr. Quinzii, they will analyze relevant literature, design and conduct experiments, analyze data and write manuscripts for publication. They will give oral presentations of research at internal (Columbia), national, and international meetings. Minimum Qualifications: PhD, MD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Neuroscience, Molecular Biology, Cell Biology, Biochemistry, Biomedical Sciences, Genetics, Physiology, or a related field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193139": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Anesthesiology Location: 02 The Department of Anesthesiology seeks an Associate Research Scientist with experience neuroscience and/or neuroanesthesia to investigate EEG-guided analgesic titration to improve early neurocognitive recovering in older patients. Job Responsibilities: The Associate Research Scientist will: Independently design, execute and analyze complex research studies Contribute to the development of new research hypotheses and experimental approaches within the lab’s focus area of neuroanesthesia and cognition Maintain detailed and accurate records and data Prepare manuscripts for publication in high-impact scientific journals, including data analysis, writing, and editing. Present research findings at scientific conferences and seminars. Assist in the preparation of grant applications to secure funding for research projects. Collaborate with other researchers within the laboratory and across disciplines to facilitate research progress. Mentor and train graduate students, technicians, and other junior lab members on experimental techniques and research protocols. Actively participate in lab meetings, journal clubs, and scientific discussions. Minimum Qualifications: PhD in biological science, neuroscience, neurology or other related field Excellent written and oral communication skills, including the ability to present research findings at conferences and in scientific manuscripts Ability to work independently and as a part of a team Strong critical thinking and problem-solving skill"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193243": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Biological Sciences invites applications for a position of Senior Staff Associate I (SSAI) to begin 01/01/2027. The Senior Staff Associate I will participate in research in neuroanatomy. He/she will culture unusual nematode species under aquatic conditions and perform high pressure freezing, animal sectioning, and electron microscopy analysis. He/She will be involved in confocal imaging experiments, patch-clamp experiments and animal experiments. Duties: Transmission electron microscopy, 40% Sample preparation (high pressure freezing and staining), 30% Nematode strain growth, 10% Data collection, visualization and analysis, 10% Equipment maintenance 10% B.A./B.Sc. in Biology/Biochemistry Skills: EM, confocal and compound microscope Electron Microscopy 3D tissue analysis C Elegans Research Nematode stain handling Molecular and cellular biology techniques At least 10 years experience All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR) . On the site, please upload cover letter, CV, and a list of 3 reference providers (who may be asked to provide a letter at a later date)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193245": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic Location: 01 The Division of Social Sciences seeks to hire one social scientist working on “AI and society” broadly defined, including the application of AI to innovative research questions and/or the implications of AI for society. The appointment will be a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor and may begin as early as July 1, 2027. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication profile and teach in the relevant undergraduate and graduate programs. The successful candidate will be appointed in one of the following departments: --African American and African Diaspora Studies --Anthropology --History --Sociology The successful candidate must have a completed the PhD by June 30, 2027, the time of appointment. Candidates from all social science fields are welcome to apply. We seek candidates with extraordinary promise of scholarly development and demonstrated accomplishments appropriate to time-from-degree. All applications must be submitted through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ARS) system. To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, an article-length writing sample, and three letters of recommendation. Applicants should specify in their cover letter which of the four departments would be a good affiliation based on their background or interests. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193261": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Physics Location: 01 The Department of Physics at Columbia University invites applications for an Assistant Professor position to start July 1, 2027. This position is open to recent PhD’s in the general area of theoretical/computational quantum condensed matter physics and will be a 50/50 joint appointment with the Center for Computational Quantum Physics (CCQ) of the Simons Foundation’s Flatiron Institute.  The appointee will spend half of their effort in each place, at Columbia at the rank of tenure track Assistant Professor and, at CCQ, at the rank of Associate Research Scientist. It is expected that the candidate will develop a vigorous independent research program, continuing the long Columbia record of forefront accomplishment in science, and will teach in the undergraduate and graduate programs. Applicants for this position must possess a doctoral degree in Physics or a related field and have demonstrated the ability to do pioneering theoretical and computational research as well as the promise of excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Review of applications and scheduling of interviews will begin on November 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Ph.D. All candidates must have a strong publication record and have demonstrated the potential to establish a vigorous individual research program and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. Candidates also must have the potential for future development as a scientist, scholar and teacher. In order to be considered for this joint appointment, candidates must submit separate applications BOTH through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system and through Simons Flatiron Institute’s Interfolio system. The application materials should include: A cover letter, A curriculum vitae A list of publications A research statement (current and planned research)- No more then 3 pages A teaching statement- No more then 3 pages Names and contact information of three scientists who will receive links at which they can upload their letters directly. For more information about the Physics Department please visit: http://physics.columbia.edu Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193328": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Senior Staff Associate is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers. The Department of Pediatrics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is the top in comparison with other pediatrics department in the NY region in NIH research funding. RESPONSIBILITIES: Works independently to design experiments, analyze data and present their findings. Develop and optimize protocols for imaging and image analysis experiments. A dministrative oversight for lab purchasing and lab management. Grows and maintains human immune cell cultures and tumor spheroids. Isolates human peripheral blood cells and processes for single cell analysis. Designs, executes, and analyzes confocal and super-resolution microscopy experiments. Performs biochemistry experiments. Assists with lab management including ordering and inventory. Performs related duties & responsibilities as assigned/requested. Performs technical maintenance and supervises use of high-end microscopy systems. Must have a Bachelor’s degree in specific research field and 6-10 years of experience. Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions; and The ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193336": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatrics at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of either Assistant Professor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. The Division of Allergy and Immunology in the Department of Pediatrics is seeking an academic faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor. The position will be a full-time Columbia University faculty position with benefits. The individual must be able to advance medical knowledge and translate it into clinically relevant strategies for diagnosing and treating allergy and immunology conditions. The individual will be joining an actively growing and expanding research and clinical operation covering a wide range of allergic and immunologic disorders and research programs.  The ideal candidate should be a physician-scientist able to develop niche clinical programs, perform laboratory-based research in a mentored setting, and compete for independent research funding. RESPONSIBILITIES Establish an independent research program in basic and translational immunology. Secure external grant funding for research programs. Establish scientific and translational collaborations with clinicians and immunologists within the division and across the institution. Deliver exceptional, specialized patient care with consistently excellent outcomes. Contribute to departmental initiatives, including quality improvement and patient safety efforts. Engage in continuing medical education (CME) and maintain relevant advanced certifications. Serve as a trusted resource for complex cases and high-acuity patients. Achieve and maintain high patient satisfaction scores. Meet or exceed productivity targets. Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License Must be board certified/board eligible in Pediatrics, or Internal Medicine, and Allergy/Immunology with clinical training in pediatrics. Prior history of research publications and funding that demonstrates an interest in pursuing an independent research career in the future Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193421": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Yuste Lab in the Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist position. The successful candidate will use advanced optical methods to investigate glutamate release onto the dendrites of Baz1a neurons in the mouse primary visual cortex in vivo. This research will test the hypothesis that dendrites function as independent computational compartments and will contribute to a deeper understanding of dendritic processing and neuronal function. Application screening will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Minimum Qualifications: PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Neuroscience or related scientific discipline. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR) . On the site, please upload cover letter, CV, and a list of 3 reference providers (who may be asked to provide a letter at a later date)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193434": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Dermatology Location: 02 The Department of Dermatology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) invites applications for the Associate Research Scientist position within our Basic Science Research Group. Responsibilities: Candidates should possess robust scientific and postdoctoral experience. Key responsibilities include running an independent research program, mentoring graduate students and postdocs, and actively participating in all departmental and program activities. Current research interests within the department include, but are not limited to: skin immunology, tissue engineering, cell and gene therapies, epidermal differentiation, skin stem cells, signal transduction, skin cancer (both non-melanoma and melanoma), mouse models of skin cancer, immuno-oncology, complex organotypic skin modeling, and autoimmune/autoinflammatory and inherited skin diseases such as alopecia areata, epidermolysis bullosa, hidradenitis suppurativa, pemphigus and atopic dermatitis. A Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent degree. At least two years of postdoctoral research experience with a track record of first-author publications from graduate/postdoctoral work. Proven ability to conduct independent research and to supervise graduate students and technicians. Candidates should apply online at Columbia Academic Careers and submit a CV along with a Letter of Interest and Career Goals to Jody-Ann McLeggon (jm5257@cumc.columbia.edu). Please indicate how your past experience will contribute to the work of the Principal Investigator whose laboratory you’d like to join."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193437": {
      "title": "Faculty- All Ranks (Assistant, Associate, Professor) - Associate Director, Regulatory & Safety, PET Center",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Radiology Location: 02 Position Summary: The Associate Director, Regulatory & Safety, PET Center is a pharmacist (PharmD) who provides leadership and operational oversight for radiopharmaceutical production, quality control, dispensing, and clinical PET imaging, and serves as a key institutional resource for patient safety, quality assurance, GMP manufacturing, regulatory affairs, and FDA compliance. The position operates at the intersection of clinical nuclear medicine, radiochemistry, radiopharmaceutical manufacturing, radiation safety, and regulatory affairs. The successful candidate will have experience with radiopharmaceutical production and clinical PET operations and will possess a strong understanding of FDA and other federal and state regulatory requirements governing radioactive materials, radiopharmaceuticals, and investigational drug products, including documentation of manufacturing processes, quality-control testing, batch records, deviations, corrective and preventive actions, and preparation for regulatory inspections. The role carries primary responsibility for maintaining regulatory and quality systems for the PET Center and serves as a principal point of contact for FDA inspections, radioactive-material licensing, radiopharmaceutical regulatory requirements, and institutional quality and safety programs. The Associate Director will work closely with radiochemists, nuclear medicine physicians, PET Center leadership, medical physicists, radiation safety personnel, research investigators, quality personnel, and institutional regulatory offices to ensure that radiopharmaceuticals are produced and administered safely, consistently, and in compliance with applicable regulatory and institutional requirements. This position represents an opportunity to help build and establish a state-of-the-art academic PET and radiopharmaceutical program supporting clinical care, translational research, and development of novel radiopharmaceuticals. Key Responsibilities Clinical PET and Radiopharmaceutical Operations ● Provide pharmacy expertise and operational oversight for clinical PET radiopharmaceutical procurement, receipt, storage, preparation, dispensing, and administration. ● Collaborate with nuclear medicine physicians, technologists, and other clinical personnel to ensure timely and appropriate availability and use of radiopharmaceuticals for clinical and research imaging. ● Develop and implement SOPs and quality processes for clinical radiopharmaceutical operations. ● Provide leadership for patient-safety and quality-assurance processes related to radiopharmaceutical preparation, dispensing, administration, and clinical PET operations. ● Support integration of new radiopharmaceuticals and imaging agents into clinical practice. Radiopharmaceutical Manufacturing and Radiochemistry ● Provide pharmacy and manufacturing expertise for production of PET radiopharmaceuticals in accordance with applicable FDA, USP, institutional, and radiation-safety requirements. ● Work closely with radiochemists and production personnel on development, validation, optimization, and implementation of radiopharmaceutical manufacturing processes. ● Maintain technical familiarity with automated radiochemistry synthesis equipment, including GE FX, FASTlab, and comparable automated synthesis systems, as well as associated modules, consumables, and production workflows. ● Review and approve production documentation, including master production records, batch production records, equipment logs, and related quality documentation. ● Provide pharmacy oversight at the interface between radiochemistry production and clinical PET operations, ensuring that radiopharmaceuticals released for patient use meet established specifications and requirements. ● Support translation of experimental radiopharmaceutical production methods into reproducible, documented, and clinically appropriate manufacturing processes. FDA Regulatory Compliance, Quality Systems, and Inspection Readiness ● Serve as a principal resource for FDA compliance, regulatory inspections, and federal and state requirements governing radiopharmaceuticals and radioactive materials. ● Maintain inspection readiness and coordinate preparation for FDA inspections, regulatory audits, and institutional reviews. ● Participate directly in inspections and serve as a subject-matter expert regarding radiopharmaceutical production, quality control, documentation, and clinical operations. ● Support preparation, maintenance, and amendment of radioactive-material licenses and related federal, state, and institutional regulatory documentation. ● Provide regulatory expertise for investigational radiopharmaceutical programs, including IND-related documentation and clinical research activities. ● Develop and maintain quality systems encompassing document control, SOPs, batch records, deviations, investigations, CAPA, change control, out-of-specification results, equipment qualification, process validation, and inspection readiness. ● Ensure that production, testing, and experimental activities are documented accurately, contemporaneously, completely, and in a manner suitable for regulatory review and clinical translation. ● Review quality-control results and ensure compliance with established specifications, including applicable testing for radiochemical purity, radionuclidic identity and purity, chemical purity, residual solvents, pH, sterility and endotoxin, and other release criteria. ● Coordinate responses to regulatory findings and oversee implementation and documentation of corrective actions. Radiation Safety ● Work closely with the institutional Radiation Safety Office and medical physics personnel to ensure safe handling, production, dispensing, and administration of radioactive materials. ● Maintain working knowledge of radiation protection principles, radioactive-material regulations, contamination control, shielding, radioactive waste management, radiation detection equipment, and personnel monitoring applicable to PET operations. ● Develop and implement radiation-safety procedures for radiopharmaceutical production and clinical use, and participate in radiation-safety investigations, inspections, and corrective actions as appropriate. ● Provide radiation-safety expertise for new radiopharmaceuticals, production processes, equipment, and facility modifications. Research and Translational Medicine ● Support investigators developing and translating novel radiopharmaceuticals from preclinical research through first-in-human and subsequent clinical studies. ● Provide pharmacy and regulatory expertise for investigational radiopharmaceutical manufacturing, clinical trial protocols, and IND-related activities. ● Collaborate with investigators, radiochemists, physicians, and regulatory teams to establish manufacturing and quality strategies appropriate for early-phase clinical trials. ● Review research radiopharmaceutical protocols from the perspectives of patient safety, pharmaceutical quality, regulatory compliance, and clinical feasibility. ● Contribute to an integrated translational infrastructure linking radiochemistry, manufacturing, PET imaging, dosimetry, regulatory compliance, and clinical research. Required Qualifications ● Doctor of Pharmacy (PharmD) or equivalent professional pharmacy degree. ● Current New York State pharmacist license or eligibility for licensure. ● Significant experience in hospital pharmacy, nuclear pharmacy, radiopharmacy, radiopharmaceutical production, or a closely related environment. ● Demonstrated understanding of radiopharmaceutical preparation, quality control, and clinical PET operations, including familiarity with PET radiotracers such as FDG, F-18 labeled compounds, Ga-68 labeled compounds, and other investigational radiopharmaceuticals. ● Experience with FDA-regulated pharmaceutical or radiopharmaceutical manufacturing environments. ● Knowledge of GMP and cGMP principles and applicable USP requirements. ● Demonstrated experience with regulatory documentation, quality systems, and inspection preparation. ● Experience supporting FDA inspections or regulatory submissions involving radiopharmaceuticals, and experience developing SOPs, batch records, specifications, and quality-management systems. ● Familiarity with radiation safety principles and radioactive-material handling. ● Experience with automated radiochemistry synthesis equipment and radiopharmaceutical production systems. ● Strong written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work effectively across clinical, scientific, technical, and regulatory teams. ● Demonstrated ability to interpret and implement federal and state regulations governing radioactive materials and radiopharmaceuticals. Preferred Qualifications ● Residency or fellowship training in nuclear pharmacy, radiopharmacy, or a related discipline. ● Board certification in Nuclear Pharmacy (BCNP). ● Experience with IND-directed radiopharmaceutical manufacturing. ● Experience with aseptic processing and sterile drug manufacturing, process validation, analytical method validation, equipment qualification, and computerized systems validation. ● Experience with GE FX, FASTlab, or comparable automated radiochemistry systems. Key Attributes The successful candidate will be detail-oriented, collaborative, an excellent communicator, and solutions-oriented. The role contributes to the expertise and discipline of a team ensuring that Columbia's expanding PET and radiopharmaceutical activities meet the highest standards of patient safety, regulatory compliance, and scientific rigor."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193515": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 Position Summary The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will contribute to research investigating the effects of electrical spinal cord stimulation on motor and autonomic control pathways. The fellow will conduct and support spinal cord stimulation mapping studies in rodent models and analyze descending neural connections to advance understanding of how motor and autonomic systems interact within the spinal cord. The fellow will also contribute to broader research activities within Dr. Carmel’s laboratory. Responsibilities will include conducting experimental procedures, collecting and analyzing research data, interpreting findings, contributing to scientific discussions, and supporting related investigations aligned with the laboratory’s research objectives. Key Responsibilities Conduct spinal cord stimulation mapping studies using established rodent research models. Investigate the effects of spinal cord stimulation on motor and autonomic control pathways. Perform and support experimental procedures in accordance with approved research protocols. Analyze descending neural connections and their interactions within the spinal cord. Collect, organize, and analyze experimental data using appropriate research methods. Interpret research findings and contribute to the development of scientific conclusions. Maintain accurate and comprehensive experimental records and research documentation. Participate in laboratory meetings, research discussions, and collaborative scientific activities. Contribute to manuscripts, presentations, reports, and other scientific communications as appropriate. Support additional research projects and experimental activities within Dr. Carmel’s research program. Collaborate with other members of the laboratory and research team to advance the overall goals of the research program. M.D. or Ph.D."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193541": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Japanese Language",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of East Asian Language and Culture Location: 01 The Japanese Language Program (JLP) in the Department of East Asian Languages and Cultures at Columbia University invites applications for a position at the rank of Lecturer in Japanese beginning July 1, 2027. The position is full-time with multi-year renewal contingent on successful review. Primary responsibilities include teaching two to three courses in Japanese language each semester in consultation with the JLP Director and EALAC department administration. Administrative duties include curriculum development (both individual and collaborative) and assisting with the design and execution of program events.  MA degree required, PhD or EdD preferred. High value is placed on ability to work closely with colleagues in the program. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Minimum Degree Required: MA degree in hand by the time of appointment, with specialization in Japanese linguistics, Japanese language pedagogy, second language acquisition, applied linguistics, or a related field. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must have professional-level proficiency in Japanese, excellent command of English, and experience teaching at the college-level in the United States. Preferred Qualifications: A doctoral degree is preferred. Experience teaching Japanese at various levels, demonstrated leadership in collaborative projects and curriculum innovation, and experience with instructional technology are also desirable. All applications must be submitted through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting system (ASR). Applicants should upload the following required materials: cover letter, CV, brief statement of teaching philosophy, teaching portfolios (including course descriptions and sample syllabi), samples of student teaching evaluations, contact information for three professional references, and a sample teaching video (please provide a link to a sample lesson or lecture hosted on YouTube, Google Drive, or a similar platform). Professional references will not be contacted without prior notification to the applicant."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193548": {
      "title": "Instructor in Dermatology - Physician/Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Dermatology Location: 02 Position Summary The Department of Dermatology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites applications for a full-time Instructor in Dermatology with a primary focus on investigator-initiated research. This non-tenured junior faculty position is designed for a board-eligible or board-certified dermatologist pursuing an academic career in dermatology with the goal of developing a nationally renowned, externally funded research program in fields relevant to cutaneous biology and skin health and disease. In addition to the primary research activities, the Instructor will maintain an independent dermatology practice and participate in medical education through clinical supervision for outpatient clinic and inpatient dermatology consults, plus didactic lectures as appropriate. The instructor will receive individualized mentoring from Columbia Dermatology physician-scientist and research faculty for development of an independent, investigator-initiated scientific program and academic plan, including grant and manuscript review. About Columbia Dermatology Columbia Dermatology has over a century-long tradition of excellence in dermatologic research, education, and patient care. Our faculty have pioneered landmark discoveries including: The use of corticosteroids for autoimmune skin diseases Development of the antifungal agent nystatin Innovations in photopheresis and Janus kinase inhibitors Cell and gene therapies in dermatology Advances in precision medicine, tissue engineering, and diagnostics The department is internationally recognized for its contributions to skin cancer, autoimmunity, bioengineering, wound healing, translational science, and more. Responsibilities Develop an independent, externally funded research program in basic, translational, or clinical dermatology Collaborate across CUIMC and Columbia University to foster innovative interdisciplinary research Maintain an independent faculty dermatology practice within ColumbiaDoctors Precept in Dermatology outpatient resident teaching clinics and on-call inpatient consults Participate in academic activities, including Grand Rounds, Dermatology research-in-progress meetings, and visiting lecture series as appropriate MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent medical degree; Demonstrated excellence in research and scholarship Board eligibility or board certification in dermatology; Eligibility for New York State medical licensure Eligibility for appointment to the faculty of Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons; Interested applicants should submit: A cover letter outlining research interests and career goals Curriculum vitae At least one letter of support for the candidate’s research qualifications and goals from a prior research faculty mentor"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193560": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Center for Infection and Immunity Location: 02 The Center for Infection and Immunity (CII) – one of the world’s most advanced academic centers for research in infectious disease surveillance, diagnosis and discovery is seeking an Associate Research Scientist with expertise in molecular microbiology including methods for building and validating polymerase chain reaction and sequencing assays. S/he will be responsible for optimizing capture sequencing methods for detection of bacteria, fungi, viruses and parasites in environmental and clinical samples using both the Illumina and ONT platforms and completing applications for regulatory approval under the direction of the Center director/ The candidate will perform other related duties as assigned. Doctorate or terminal professional. Requires a PhD in virology/microbiology/biotechnology or a related field with more than 5 years' experience in laboratory-based research in molecular biology and virology. Extensive experience with all aspects of Next Generation Serology and sequencing required, including data analysis. Salary Range - Associate Research Scientist:  $100,000 - $125,000 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training.  The above hiring range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193587": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Mathematics Location: 01 The Department of Mathematics invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, to begin July 1, 2027. Candidates from mathematical physics are especially encouraged to apply. All candidates should have an outstanding record in research and be committed to excellence in teaching. Duties include research, service, and teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. PhD is required at the time of appointment. Candidates must have excellent scholarship and promise for future development. Candidates for assistant professor should have completed successful graduate training and are beginning a career of independent professional accomplishment. Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV through the Columbia Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) website. Applicants must also apply at Mathjobs for the application to be considered complete. In Mathjobs, applicants must submit a cover letter, teaching and research statements, a CV, list of publications, at least three letters of recommendation, plus one additional teaching recommendation letter. Review of applications will begin November 16, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193592": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Mathematics Location: 01 The Department of Mathematics invites applications for a senior faculty position in Complex Geometry and Partial Differential Equations at the rank of Asscociate Professor or Professor. All candidates should have an outstanding record in research and be committed to excellence in teaching. Duties include research, service, and teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate level. PhD is required at the time of appointment. Candidates for all ranks must have excellent scholarship and promise for future development. Candidates for a tenured position should also have strong publishing and teaching records. Candidates for professor should be widely recognized for their distinction and professional accomplishment. Candidates for associate professor should possess a demonstrated record of professional achievement and show great promise of attaining distinction in their fields of specialization. Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV through the Columbia Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) website. Applicants must also apply at Mathjobs for the application to be considered complete. Following materials are required (to be provided on the Mathjobs site). • Curriculum Vitae • Research statement • Publication list Review of applications will begin November 16, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193604": {
      "title": "Joseph Fels Ritt Assistant Professorships",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Mathematics Location: 01 Applications are invited for Joseph F. Ritt Assistant Professorships in the Department of Mathematics at Columbia University. These are rotating positions at the junior level with one year appointments, normally renewable for three more years. Teaching load is two courses one semester and one course the other semester. Candidates must have excellent scholarship and show promise for future development; must demonstrate the potential to develop strong publishing and teaching records; and must have the potential to do pioneering research and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. PhD is required at the time of appointment. Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV through Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ARS) system. Applicants must also apply at Mathjobs for the application to be considered complete. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193680": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Systems Biology Location: 02 The Wang Lab in the Department of Systems Biology at Columbia Univeristy Irving Medical Center is looking for a full-time Associate Research Scientist to join the team. The Associate Research Scientist will conduct research in synthetic biology and microbiome engineering, with a focus on engineering gut microbes and microbial communities for therapeutic applications. The position will involve developing and applying genetic and molecular tools to program bacteria to detect disease-associated signals, deliver therapeutic molecules, and modify microbial activities and host–microbe interactions in the gastrointestinal tract. Research will explore applications in inflammatory bowel disease, metabolic disorders, and other conditions influenced by the gut microbiome. The researcher will design and execute experiments, analyze and interpret data, develop new methods and technologies, prepare manuscripts and research presentations, and contribute to collaborative projects within the laboratory. Research activities may include microbial genome editing, synthetic gene circuit design, direct engineering of resident gut bacteria, and evaluation of engineered microbial functions in laboratory and preclinical models. The position will also involve investigating the stability, specificity, and safety of engineered microbes and their ability to function within complex gut communities, with the goal of developing new approaches to prevent and treat disease. Ph.D. degree required. Minimum of three years experience preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193681": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Medicine at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Digestive and Liver Diseases Location: 02 The Department of Medicine, Division of Digestive and Liver Diseases at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons is seeking applications for a full time clinical faculty position.  An Assistant or Associate Professor level, academic transplant hepatologist is wanted to work in the Center for Liver Disease & Transplantation (CLDT), part of the Abdominal Organ Transplant Division within the Department of Surgery. Responsibilities: The clinical faculty is responsible for comprehensive care of patients with hepatobiliary disorders, including pre- and post-transplant care, on-call coverage of Liver service, participation in the education of fellows, residents and medical students; participation in outreach, participation in clinical and/or basic science research, participation in compliance and quality care programs of the University and of the Departments of Medicine and Surgery. M D degree or equivaletmt from an approved LCME medical school. Completed an ACGME approved fellow in gastroenterology and transplant hepatology. Board eligible/certified in gastroenterology and transplant hepatology. Meet UNOS qualifications as a transplant physician. Eligible to be licensed to practice in the States of New York, New Jersey and Connecticut."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193683": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Systems Biology Location: 02 Yufeng Shen lab ( http://www.columbia.edu/~ys2411/ ) in the Department of Systems Biology at Columbia University is seeking a full-time bioinformatics analyst for a Staff Associate I position. The Staff Associate I will provide support in large-scale genomic data analysis and computational tools for a multi-disciplinary research team of computational and clinical scientists. Responsibilities include: Provide support for analyzing genome sequencing data in genetic studies of human diseases and conditions; Maintain and optimize foundational bioinformatics pipelines and workflows for genomics analysis, especially annotation for coding and non-coding variants; Maintain and optimize quality control procedures; Manage genomic data sets. Responsibilities: a. Functional Knowledge - 30% Knowledge of Genome annotation and functional and genetic impacts of noncoding variants and genome language models. b. Problem Solving - 50% Utilizes analytical skills to solve genome benchmarking problems for genetic analysis and possible genetic data solutions based on guidleines, protocols, and procedures. c. Decision Making/Autonomy - 10% Works within guidelines to carry out assigned duties in the design of apparatus, the adaptation of relevant technology to the needs of a project, the conduct of specific computational and statistical experiments, and the analysis of data. d. Technical Expertise - 5% Ability to operate and maintain equiptment, computers, software programs such as python and research tools in support of the project. e. Communication - 5% Shares information and confirmation of data. Bachelor's degree in computational biology or a similar major required. Four years experience in projects with data science or machine learning as a core component preferred"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193750": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Surgery Location: 02 The Department of Surgery at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join a multidisciplinary research program focused on robotic liver surgery, robotic transplantation, and surgical innovation. The fellow will participate in clinical research, outcomes studies, educational scholarship, and the development of multimedia educational resources that advance robotic hepatobiliary and transplant surgery. A major objective of the fellowship is the creation of a comprehensive web-based multimedia educational platform featuring operative videos, animations, technical guides, lectures, and interactive learning modules for surgeons, trainees, and patients. Responsibilities: Design and conduct clinical and translational research projects in robotic liver surgery, living donor liver transplantation, and abdominal transplantation. Develop original research projects from study conception and IRB submission through manuscript publication. Analyze institutional and national datasets and present findings at regional, national, and international meetings. Collaborate with surgeons, trainees, engineers, videographers, and software developers to create a multimedia educational website dedicated to robotic liver surgery. Curate and edit high-quality operative videos, surgical illustrations, animations, and educational content. Assist in developing surgical technique guides, podcasts, webinars, and online learning modules. Support prospective clinical databases and quality improvement initiatives. Participate in weekly research and mentorship meetings. Contribute to grant writing and manuscript preparation. Mentor medical students and residents participating in research projects. Develop innovative ideas that advance robotic surgery education and academic scholarship. Apply for grants whenever appropriate mechanisms become available. MD with completion of at least 3 years of a general surgery residency program or completion of internal medicine/pediatric residency. Previous presentations and/or publications of output from research projects. Interest in hepatobiliary surgery, abdominal transplantation, robotic surgery, surgical education, or medical innovation. Experience with clinical research, scientific writing, and statistical analysis. Experience with video editing, website development, graphic design, multimedia production, or AI-assisted educational content is desirable but not required. Strong communication, organizational, and collaborative skills."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193777": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Staff Associate II is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center is one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers. The Department of Pediatrics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is the top in comparison with other pediatrics department in the NY region in NIH research funding. This position is under the supervision of the Principal Investigator, and may include but not limited to conducting research, managing data, operating equipment, pulling samples, or other functions as directed by the PI. The Staff Associate applies knowledge of the research subject and scientific methods of analysis to project work and utilizes analytical skills to solve moderately complex problems based on established guidelines, protocols, and procedures. Working within guidelines, the incumbent carries out assigned duties in the design of apparatus, the adaptation of relevant technology to the needs of the project, the conduct of specific experiments, and the analysis of data. The Staff Associate operates and maintains equipment, computers, software programs, and research tools in support of the project, and shares information and confirmation of data with the Principal Investigator and other research team members. This is an individual-contributor role with potential supervisory responsibilities. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Perform routine to intermediate-level laboratory experiments in supporting of ongoing research under the guidance of PI. Execute and documents experiments using techniques including but not limited to Cell culture, Western blotting, PCR/qPCR, Flow cytometry, ELISA, immunofluorescence, immunohistochemistry, cell viability, lipid extraction. Utilizes analytical skills to solve moderately complex problems based on guidelines, protocols, and procedures. Work within guidelines to carry out assigned duties in the design of apparatus, the adaptation of relevant technology to the needs of the project, the conduct of specific experiments, and the analysis of data. Ability to operate and maintain equipment, computers, software programs, and research tools in support of the project. Shares information and confirmation of data. Support animal experiments related to ongoing research in accordance with approved protocols: Animal colony support and coordination with vivarium staff. Experimental preparation and scheduling. Sample collection/processing. Accurate recordkeeping for animal studies and experimental endpoints. Adhere to all IACUC, biosafety, and institutional policies; complete required training prior to performing independent work. Laboratory maintenance including inventory, purchasing of medical, surgical, and analytical supplies, equipment components. .. Must have a Bachelor’s degree in specific research field and 4 years of experience. Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines. The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions. The ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. Master’s Degree Preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193811": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Biostatistics Location: 02 The Department of Biostatistics at the Mailman School of Public Health, Columbia University, is seeking a full-time Associate Research Scientist. The Associate Research Scientist will provide scientific and technical leadership for AI-enabled health data science research and for the Dean's AI Research Development Program. Responsibilities and Duties : Support research projects focused on building a secure Columbia Brain Health Databank(CBDB) research sandbox and developing an agentic AI system on top of CBDB for Alzheimer's disease and broader data science research. Support the scientific and technical operations of the Dean's AI Research Development Program, including hands-on AI solution development for two funded project teams. Work with faculty investigators and research staff to translate scientific questions into reproducible AI workflows, prototypes, evaluation plans, and research outputs, while promoting rigorous, privacy-aware, and responsible use of clinical and research data. Minimum Educational Requirements : Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in data science, computer science, statistics/biostatistics, biomedical informatics, engineering, or a closely related quantitative field Professional Work Experience : 5 years recommended Demonstrated research experience in AI/machine learning, health data science, computational methods, or related areas. Experience developing research software or AI-enabled analytic workflows and working with complex biomedical or clinical data in multidisciplinary research teams. Experience with EHR data, Alzheimer's disease/dementia research, large language models or agentic AI, and secure computing environments is preferred Skills : Strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience with machine learning and modern AI/LLM methods, including tool-using or agentic workflows; familiarity with SQL/databases, APIs, version control, and reproducible research practices; ability to work in secure data environments; strong scientific communication, documentation, collaboration, and problem-solving skills."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193902": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Assistant/Associate/Professor of Ophthalmology at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Ophthalmology Location: 02 The Department of Ophthalmology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center and the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons invites applications for a full-time vitreoretinal surgeon to join the Retina Division at the Edward S. Harkness Eye Institute. This is an opportunity to join a leading academic ophthalmology program with a longstanding tradition of excellence in patient care, education, discovery, and innovation. We seek candidates who combine clinical and surgical excellence with a strong commitment to academic medicine. The position includes participation in the clinical and academic programs of the Department, as well as teaching and mentorship of residents, fellows, and medical students. Candidates with an established record of scholarship, clinical innovation, collaborative research, or excellence in education are particularly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will receive a full-time faculty appointment in the Department of Ophthalmology at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons, with clinical privileges at NewYork Presbyterian Hospital. Academic rank and base compensation will be commensurate with qualifications, experience, and academic accomplishments. Medical Degree Candidates must hold or be eligible for a license to practice medicine in New York State, be board certified or board eligible by the American Board of Ophthalmology, and have fellowship training in vitreoretinal diseases and surgery. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, and three professional references to: George A. Cioffi, MD Chair, Department of Ophthalmology Edward S. Harkness Professor of Ophthalmology Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons gac2126@cumc.columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193838": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Interdisciplinary: Columbia Astrophysics Lab Location: 05 The Columbia University Astrophysics Laboratory (CAL) invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist position in laboratory astrophysics. The successful candidate will join the group of Dr. Daniel Wolf Savin and will be involved in a broad research program in experimental studies of astrochemistry. In particular, the candidate will focus on merged-fast-beams studies of deuterating reactions that form key molecules used to probe the properties of prestellar cores and protoplanetary disks. The successful candidate will be expected to be actively involved in defining achievable objectives for the project, executing the project plan and modifying it as necessary, demonstrating an awareness of the relevant work of others in the field and establishing the context for the project, organizing project tasks efficiently, keeping detailed records, meeting deadlines, obtaining meaningful results, analyzing data, formulating supportable conclusions, clearly reporting their findings (both orally and in writing), accepting constructive criticism, and working independently as well as cooperatively and professionally with our team and with others. The successful candidate will be appointed at Columbia University’s Nevis Laboratories in Irvington, NY. The expected start date is on or around 01 July 2027. The initial appointment is for one year, with possibility of renewal, contingent upon work eligibility and the usual standards for satisfactory performance and the availability of funding. Minimum Qualifications : The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. or the equivalent degree in Physics, Chemistry, Astrophysics, or a related field, with an emphasis in molecular physics. Preferred Qualifications : Desired laboratory skills include experience with ion sources, fast ion beams, ion optics, ultra-high vacuum techniques, infrared lasers, apparatus design and construction, single particle detectors and associated electronics, analog and digital signal processing, data reduction, and statistical and systematic error propagation.  Desired computer skills include programming, multidimensional data analysis, LabView, 3D CAD, SIMION, Mathematica, or similar, Linux OS, and Windows OS. The successful candidate will have a strong background in some of the areas listed above, a proven research ability, and evidence of future research potential. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a curriculum vita (including a list of publications), a statement of past research (three-page limit), and contact information for at least two references, who will be asked to provide a confidential letter of recommendation. Applications will be considered complete only after all requested materials have been received. Review of completed applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193853": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Interdisciplinary: Columbia Astrophysics Lab Location: 01 The Theoretical High-Energy Astrophysics (THEA) group in the Columbia Astrophysics Laboratory invites applications for the THEA Postdoctoral Fellowship. The initial appointment in the rank of a Postdoctoral Research Scientist will be for one year, with anticipated renewal for up to three years, contingent upon work eligibility and the usual standards for satisfactory performance and the availability of funding. Successful candidates will conduct independent and collaborative research under the guidance of Andrei Beloborodov, Luca Comisso, Yuri Levin, Brian Metzger and Lorenzo Sironi. More information about THEA group can be found at https://thea.astro.columbia.edu . THEA Fellows have access to high-performance computational facilities and travel funds. The expected start date is on or around September 1, 2027. The salary range for this position is $73,000-$83,000. Minimum Qualifications: The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Astronomy, Physics, or a related discipline by the date of the appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae with the list of publications, a research statement that does not exceed 3 pages, and contact information for three references, who will be asked to provide a confidential letter of recommendation. Applications will be considered complete only after all requested materials have been received. All application materials should be submitted by November 6, 2026. Late applications may also be considered. THEA applicants are also strongly encouraged to apply for the Ernest Kempton Adams Fellowship at Columbia University. Further information on the EKA Fellowship can be found at https://apply.interfolio.com/192942 . Candidates interested in the physics of neutron stars and black holes should also apply to the positions offered by the Simons Collaboration on Extreme Electrodynamics of Compact Sources (SCEECS), https://aas.org/jobregister/ad/dce72175 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194066": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist - Magnetically Confined Plasma Research",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics Location: 01 The Department of Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics (APAM) at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for Associate Research Scientist positions in the area of magnetically confined plasma research. There are up to 3 positions under consideration. The successful candidate will advance research in magnetically confined plasmas and support the broader goals of the Columbia Fusion Research Center. In the tokamak area, the candidate may conduct research on experimental tokamak facilities in the public sector. Examples include the DIII-D tokamak located at General Atomics or the National Spherical Torus Experiment Upgrade located at Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory, or tokamaks located abroad. In the stellarator area, the candidate may conduct research in coil optimization, energetic particle physics, and other related activities. Activities may also be conducted in support of industrially sponsored research contracts originating from a wide array of sponsors, including concepts beyond tokamaks and stellarators. The successful candidate will also be expected to assist in the mentorship of more junior scientists, post-doctoral researchers, and students. In addition, the candidate is expected to conduct both supporting and independent research towards the execution of the Columbia Fusion Research Center research program, likely supporting multiple research awards. Candidates for professional officer of research positions are expected to have established their ability to conduct original, independent scientist research.  To the rank of an associate research scientist, candidate’s qualifications and contributions to their fields of research must be equivalent to those of an assistant professor.   All candidates for all officer of research positions are expected to be able to work well in a team and to communicate effectively the results of their research or research activities both orally and in writing. The level of appointment is commensurate with experience.  The earliest position is expected to begin on approximately Jan 1 st 2027 or sooner if possible, with possible additional positions being filled later in 2027. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. (or equivalent professional degree) in plasma physics or a related field, and experience with different computer environments and languages especially Python, Matlab, IDL, FORTRAN and UNIX/LINX. Preferred Qualifications:  Familiarity of working with magnetically confined plasma computational modeling tools and their application to interpret experimental data. Familiarity with magnetically confined plasma experimental data and measurement methods. Demonstrated track record of mentorship of junior scientists, post-doctoral fellows, or students. Publications in top-rated journals in the field and presentations at national and international conferences Candidates should be open to relocation to the New York City area. Minimum Degree Required: Ph.D. in (specialization) or related field. Candidates for professional officer of research positions are expected to have established their ability to conduct original, independent scientist research.  To the rank of an associate research scientist, candidate’s qualifications and contributions to their fields of research must be equivalent to those of an assistant professor. All candidates for all officer of research positions are expected to be able to work well in a team and to communicate effectively the results of their research or research activities both orally and in writing. Preferred Qualifications Familiarity of working with magnetically confined plasma computational modeling tools and their application to interpret experimental or simulated data. Familiarity with magnetically confined plasma experimental data and measurement methods. Demonstrated track record of mentorship of junior scientists, post-doctoral fellows, or students. Publications in top-rated journals in the field and presentations at national and international conferences. Applicant should apply through this Interfolio posting. Applications must include: (a) curriculum vitae (b) statement of research (c) names of at least three references who may be asked to provide letters. The search will close no sooner than December 1 st 2026 and will remain open until the positions are filled. Applicants are encouraged to consult http://www.apam.columbia.edu for more information about the department, and https://fusion.columbia.edu for more information about the Columbia Fusion Research Center. Columbia University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193960": {
      "title": "Staff Associate - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 01 Columbia Business School is seeking to hire one or more Research Staff Associates for the Finance Division. The work will include quantitative and qualitative research and analysis, collection, coordination, and management of information and data, development of research materials, and work on special projects relating to the research areas within the division. This position provides an opportunity to gain experience in academic business research, with a special focus on fields such as finance, economics, statistics, and data science; and, would be ideal preparation for a Ph.D. program or other graduate study.   This is a one-year position with a possible extension to a second year. The Research Staff Associate duties involve work in one or more of the following areas of research: finance, economics, and statistics. Specific duties include: Collect, clean, and maintain computer datasets and databases. Assist with data entry and ensure data integrity. Extract and link data from multiple databases for analysis. Prepare detailed documentation. Develop models and implement program code (STATA, Python, SQL, R, SAS, Matlab, etc.). Perform statistical analysis, including regression analysis and machine learning techniques. Assist with data analysis and dissemination of findings through the preparation of reports, journal articles, presentations, websites, and other research outlets. Perform case-based research, including work with detailed primary documents. Applicants for the Staff Associate position are expected to have the following: Programming experience in STATA is required. Strong preference for those who also know Python. Additional programming experience in one or more of the following languages: R, SQL, SAS, Matlab, and/or C++ is preferred. Familiarity with statistical tools such as linear regression is required. Familiarity with text parsing and analysis (natural language processing) and/or machine learning techniques is preferred. Demonstrated exceptional written and oral communications skills needed – a writing sample is required. The candidate must be organized and able to work on multiple projects simultaneously. At least 6 months of research experience in a research role as a research assistant during the academic year or a summer internship in a research lab or governmental institution such as the NY Fed. Bachelor’s degree in Economics or in a quantitative discipline such as Engineering, Computer Science, Mathematics, Statistics, with Economics coursework (including the econometrics sequence) For questions, please email financesearch@gsb.columbia.edu Bachelor’s degree in Economics or Finance or in a quantitative discipline such as Mathematics, Statistics, Engineering with coursework in Economics and Finance."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193973": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Columbia Aging Center Location: 02 Description The Robert N. Butler Columbia Aging Center (CAC) is a multidisciplinary, university-wide Columbia University Center for research, education, and policy on aging and longevity, housed at the Columbia Mailman School of Public Health. For this position, we seek an economist or economist policy specialist who has training and experience in population aging and healthy longevity in US and global contexts, and an interest in building a career focused on the return on investment to individuals and society of system transformation to support successfully aging societies, and the potential to create a Third Demographic Dividend through these interventions. Primary Responsibilities Collaborate on or lead quantitative analysis for scientific presentations, manuscripts, and grant applications; produce tables and figures based on study results Work with the mentor, principal investigator, project director, associate research scientist, student researchers, and other collaborators to develop and execute clear, well-documented research projects and manuscripts Perform data auditing across multiple projects and assist with quality control Work with research team members and collaborators to identify appropriate variables for proposed analysis across projects Create datasets for new projects and integrate new variables with existing datasets as needed Assist or develop grant proposals, including performing literature reviews and running preliminary analysis Assist with research related administrative tasks including IRBs, MTAs, DUAs, and progress reports Take initiative to acquire new statistical and/or programming skills as needed REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: PhD or doctoral degree in economics Strong interpersonal, leadership, and verbal/written communication skills Significant research experience evidenced by peer-reviewed publications Experience with SAS, STATA, and/or R programming software PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience or willingness to gain expertise in various programming languages Experience in measurement and analysis of biological and social variables in the study of health and aging as well as economic methods and hypothesis development and testing"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193975": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Psychology Location: 01 The Columbia University Department of Psychology invites applications for an Assistant Professor position to begin on July 1, 2027.  We seek applications from individuals studying human-centered Cognitive Science and/or Cognitive Neuroscience that complement current areas of expertise within the Department at the intersection of mind, behavior, computation, and artificial intelligence. Examples of research areas of special interest include perception, attention, learning and memory, cognitive control, language, curiosity, and higher-level cognition. Individuals with expertise in methods such as behavioral experiments, neuroimaging, computational approaches, patient studies, psychophysiology and/or individual differences will all be considered. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated record of research productivity and teaching commensurate to the level of the faculty position. All candidates must have a strong publication record and have demonstrated the potential to establish a vigorous individual research program and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. Applicants for the positions must possess a doctoral degree in Psychology or a related field and have demonstrated an ability for pioneering research as well as the promise of excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Review of applications will begin October 1 st and will continue until the position is filled. All candidates must have a strong publication record and have demonstrated the potential to establish a vigorous individual research program and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. Candidates also must have the potential for future development as a scientist, scholar and teacher. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR), which can be accessed via Interfolio. Candidates should submit their vitae, statements of research and teaching interests, copies of relevant papers, and arrange for three letters of reference. Please note that all applications must be submitted through the online application site, where you can also find further information about this position. For more information about the Psychology Department please visit:  http://psychology.columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193979": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Psychology Location: 01 The Department of Psychology at Columbia University invites applications for a faculty position at the tenure-track assistant professor level in social psychology to begin July 1, 2027. We seek applications from individuals conducting social psychological research in areas that complement and build on the Department’s current areas of expertise, including social cognition, affect, person perception, relationships, intergroup relations, and inequality and social justice. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated record of research productivity and teaching commensurate with the position level. Individuals with multi-method expertise, including computational approaches to the study of social behavior, psychophysiology, use of big data, and other novel methods, are especially encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated record of research productivity and teaching commensurate to the level of the faculty position. All candidates must have a strong publication record and have demonstrated the potential to establish a vigorous individual research program and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. Applicants for the positions must possess a doctoral degree in Psychology or a related field and have demonstrated an ability for pioneering research as well as the promise of excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Review of applications will begin October 1 st and will continue until the position is filled. PhD All candidates must have a strong publication record and have demonstrated the potential to establish a vigorous individual research program and to teach effectively using modern tools of instruction. Applicants for the position must possess a doctoral degree in Psychology or a related field and have demonstrated an ability for pioneering research as well as the promise of excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR), which can be accessed via Interfolio. Candidates should submit their vitae, statements of research and teaching interests, copies of relevant papers, and arrange for three letters of reference. Please note that all applications must be submitted through the online application site, where you can also find further information about this position. For more information about the Psychology Department please visit:  http://psychology.columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193984": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Psychology Location: 01 The Columbia University Department of Psychology invites applications for an Associate Professor or Full Professor whose research focuses on human mental health, broadly construed.  It is anticipated that this hire will be followed in subsequent years by two additional Assistant Professor hires (pending future budgetary approval) whose research also concerns mental health. For this Associate/Full faculty position, we seek applicants with an established track record of impactful research addressing core aspects of human mental health using conceptual approaches, methods, and techniques that complement current areas of expertise within the Department.  We welcome a broad range of research foci, from characterizing resilient vs. dysfunctional behavior and experience across the lifespan to designing interventions at the individual or community level. Multimethod approaches may be rooted in any of a variety of fields, including (but not limited to) clinical, affective, social, community, health, developmental and cognitive (neuro)science/psychology.  Diverse types of methodological expertise are welcome, including research with normative and/or clinical populations using behavioral, psychophysiological, neuroendocrine, neuroimaging, computational and/or AI approaches.  The successful candidate will be expected to help anchor and foster the growth of mental health research as a core area within the Department of Psychology. Applicants for the position must possess a doctoral degree in Psychology or a related field and have demonstrated the ability to conduct pioneering research, as well as exhibit excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Review of applications will begin on November 1st and will continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. PhD Candidate must have demonstrated ability to conduct pioneering research, and exhibit excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR), which can be accessed via Interfolio. Candidates should submit their vitae, statements of research and teaching interests, copies of relevant papers, and arrange for three letters of reference. Please note that all applications must be submitted through the online application site, where you can also find further information about this position. For more information about the Psychology Department please visit:  http://psychology.columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193989": {
      "title": "Joan Konner Visiting Professor of Journalism  (Open Rank: Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Journalism School Location: 01 The Graduate School of Journalism at Columbia University invites applications for a full-time, visiting professor (rank to be determined) to be appointed for two years. We are seeking a leading practitioner and instructor in journalism who can help students understand fast-paced news environments and the changing demands of the profession. The visitor is expected to teach core courses, such as Reporting and Image and Sound, in which students produce work often focusing on municipal government, courts, education, diverse communities and similar lines of reporting near our New York campus. As the Joan Konner Visiting Professor of Journalism, teaching assignments may include teaching a foundational Reporting and Image and Sound Course and/or a seminar in the candidate’s specialty area, supervising masters’ projects, and advising students. The candidate may also contribute to the Master of Arts program as well as the dual degree journalism and computer science program. The ideal candidate would have a substantial publishing record as well as experience as a teacher, writer and editor. Demonstrated experience with multimedia production and storytelling is preferred. To qualify for this two-year visiting professorship the candidate must currently hold an appointment at another academic institution. A bachelor’s degree is required; an advanced degree is preferred. This two-year visiting position is expected to be appointed from July 1, 2027 through June 30, 2028, with a renewed appointment from July 1, 2028 through June 30, 2029, pending performance and administrative approval. We request that you submit a curricula vitae and a cover letter for consideration.  We also welcome examples of your best work and/or other related documentation to support your candidacy. The hiring committee will continuously review applications, with the expectation of conducting first round interviews by December 2026.  The search will remain open no less than thirty days after posting and until the position is filled. To qualify for this visiting professorship the candidate must currently hold an appointment at another academic institution.  A bachelor’s degree is required; advanced degree preferred. Hiring Salary Range: $100,000 - $120,000 Assistant Professor: $100,000 - 110,000 Associate Professor: $105,000 - $115,000 Professor: $110,000 - $120,000 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training. The above hiring range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. We request that you submit a curricula vitae and a cover letter for consideration.  We also welcome examples of your best work and/or other related documentation to support your candidacy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193999": {
      "title": "Newsday/David Laventhol Visiting Professor (Open Rank: Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Journalism School Location: 01 The Graduate School of Journalism at Columbia University invites applications for a full-time visiting professorship during the 2027-2028 academic year, renewable for up to one additional year. We are seeking a leading practitioner and instructor in journalism who can help students understand fast-paced news environments and the changing demands of the profession. The visitor is expected to teach core courses, such as Reporting and Image and Sound, in which students produce work often focusing on municipal government, courts, education, diverse communities and similar lines of reporting near our New York campus. As the Newsday/David Laventhol Visiting Professor, the successful candidate will demonstrate expertise in reporting on diversity in media issues. Teaching assignments may include teaching a foundational Reporting or Image and Sound course and/or a seminar in the candidate’s specialty area, supervising masters’ projects, and advising students. The candidate may also contribute to the Master of Arts program as well as the dual degree journalism and computer science program. The ideal candidate would have a substantial publishing record as well as experience as a teacher, writer and editor. Candidates with the ability to teach investigative reporting as well as demonstrated experience with multimedia production and data preferred. To qualify for this visiting professorship the candidate must currently hold an appointment at another academic institution. A bachelor’s degree is required; advanced degree preferred. This visiting professorship is expected to be appointed from July 1, 2027 through June 30, 2028, pending administrative approval, with the possibility of reappointment for one additional year. We request that you submit a curriculum vitae and a cover letter for consideration. We also welcome examples of your best work and/or other related documentation to support your candidacy. The hiring committee will continuously review applications, with the expectation of conducting first round interviews by December 2026.  The search will remain open no less than thirty days after posting and until the position is filled. To qualify for this visiting professorship the candidate must currently hold an appointment at another academic institution. A bachelor’s degree is required; advanced degree preferred. Hiring Salary Range $100,000 - $120,000 a year Assistant Professor: $100,000 - 110,000 Associate Professor: $105,000 - $115,000 Professor: $110,000 - $120,000 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training. The above hiring range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194007": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Environmental Health Science Location: 02 The Staff Associate II will assist the PI and Data Manager in managing and analyzing data and samples from planned, ongoing, and completed epidemiologic studies. Data includes both local cohort data as well as data from a national consortium.  Typical tasks include cleaning and creating databases for completed studies with appropriate documentation, creation of analytic databases, and undertaking statistical analyses of existing data for write up of reports, abstracts and manuscripts. The position involves establishing systems for tracking samples, images and other data and developing quality control systems. The position also involves independent design, initiation and conduct of statistical analyses for write-up for publication as well as planning for future studies. Responsibilities: Data management and cleaning of existing data. Working with Center investigators to develop analysis proposals using consortium data. Consulting with the consortium representatives and conducting analyses on the consortium workbench enclave. Conducting analyses for progress reports and abstracts/manuscripts. Assembling and reviewing literature from related studies, as needed. Preparing documents needed for regulatory and IRB review. Establishing databases and creating metadata for samples and images in storage. Developing tracking systems and quality control of samples. Generating new ideas for manuscripts and studies. Providing statistical analysis support to the study team. Perform other related duties and responsibilities as assigned/requested. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree required in a specific research field. Preferred Qualifications 4 Years recommended. Master's degree in quantitative field, public health preferred. Experience in management and analysis of data from epidemiologic studies. Proficiency in statistical software including R/R-Studio"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194009": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor position in Luso-Brazilian cultural studies since 1800",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Latin American and Iberian Cultures Location: 01 The Department of Latin American and Iberian Cultures at Columbia University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Luso-Brazilian cultural studies since 1800, to begin July 1, 2027. We are especially interested in scholars working in a broader Latin American and/or transoceanic perspective on: literature, visual arts, film, or music. Scholars working in Afro-Brazilian studies and/or on indigeneity and gender are particularly encouraged to apply. Ph.D. must be in hand by the appointment start date. The selectee will be expected to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels and to contribute to Columbia College's Core Curriculum. Candidates must have an outstanding research agenda and a teaching record that reflects eagerness to work closely with graduate and undergraduate students. Fluency in Portuguese is required. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Ph.D. in Luso-Brazilian cultural studies and related fields. Teaching experience in language and culture in graduate school or in current position. Our criteria involve, but are not limited to, scholarly promise and achievement, outstanding potential in teaching, a strong commitment to cultural studies, and an eagerness to work closely with graduate and undergraduate students. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: CV, cover letter, student evaluations, and a writing sample. Please also provide contact information for 3 references, who will be asked to upload letters of recommendation. For questions concerning the receipt of your application, please contact Eunice Rodríguez Ferguson (fe2114@columbia.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194033": {
      "title": "Faculty- All Ranks - Clinical: Instructor/ Assistant Professor/ Associate Professor / Professor at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics: Pediatrics - Primary Care Initiative Location: 02 Position Description Columbia Doctors Primary Care is actively hiring exceptional clinical faculty physicians to provide primary and preventative healthcare in established and opening practices across New York City and Westchester County. At Columbia Doctors, we are dedicated to a comprehensive and compassionate approach to delivering advanced personalized medical care. We are experts in diagnosing, monitoring, and promoting all aspects of our patients' health, from chronic conditions to mental health. We seek dynamic team members who share our vision of high-performing, high-quality, patient-first primary care. Centered in one of the nation's highest-ranking medical schools and hospital systems, Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons and NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital's leadership and faculty are committed to teaching and training medical students and mentoring residents. As a faculty member, you'll also have the opportunity to: ·              Be part of a vast physician care network and multispecialty referral system. ·              Collaborate with world-renowned medical professionals. ·              Join a culture that values and promotes exceptional care, research interests, and experience. ·              Advance your medical career in a supportive, compassionate, and innovative environment. Minimum Degree: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Minimum Qualifications: Must be board certified/eligible in Pediatrics and eligible to practice in the State of New York."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194048": {
      "title": "Instructor in Pediatrics (Non-Invasive Imaging) at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY/POSITION DESCRIPTION This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of an Instructor in Pediatrics in Non-Invasive Imaging is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers. The Department of Pediatrics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center is the top in comparison with other pediatrics department in the NY region in NIH research funding. Columbia University Medical Center (CUMC)/Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital of New York is now accepting applications for advanced fellowship training in non-invasive imaging in the upcoming academic year. Applicants will have completed their general pediatric cardiology fellowship training at an accredited ACGME institution prior to the start date of July 1 st will be considered. The Pediatric Non-Invasive Imaging Laboratory at Columbia University Irving Medical center is one of the largest imaging programs for children with congenital heart disease in the United States. Activities include fetal cardiology, transesophageal echocardiography, cardiac magnetic resonance and cardiac computed tomography. The instructor (Advanced fellow) in non-invasive imaging will participate in our fellowship program as a non-ACGME fellow. This structure is in place to train this talented fellow while he/she participates in inpatient rounds and outpatient care. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES · Maintain timely and complete EPIC chart notes, reports, and communication with primary care/referring physicians and other relevant subspecialty physicians. Participate in multidisciplinary clinical management initiatives with other subsections within Pediatric Cardiology. · Participate in division and department quality improvement initiatives. · Maintain varied hours on a rotated schedule with other physicians to ensure that there is always a physician on-call during non-business hours. · Attend patient rounds and complete consultation and inpatient notes. Comply with all billing, coding, documentation, and regulatory requirements. · Participate in divisional conferences, Department of Pediatrics grand rounds, morbidity and mortality conferences, and faculty development education. · Additional clinical responsibilities will include outpatient Cardiology coverage and inpatient service as well as conference preparation and on-call duties. COMMITMENT OF DIVERSITY: Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and Educator.  The University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment, and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Columbia University welcomes applications from individuals who may have had nontraditional career paths, or who may have taken time off for family reasons (e.g. children, caring for disabled or elderly family), or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of academia (e.g., in professional or industry service).  The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and is committed to supporting the work-life balance of its faculty.  We are interested in candidates who have a record of success advising and mentoring individuals from groups underrepresented in higher education and is particularly interested in candidates who have research interests in subjects that will contribute to the understanding of diversity and equal opportunity. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS: · Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License · Must be Board certification in Pediatrics. · Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; · The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. COMPENSATION: Compensation arrangements are competitive and commensurate with both experience and achievement."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194070": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences (DEES) of Columbia University (CU) seeks applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Physical Oceanography with AI . Research in the Department occurs at the Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory (LDEO), which offers a rich, collaborative geoscience community with laboratory and graduate teaching space on its campus in Palisades, New York. Most teaching will take place on the CU campus in New York City.  We welcome applications from all qualified candidates who are committed to the mentoring and success of students and early-career scientists from all backgrounds. We seek candidates who address fundamental questions in the physics of the global ocean and the ocean’s role in the Earth system and who do so with a substantial and sophisticated use of artificial intelligence (AI). Specific areas of interest include, but are not limited to, ocean circulation; transport, storage, and uptake of heat and carbon; air-sea interactions; mesoscale and submesoscale processes; and the ocean's role in climate variability, change, and predictability. Potential AI areas include learning dynamics from sparse observations; hybrid physics-AI models; machine-learned parameterizations; emulation; data assimilation; and quantification of uncertainty. We also welcome the development of novel AI methods, provided they are paired with state-of-the-art research in physical oceanography. We seek a candidate who will run a high-impact research program focused on problems of global significance, complementing existing faculty interests in DEES and LDEO. The candidate must also have strong potential for effective teaching and mentoring in both physical oceanography and AI for Earth science at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The ideal candidate will engage fully in the methodological innovation and collaborative ocean and Earth science within the department and the broad community of scientists at LDEO and across CU, including the Data Science Institute and the Learning the Earth with AI and Physics (LEAP) Science and Technology Center. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication program and teach in the undergraduate and graduate programs in ways that complement the DEES curriculum. Review of applications will begin October 26, 2026. Minimum Degree Requirement: PhD All candidates must demonstrate excellent scholarship and significant promise for continued growth. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, a statement of research, and a teaching and mentoring statement that includes a vision for how the candidate can contribute to the department’s curriculum through existing and/or new courses, as well as their approach to mentoring. Applicants should also provide the names of 3 individuals who may be contacted as references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194082": {
      "title": "Faculty- All Ranks - Clinical: Instructor/ Assistant Professor/ Associate Professor / Professor at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Primary Care Initiative Location: 02 Position Description Columbia Doctors Primary Care is actively hiring exceptional clinical faculty physicians to provide primary and preventative healthcare in established and opening practices across New York City and Westchester County. At Columbia Doctors, we are dedicated to a comprehensive and compassionate approach to delivering advanced personalized medical care. We are experts in diagnosing, monitoring, and promoting all aspects of our patients' health, from chronic conditions to mental health. We seek dynamic team members who share our vision of high-performing, high-quality, patient-first primary care. Centered in one of the nation's highest-ranking medical schools and hospital systems, Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons and NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital's leadership and faculty are committed to teaching and training medical students and mentoring residents. As a faculty member, you'll also have the opportunity to: ·                Be part of a vast physician care network and multispecialty referral system. ·                Collaborate with world-renowned medical professionals. ·                Join a culture that values and promotes exceptional care, research interests, and experience. ·                Advance your medical career in a supportive, compassionate, and innovative environment. Minimum Degree: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Minimum Qualifications: Must be board certified/eligible in Internal Medicine/Family Medicine and eligible to practice in the State of New York."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194121": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of International and Public Affairs Location: 01 The Staff Associate I will work with SIPA Professor Robert Metcalfe. The incumbent will support research and assist on academic projects and papers in the area of applied microeconomics, including projects related to the impacts of AI on economic outcomes. The Staff Associate will follow protocols and procedures set by Professor Metcalfe, utilizing analytical skills to solve moderately complex problems. They will take initiative in independent judgment based on said protocols and procedures set by Prof. Metcalfe to support administrative items as requested, including processing of financial requests and reviewing monthly and year-end financial reports. The Staff Associate will monitor and respond to emails. Bachelor’s degree in Economics. Knowledge in the areas of economic theory, econometric analysis, data science, AI and coding."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194132": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Statistics Location: 01 The Department of Statistics invites applications for a Staff Associate I who will conduct research detecting spatially variable genes which are correlated with tension phenotypes. Applied to spatial transcriptomics datasets, this analysis will help understand the molecular basis of aging phenotypes in patients with Werner syndrome in the lab of Prof Bianca Dumitrascu. Responsibilities will include: --(40%): Build and run reproducible Python/R pipelines for spatial transcriptomics data on the lab’s computing cluster, including quality control, normalization, cell segmentation, and detection of spatially variable genes --(25%): Implement, adapt, and benchmark statistical methods that relate spatial gene expression to tissue tension phenotypes -- (15%): Acquire and apply working knowledge of spatial genomics, statistical methods, and the biology of aging and Werner syndrome -- (10%): Prepare figures, written summaries, and documentation; present results at lab meetings; contribute to methods sections of manuscripts --(10%): Organize and maintain datasets, code repositories, and computing environments Bachelor's degree in statistics, computer science, mathematics, computational biology, or a related quantitative field. Python and/or R programming; statistics; data visualization; git and command line. Prior research experience, including undergraduate research, preferred. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: Curriculum vitae."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194142": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Taub Institute Location: 02 The Taub Institute for Research on Alzheimer's Disease and the Aging Brian is seeking a talented and highly motivated scientist with strong expertise in biochemistry, molecular and cellular biology for the position of an Associate Research Scientist. The position will involve participation in NIH-funded projects aimed at understanding the mechanisms of neurodegenerative and neurodevelopmental diseases. The projects employ multidisciplinary approaches in the areas of genomics, RNA/protein biochemistry and neuroscience using cellular and animal model systems. We are primarily interested in candidates with wet-lab experience in animal models and microglia primary cultures. The core focus areas are innate immunity, tau and amyloid pathology, single- cell/single-nucleus transcriptomics, proteomics. The candidate in this position will have to utilize various in vivo and in vitro techniques to investigate the relationship between synaptic tau, proteolysis in immune cells and pathways related to memory and plasticity. The applicant will carry out experimental work on research projects established in liaison with the PI and to be performed either independently or in collaboration with postdoctoral research scientists and graduate students. PhD or MD degree, plus 2 to 3 years of postdoctoral research experience Submit application on-line and provide CV"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194160": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Professional Practice - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 06 The Finance Division at Columbia Business School welcomes applications for one or more non-tenure track positions at the assistant, associate, or full professor of professional practice rank, commensurate with experience and qualifications. Candidates are expected to contribute to the School’s mission through excellence in teaching, curriculum development, and service. Faculty in this role draw upon substantial professional experience to deliver high-quality instruction in degree programs, develop and update course materials, and contribute to the intellectual and pedagogical life of the School. In addition, candidates are expected to engage in service activities that support the Division, the School, and the broader professional community, and to contribute to institution-building efforts as appropriate. Applicants must have an MBA, PhD/DBA preferred, and should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and to institution-building contributions that advance the mission of the School. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrate strong potential for, effective teaching, curriculum development, and service. In addition, candidates are expected to draw upon substantial professional experience to inform their teaching and contributions to the academic community. Assistant professors of professional practice candidates should have at least three years of business or other applicable experience. Associate professors of professional practice candidates should have at least eight years of business or other applicable experience. Professors of professional practice should have at least 12 years of business or other applicable experience. For questions, please email financesearch@gsb.columbia.edu Applicants must have an MBA, PhD/DBA preferred, and should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and to institution-building contributions that advance the mission of the School. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrate strong potential for, effective teaching, curriculum development, and service. In addition, candidates are expected to draw upon substantial professional experience to inform their teaching and contributions to the academic community. Assistant professors of professional practice candidates should have at least three years of business or other applicable experience. Associate professors of professional practice candidates should have at least eight years of business or other applicable experience. Professors of professional practice should have at least 12 years of business or other applicable experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194162": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Discipline - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 06 The Finance Division at Columbia Business School welcomes applications for one or more non-tenure track positions at the lecturer or senior lecturer in discipline rank, commensurate with experience and qualifications. Candidates are expected to contribute to the School’s mission through excellence in teaching, curriculum development, and service. Faculty in this role are responsible for delivering high-quality instruction in degree programs, developing and updating course materials, and contributing to the intellectual and pedagogical life of the School. In addition, candidates are expected to engage in service activities that support the Division, the School, and the broader professional community, and to contribute to institution-building efforts as appropriate. Applicants must have a PhD/DBA in finance or a related field and should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and to institution-building contributions that advance the mission of the School. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrate strong potential for, effective teaching, curriculum development, and service. Lecturer in discipline candidates should have at least two years of teaching or other applicable experience. Senior lecturer in discipline candidates should have at least eight years of teaching or other applicable experience. For questions, please email financesearch@gsb.columbia.edu Applicants must have a PhD/DBA in finance or a related field and should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and to institution-building contributions that advance the mission of the School. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrate strong potential for, effective teaching, curriculum development, and service. Lecturer in discipline candidates should have at least two years of teaching or other applicable experience. Senior lecturer in discipline candidates should have at least eight years of teaching or other applicable experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194179": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 06 The Graduate School of Business at Columbia University is seeking to hire one or more tenure-track faculty members in the area of finance, including entrepreneurship, corporate finance, asset pricing, household finance, and real estate. Applicants for an assistant professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, demonstrate the promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an associate and professor level appointment should have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. For questions, please email financesearch@gsb.columbia.edu Applicants for an assistant professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a PhD from an accredited institution, demonstrate promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an associate and professor level appointment should have a PhD from an accredited institution, a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194181": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 06 The Graduate School of Business at Columbia University is seeking to hire one or more tenured faculty members in the area of finance. All applicants should have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution and a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research, teaching and service.  Applicants should have accomplishments and scholarly standing comparable to the very best scholars in their field. For questions, please email financesearch@gsb.columbia.edu All applicants should have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution and a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research, teaching and service.  Applicants should have accomplishments and scholarly standing comparable to the very best scholars in their field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194182": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Economics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Economics Location: 06 The Graduate School of Business at Columbia University is seeking to hire one or more tenure-track faculty members in any area of economics.   We welcome scholars whose research focuses on organizations (firms, public entities, non-profits), market institutions, and other topics of particular interest to business schools. Applicants for an assistant professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, demonstrate the promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an associate and professor level appointment should have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution, a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an assistant professor level appointment should have, or be close to completing, a PhD from an accredited institution, demonstrate the promise of becoming an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school. Applicants for an associate and professor level appointment should have a PhD from an accredited institution, a record of being an outstanding scholar in every respect, including research and teaching, and should combine exceptional disciplinary training with a strong interest in the professional mission of the school."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194195": {
      "title": "",
      "description": ""
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194229": {
      "title": "Associate Professor (tenured)/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Economics Location: 01 Columbia University’s Department of Economics invites applications for an associate professor with tenure or full professor to begin as early as July 1, 2027. We are particularly interested in hiring in the areas of macroeconomics, labor, or financial economics. We seek an individual with an outstanding publication record, proven excellence as a teacher, and an interest in participating in all aspects of the Department's pedagogical program. The responsibilities of the position include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research and publication, and institutional service. Review begins immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Ph.D., outstanding publication record, proven excellence as a teacher. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required material: curriculum vitae Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV in Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Additionally, the following supporting documents must be uploaded at www.econjobmarket.org (EJM): curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching ability, and a selection of research papers. Please note that an application will not be considered complete unless supporting materials are uploaded in EJM and the applicant has created a profile and uploaded a CV in ASR."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194233": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Economics Location: 01 Columbia University’s Department of Economics invites applications for a tenure-track assistant or associate professor to begin as early as July 1, 2027. We primarily seek scholars who specialize in the areas of Finance, Labor Economics, and Macroeconomics, but may consider other fields. The responsibilities of the position include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research and publication, and institutional service.  The appointment can be at a tenure-track assistant or associate professor rank. Applications received by November 13, 2026, will receive full consideration. Ph.D. in Economics or a related field is required at the time of appointment. Applicants should have a demonstrated ability to engage in research and teaching at the undergraduate level and show promise for future development in research and teaching at the graduate level. Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV in Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. ASR is the Columbia University version of Interfolio. If you have a dossier account with Interfolio, after you select \"Apply Now\" you will be prompted to login to your dossier account or you can create an account if you do not have one. There is no charge to create a dossier account or to apply for any CU position. Additionally, the following supporting documents must be uploaded at www.econjobmarket.org (EJM): curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching ability, a selection of research papers, and three letters of recommendation. Please note that an application will not be considered complete unless supporting materials are uploaded in EJM and the applicant has created a profile and uploaded a CV in ASR. For inquiries, please contact econrecruitment@columbia.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194305": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatric at CUMC -Newborn Nursery",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 THE OPPORTUNITY/POSITION DESCRIPTION This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Assistant Professor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Provide timely and high-quality care to healthy newborns in the busy well-baby nursery, located at Columbia University Irving Medical Center/Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital. · Provide comprehensive medical evaluation and management of healthy and medically complex newborns in the Well Baby Nursery, delivering evidence-based, family-centered care from admission through hospital discharge. · Admit, evaluate, and discharge newborn patients, including conducting admission histories and physical examinations, developing and implementing treatment plans, coordinating discharge readiness, and ensuring appropriate outpatient follow-up. · Provide clinical coverage for the Well Baby Nursery on a rotating schedule, including weekdays, weekends, holidays, and shared call responsibilities, to ensure continuous physician coverage and timely evaluation and management of newborn patients. · Perform daily newborn rounds and manage common neonatal conditions, including feeding difficulties, hyperbilirubinemia, hypoglycemia, neonatal abstinence syndrome, and other conditions requiring close monitoring or intervention. · Collaborate with obstetric, maternal-fetal medicine, neonatal intensive care, nursing, lactation, social work, and pediatric subspecialty teams to ensure coordinated, multidisciplinary care and seamless transitions throughout the newborn hospitalization. · Educate and counsel parents and caregivers regarding newborn health, breastfeeding and nutrition, safe sleep practices, immunizations, routine newborn screening, and discharge instructions to support a safe transition to outpatient pediatric care. · Teach and supervise trainees in the clinical care of newborns, while participating in quality improvement initiatives, patient safety programs, and scholarly activities that advance the clinical, educational, and research missions of the Department of Pediatrics. commitment of diversity Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and Educator. The University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment, and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Columbia University welcomes applications from individuals who may have had nontraditional career paths, or who may have taken time off for family reasons (e.g. children, caring for disabled or elderly family), or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of academia (e.g., in professional or industry service). The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and is committed to supporting the work-life balance of its faculty. We are interested in candidates who have a record of success advising and mentoring individuals from groups underrepresented in higher education and is particularly interested in candidates who have research interests in subjects that will contribute to the understanding of diversity and equal opportunity. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS · Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License · Must be Board certification in Pediatrics · Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; · The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194370": {
      "title": "Legal Research Fellow II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Law School Location: 01 The Columbia Center on Law and the Economy (CLE), founded by Associate Professors of Law Lina M. Khan and Lev Menand, is seeking a Legal Research Fellow II to support legal and policy research in competition, consumer protection, financial regulation, corporate governance and other economic law and policy domains. This position will support the Center’s research agenda on the laws, policies, and practices that shape the economy. This position also supports CLE’s advisory work with policymakers and civil society organizations, including through supporting and participating in events and trainings. Responsibilities include: Conducting legal and policy analysis across a variety of economic domains. Preparing materials for and providing advisory support to policymakers, civil society organizations, and other stakeholders as needed. Supporting internal coordination and review processes for select research projects. This appointment is as an Officer of Research in the rank of Senior Staff Associate I. Minimum qualifications: JD required plus 2 - 3 years of relevant work experience, preferably in litigation, public policy, government, or legal research. Willingness to take initiative and learn new areas, and to work in a highly interdisciplinary, flexible, and dynamic environment. Excellent organizational and project management skills. Excellent interpersonal skills Must be able to work with diverse constituencies and support an inclusive work environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194374": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Associate Research Scientist/Instructor/Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pathology Location: 02 The Department of Pathology and Cell Biology at the Columbia University Medical Center seeks highly qualified individuals for faculty positions in anatomic pathology, clinical pathology and research. Appointments can be at the Assistant, Associate, Professor, or Associate Research Scientist level depending on experience and qualifications. All positions require outstanding academic credentials and a record of scholarly productivity. Clinical positions require board certification or board eligibility and a NYS license. Research positions require record of publication in leading journals and a The Department of Pathology and Cell Biology at the Columbia University Medical Center seeks highly qualified individuals for faculty positions in anatomic pathology, clinical pathology and research. Appointments can be at the Assistant, Associate, Professor, or Associate Research Scientist level depending on experience and qualifications. All positions require outstanding academic credentials and a record of scholarly productivity. Clinical positions require board certification or board eligibility and a NYS license. Research positions require record of publication in leading journals and a statement of research directions. A history of successful research funding is desirable. Columbia University’s Department of Pathology & Cell Biology invites applications for a junior/senior level faculty position. Candidates should have a strong record of research and teaching qualifications (distinction). Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication portfolio and supervise graduate students and fellows. Candidate will play a vital role in departmental activities. At CUIMC, we stand together because diverse experiences, perspective, and values enrich every dimension of our work. Join our team and see how your unique skills and experiences can create a real impact by changing lives. Minimum Degree Required: Ph.D, M.D., M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent. Minimum Qualifications: Depending on the field of research and current needs for field and instructional coverage within the department, certain subspecialties may be preferred. Current research specialties: Cancer Biology, Neurodegenerative Diseases, Cellular and Molecular Neurobiology, Cell Motility, Cytoskeleton and Intracellular Trafficking, Virology, Neuronal Stem Cells, Precision Medicine, Clinical or Anatomic subspecialties. Preferred Qualifications: Depending on the field of research and current needs for field and instructional coverage within the department, certain subspecialties may be preferred. All candidates must submit a current CV. Submit a cover letter and biosketch if applicable. Hiring Salary Range: $79,327 to $450,000 Associate Research Scientist: $79,327 - $125,000 Instructor: $100,727 - $150,000 Assistant Professor / Asst Professor at CUMC: $108,748 - $335,000 Associate Professor / Associate Professor at CUMC: $116,769 - $368,000 Professor / Professor at CUMC: $132,811 - $450,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194390": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Biological Sciences Location: 01 The Department of Biological Sciences at Columbia University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in any area of biology. The successful applicant will be involved in research studying important topics in modern biology, such as metabolism, microbiology, cancer biology, neurobiology, plant biology, development, and/or molecular and cell biology. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in biology; sustain an active, independent research group; participate in committee and service work at the departmental and university levels; be an effective communicator; and exemplify academic and professional leadership. The appointment will begin July 1, 2027. The Columbia University Department of Biological Sciences is one of the leading biology departments in the world and offers many opportunities for faculty members to collaborate with colleagues around Columbia and at nearby institutions, including the Columbia University Irving Medical Center, the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, the Irving Institute for Cancer Dynamics, the New York Structural Biology Center, the New York Genome Center, and the Columbia Data Science Institute. Additionally, New York City is home to other world-class biomedical institutions, such as NYU, Weill Cornell Medical Center, Rockefeller University, Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, Mount Sinai School of Medicine, Albert Einstein School of Medicine, CUNY ASRC, and others. Applicants must have a PhD at the time of appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicant Materials (which should have 0.5 inch margins, single spaced, 11 point Arial font) should include: Curriculum Vitae . No page limit. All relevant information should be included, and publications should be listed. Research Statement. Up to 5 pages, including a summary of previous research achievements and future research plans, with references. Figure captions should be no smaller than 8 point font. The applicant’s previous and future research should be described. Teaching Statement . Describe your previous teaching experience, if any, and your pedagogical approach to teaching, as well as what courses you would be qualified to teach. Names and contact information for three individuals who may be asked to provide letters of reference .  The most helpful letters will address the applicant’s accomplishments in teaching, mentorship, research, independence and creativity, proposal and grant writing, and communication.  Letters will be solicited for a subset of candidates and will be due in January 2027 Cover Letter .  A cover letter (maximum 1 page) summarizing your qualifications and plans. R eview of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194451": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Philosophy Location: 01  The Department of Philosophy at Columbia University invites applications for a tenure track Assistant Professor/Associate Professor position in social and political philosophy, broadly construed to include historical and contemporary approaches across analytic, continental, and non-Western traditions. Area of Specialization: social and political philosophy. Area of Competence: open. The position will begin July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach 4 courses per year (2/2) at the undergraduate and graduate levels, supervise Ph.D. students, and provide service to the Department. Teaching in Columbia’s Core Curriculum may be part of the teaching load. Ph.D. must be in hand at the time of the appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s online Academic Search and Recruitment System (ASR). Please upload the following required materials into ASR: cover letter, curriculum vitae, 1 writing sample, research statement, teaching statement, and 3 letters of recommendation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194484": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor and Chair, Department of Sociomedical Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Sociomedical Sciences Location: 02 The Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health invites nominations and applications for the role of Stephen Smith Professor and Chair of the Department of Sociomedical Sciences (SMS). One of the nation's leading departments focused on the social and structural determinants of health, SMS has built an international reputation for interdisciplinary scholarship, innovative education, and impactful research that advances public health theory, policy, and practice. The successful candidate will be a distinguished scholar and respected academic leader with a demonstrated record of scientific achievement, substantial extramural funding, and a commitment to advancing knowledge related to the social and structural determinants of health. They will bring an ability to build partnerships, foster interdisciplinary collaboration, support outstanding faculty and students, strengthen employee teams, and navigate the opportunities and challenges facing research-intensive academic institutions. DEPARTMENT OF SOCIOMEDICAL SCIENCES The Department of Sociomedical Sciences (SMS) is dedicated to analyzing and addressing the social determinants of health. SMS brings together social and behavioral science disciplines to educate the next generation of public health leaders and to conduct action-oriented research. The disciplines examine the relationship between trends in health outcomes and structural and social forces - including economics, discrimination, stigma, and law - to expose the fundamental social causes of public health outcomes. It translates theory and evidence to identify the approaches needed at population, clinical, and individual levels to provide and protect health. SMS draws upon a diverse range of analytic methods and conceptual frameworks from anthropology, behavioral science, ethics, history, political science, psychology, and sociology. Using these tools, faculty, staff, and students explore a wide array of public health issues. SMS faculty have a track record of conceptually innovative work that has produced ground-breaking findings in areas such as mental health, global population aging, cancer prevention, health of women and girls, urban health, HIV, tobacco and drug use, adolescent health, occupational and environmental health, and immigrant health. SMS has longstanding areas of expertise in women’s health, substance use, HIV prevention and treatment, gender and sexuality, health communications, and environmental health. The department also studies global health, mental health, criminal justice, energy insecurity, climate change and reproductive policies through community-engaged research. SMS has a strong foundation in implementation science, place-based interventions, ethics and data science. New ideas and areas of focus are welcome in the department. ROLE OF THE CHAIR The next Chair of the Department of Sociomedical Sciences has a unique opportunity to lead one of the nation's foremost departments dedicated to understanding and addressing the social and structural determinants of health. Building on a distinguished history of interdisciplinary scholarship, innovative education, and community-engaged research, the Chair will guide SMS into its next era of growth and influence. The Chair will provide strategic, academic, and administrative leadership for a dynamic department whose faculty, students, and research programs are advancing public health knowledge and practice locally, nationally, and globally. As the Department's academic leader, the Chair will be responsible for maintaining and extending SMS's preeminence in research, teaching, and service while fostering innovation across its scholarly and educational programs. The Chair will support and develop a distinguished faculty, promote a collaborative and inclusive departmental culture, and ensure that students continue to receive a rigorous and impactful educational experience. With responsibility for faculty recruitment, retention, mentorship, promotion, and professional development, the Chair will play a central role in cultivating the next generation of scholars and public health leaders. The successful candidate will also oversee departmental operations and resources, ensuring the long-term financial health and organizational effectiveness of the Department. KEY OPPORTUNITIES AND CHALLENGES FOR THE NEXT CHAIR Define the next chapter of sociomedical science. The next Chair will have the opportunity to articulate a compelling vision for the Department's future, building upon its distinguished legacy while expanding its intellectual influence across Columbia Mailman, CUIMC, Columbia University, and the broader public health community. Position SMS at the forefront of interdisciplinary public health science. The next Chair will have the opportunity to leverage Columbia's vast academic and research ecosystem to deepen existing collaborations, foster new interdisciplinary partnerships, and expand the Department's influence in emerging areas of public health. Strengthen and diversify the Department’s research enterprise. The Chair will help position the Department to pursue innovative research opportunities while sustaining its longstanding commitment to rigorous, policy-relevant scholarship. Recruit, develop, and support an exceptional faculty community. The next Chair will play a critical role in recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining outstanding faculty while fostering a culture of collaboration, inclusion, intellectual curiosity, and academic excellence. The successful candidate for the Stephen Smith Professor and Chair of Sociomedical Sciences will possess: A doctoral degree in public health or a related social science discipline or an MD with credentials commensurate with appointment as a tenured full professor at Columbia University; An internationally recognized record of scholarly achievement and intellectual leadership, including a distinguished body of research that has advanced understanding of the social, behavioral, structural, cultural, and political determinants of health; A strong record of securing and leading externally funded research programs and supporting the growth of a vibrant and impactful research enterprise; A demonstrated commitment to interdisciplinary public health science, with the ability to bridge disciplines, methodologies, and perspectives in pursuit of innovative solutions to complex public health challenges; The vision and strategic acumen to articulate and implement a compelling future direction for the Department while aligning departmental priorities with those of the Columbia Mailman School of Public Health, CUIMC, and Columbia University; Demonstrated success building, leading, and sustaining high-performing interdisciplinary teams, centers, programs, or academic units; A demonstrated commitment to mentorship, faculty development, and faculty success across all career stages, including recruitment, retention, promotion, leadership development, and succession planning; The ability to inspire, motivate, and lead through periods of change, growth, and evolving institutional and funding landscapes; A collaborative, transparent, and communicative leadership style characterized by integrity, sound judgment, and a commitment to shared governance and inclusive decision-making; Demonstrated skill in building consensus, navigating complex organizational environments, and addressing challenges with diplomacy, effectiveness, and respect; Strong administrative, financial, and organizational leadership abilities, including experience managing budgets, personnel, academic programs, and strategic initiatives; Exceptional relationship-building skills and the intellectual curiosity, political acumen, and institutional awareness to engage stakeholders across the School, medical center, University, community, and broader public health field; The ability to develop and strengthen partnerships across disciplines, departments, centers, universities, community organizations, governmental agencies, and other external collaborators; A demonstrated commitment to advancing equity, justice, and human rights through scholarship, education, leadership, and public engagement, and; A deep appreciation for the role of sociomedical sciences in addressing society's most pressing public health challenges and a commitment to translating research into meaningful impact on policy, practice, and population health. Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search process. Inquiries, nominations, referrals, and CVs with cover letters should be sent via the Isaacson, Miller website: https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/columbia-university-mailman-school-public-health/department-chair-sociomedical Electronic submission of materials is strongly encouraged. Stephanie Fidel, Partner Jasmine Miller, Senior Associate Maria Peña, Senior Search Coordinator Isaacson, Miller"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194593": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Neuroscience (in the Mortimer B. Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neuroscience Location: 02 The Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute ( https://zuckermaninstitute.columbia.edu/ ) and the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons ( https://www.ps.columbia.edu/ ) at Columbia University seek to recruit outstanding scientists, preferentially at the Assistant Professor tenure-track level. We are seeking candidates from diverse backgrounds who have demonstrated exceptional ability, creativity and productivity, and are committed to a culture of inclusivity and belonging. Appropriate departmental affiliation will be decided after selection. Generous start-up funds, space, and mentorship will be provided. Research labs will be housed in the Jerome L. Greene Science Center, a highly interactive state-of-the-art facility on Columbia’s Manhattanville campus. Areas of interest include research programs that: 1) employ genetic, genomic and evolutionary approaches to understand the neural basis of behavior; 2) employ state­-of­-the-­art optical, chemical or electrical methodology combined with a computational approach to link neural circuitry to behavior, especially in genetically­ tractable mammalian model systems; 3) combine behavioral and physiological approaches to study cognitive processes; and 4) explore emerging issues in molecular, cellular, and developmental neurosciences. We particularly seek individuals who can complement our strength in integrating computational and empirical approaches to understanding the brain, from molecules to mind. Columbia University has an exceptionally strong and broad program in the neurosciences with an aim to enhance interactions between basic and clinical research and to link the neurosciences with a wide range of other disciplines within the University. There are numerous opportunities for interaction with other scientific departments and programs at the Medical Center and Morningside Heights campuses. Applications received by November 30, 2026 will receive full consideration. Anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree Interested applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, and also arrange for three letters of recommendation to be submitted."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194768": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Statistics Location: 01 The Department of Statistics invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position to begin July 1, 2027. A Ph.D. in statistics or a related field by July 1, 2027 is required. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication agenda and to teach in the departmental undergraduate and graduate programs. The field of research is open to any area of statistics and probability. The Department, like the University itself, is an extraordinarily vibrant academic community. For further information about the Department and our programs, please go to our webpage at: http://www.stat.columbia.edu A Ph.D. in statistics or a related field by the date of appointment is required, as is a commitment to high-quality research and teaching in statistics and/or probability. All applications must be submitted through Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruitment portal (ARS) and must include the following materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, research statement, and the names of three references, who will be asked to upload letters of recommendation on the applicant’s behalf. Inquiries may be made to Jack Tomaselli at jack.tomaselli@columbia.edu. Review of applications begins on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194750": {
      "title": "All Ranks: Instructor, Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 The Department of Neurology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites applications for full-time faculty positions.  The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s mission by engaging in high-impact research, providing exceptional teaching, and/or clinical training. The position offers opportunities for collaboration with multidisciplinary teams in a dynamic academic and clinical environment. The successful candidate will join a highly dedicated community offer extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. For more information on the Department of Neurology visit: Columbia Neurology Compensation and academic rank will vary based on credentials, expertise, and experience. M.D. or Ph.D. Clinical Providers: Must be able to complete the New York Presbyterian Hospital credentialing requirements before starting. NYS License Board Certified/Eligible in Neurology Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV in Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system.  Preferred CV formatting guidelines: CUIMC CV Format-2.17.22."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194755": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor of Visual Arts (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of the Arts Location: 01 Field of Specialization Any Medium/Discipline The Visual Arts Program in the School of the Arts at Columbia University invites applications for a position at the rank of Assistant Professor/Associate Professor of Visual Arts (tenure-track) specializing in any medium or discipline, effective July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will be innovative and passionate , and have a significant exhibition record and advanced technical skills grounded in the history of their respective medium(s) and possess strong leadership qualities. Appointees must have a thorough knowledge of contemporary art discourse and a demonstrated ability to mentor and engage critically with our diverse student population regardless of medium. A minimum of three years teaching experience at both the undergraduate and graduate level, full-time or adjunct, strongly preferred. Columbia’s Visual Arts Program is interdisciplinary and fosters rich creative work at both the graduate (MFA in Visual Arts) and undergraduate (BA with Visual Arts major) levels in the areas of Moving Image, Painting, Photography, Printmaking, Sculpture, and Expanded practice. The Visual Arts Program, in collaboration with the Department of Music and the Computer Music Center, also offers a course of study in the discipline of Sound Art which leads to an MFA in Visual Arts. The Program offers undergraduate studio classes in all areas mentioned, enrolling over 700 registrations each school year, and offers a major in Visual Arts leading to a BA awarded by Columbia College or the School of General Studies. The appointee will teach graduate students across all mediums as well as undergraduate studio courses, advise students at both the graduate and undergraduate levels, lead Grad Studio or Group Critiques, oversee MFA theses, and mentor postgraduate careers. The full-time teaching load consists of four classes each school year, and Independent Study at faculty’s discretion. Participation is required in rotating service and leadership roles, including committee work in collaboration with other programs in the School. A fully engaged presence on campus is expected along with a willingness to collaborate with peers to help shape the future of the Visual Arts Program and the School of Arts at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. Candidates for Assistant Professor or Associate Professor (tenure-track position) must have demonstrated and shown promise of the capacity for creative work and the accompanying pedagogy qualifying them for future nomination to tenure. Rank and salary commensurate with experience. Review of applications will begin January 10th, 2027 and continue until the position is filled. Minimum degree required: BA or equivalent Preferred degree: MA or MFA strongly preferred Teaching experience: Minimum of three years teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level strongly preferred. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: cover letter, CV, teaching philosophy, contact information for three referees, and samples of your work, provided through a word or PDF document with a live URL link to a website with a minimum of 15 work sample images (No slideshows or PowerPoints accepted)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195083": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor",
      "description": "Posted: Oct 09, 2026 Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Statistics Location: 01 The Department of Statistics at Columbia University invites applications for a tenured appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor, with a research focus on statistics and artificial intelligence. We seek outstanding candidates whose research advances the foundations, methodology, or applications of statistics and AI, including the statistical and probabilistic foundations of machine learning and large language models, generative modeling, and AI methods for scientific discovery. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The department expects to support the successful candidate with a generous research allowance. The expected teaching load is two semester-long courses per year. The department currently consists of 40 faculty members and 60 PhD students. The department has been expanding rapidly and, like the University itself, is an extraordinarily vibrant academic community. We are especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching and/or service will contribute to the excellence of the academic community. For further information about the department and our activities, centers, research areas, and curricular programs, please go to our web page at: http://www.stat.columbia.edu PhD in statistics, probability, computer science, mathematics, or a closely related field  is required, as is a commitment to high quality research and teaching in statistics and/or probability. Demonstrated strong commitment to teaching, advising, and research is desired. Candidates should also evince intellectual leadership and impact on the field and potential for significant contributions to the department, University, and wider scholarly community. All applications must be submitted through Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruitment portal (ARS) and must include the following materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, research statement, and the names of three references, who will be asked to upload letters of recommendation on the applicant’s behalf. Inquiries may be made to Jack Tomaselli at jack.tomaselli@columbia.edu. Review of applications begins on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194888": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Molecular Pharmacology and Therapeutics at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pharmacology Location: 02 The Yu Laboratory in the Department of Pharmacology and Therapeutics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) is seeking a highly motivated scientist for a Research-Track Assistant Professor (Non-Tenure Track) position. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr. Yonghao Yu to help lead the laboratory's research program, mentor trainees, and contribute to the strategic and administrative direction of the lab. Key Responsibilities • Supervise and mentor graduate students and postdoctoral fellows on a day-to-day basis, including experimental design, data interpretation, and career development. • Play a central role in the preparation, writing, and submission of grant proposals (e.g., NIH R01/R21, foundation, and fellowship applications) to sustain and grow lab funding. • Organize and coordinate the research activities of the laboratory, including project planning, resource allocation, and lab management. • Contribute new ideas and help shape future research directions for the laboratory, in areas spanning genome stability, replication stress and cancer biology. • Design, execute, and interpret experiments related to the laboratory's core research programs, and represent the laboratory's work through publications and presentations at scientific meetings. Minimum Qualifications • Ph.D. in genetics, molecular biology, or cell biology. • Demonstrated expertise in genome stability, replication stress and cancer biology. • Background in the study of the mechanisms by which cells respond to DNA damage and replication stress is strongly preferred. • Solid record of first-author publications in peer-reviewed journals, commensurate with career stage. • Prior experience mentoring junior researchers and/or contributing to grant writing is highly desirable. • Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in a team-oriented research environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194906": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor of Psychiatry (General Psychiatry - Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Department of Psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites applications for a tenure-track or tenure-track equivalent full-time faculty positions. The successful candidate will contribute to the department's mission by engaging in high-impact research. Candidates with a Ph.D., M.D./Ph.D. , or M.D. will be considered at all levels of seniority (Assistant, Associate , and Full Professor ). Research programs can investigate topics relevant to any area of mental health and psychiatry across the lifespan, ranging from childhood, to adolescence, adulthood, and older adulthood (geriatrics). Research approaches may include, but are not limited to: Neuroscience research aimed at revealing mechanisms at molecular, cellular, developmental, circuit, systems , and/or computational levels of analysis in model systems and/or humans, including genetic and genomic approaches. Pathophysiology and experimental therapeutics research using translational , computational and/or imaging approaches in animals or humans to elucidate disease and therapeutic mechanisms and to test novel treatments, including pharmacological, neuromodulatory and psychosocial interventions . Epidemiolog y , prevention and health services research that uses large population and clinical data along with novel causal inference methods to inform our understanding of the development, determinants, expression and treatment of psychiatric disorders across the lifespan and across populations, including global mental health . Community-partnered implementation research and practice that focuses on improving health and behavioral health outcomes for individuals receiving services in real-world care and community settings. All mental health and psychiatric conditions , including their intersection with health and the influence of social determinants, are of potential interest, including but not limited to mood , anxiety and psychotic disorders , disorders affecting cognition , child and neurodevelopmental disorders, disorders arising in geriatric patient populations , substance use disorders , eating disorders , and suicidal behavior . The successful candidate will join a highly dedicated community that offers extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. The position offers opportunities for collaboration with multidisciplinary teams in a dynamic academic research and clinical environment. For more information on the Department of Psychiatry , visit our w ebsite . Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae, short research and teaching statements, 2-3 key publications, and 3 letters of recommendation. Applications will begin to be evaluated November 1, 2026. Minimum Qualifications: M.D. and/or Ph.D. in Psychiatry or a related field Hiring Salary Range: Assistant Professor: $108,748 - $270,000 Associate Professor: $116,769 - $304,234 Full Professor: $132,811 - $386,446"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194907": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor of Psychiatry (Computational Psychiatry - Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Department of Psychiatry at Columbia University is inviting applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant to full professor level. We seek candidates whose research is focused on computational approaches to understand neurocognitive mechanisms of psychiatric disorders. Expertise in theory-driven cognitive paradigms and computational analysis of neurofunctional (e.g., fMRI/EEG) and behavioral data is required, along with strong skills in advanced data-driven methods. Emphasis on translation into clinical applications and bridging preclinical to clinical work is strongly preferred. The successful candidate will be engaged in multidisciplinary, translational, collaborative work with clinical and preclinical researchers within and beyond the Department, who are studying related processes in humans and non-human model systems across the lifespan. Candidates will have a background in psychiatry, neuroscience, psychology, or related areas (holding an MD, MD/PhD, PhD or equivalent degree) and should have, or demonstrate strong potential to obtain, sustained extramural funding to support an independent research program. We encourage members of minority groups, women, persons with disabilities, and covered veterans to apply. Applicants should send a letter of application, curriculum vitae, short research and teaching statements, 2-3 key publications, and arrange for 3-5 letters of recommendation. The successful candidate will join a highly dedicated community that offers extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. The position offers opportunities for collaboration with multidisciplinary teams in a dynamic academic research and clinical environment. For more information on the Department of Psychiatry , visit our w ebsite . Please note that suggested salary ranges are subject to adjustment based on professional rank and a number of additional factors. Minimum Qualifications: M.D. and/or Ph.D. in Psychiatry or a related field Hiring Salary Range: Assistant Professor: $108,748 - $270,000 Associate Professor: $116,769 - $304,234 Full Professor: $132,811 - $386,446"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194932": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine  - Cell Engineering & Therapy Location: 02 The Columbia Initiative in Cell Engineering and Therapy (CICET) is seeking a highly motivated Staff Associate I. The Staff Associate I, reporting to the Associate Director of PD and GMP manufacturing, will collaborate with the CICET QC, Facility Management, and QA teams, as well as research teams, to scale and translate research production processes for cell and gene therapy products into a robust cGMP manufacturing process. The specialist will produce cell therapy drug products in a GMP environment to ensure timely, cost-effective, and high-quality products for Phase 1/2a clinical trials. Responsibilities include: Functional Knowledge - Knowledge in cell and gene therapy process development, GMP manufacturing, documentation and compliance, and analysis of cell and gene therapy drug substances and products. Problem Solving - Utilizes analytical skills to solve moderately complex problems, GMP training, and experience using sound judgment and precedent. Decision Making/Autonomy - Works within guidelines from the Associate Director for PD and GMP operations to carry out assigned duties in the design of experiments, project needs, conduct of experiments, and data analysis. Technical Expertise - Perform process development experiments and GMP manufacturing in addition to maintaining PD reagents and equipment to ensure efficient day-to-day operations. Communication - Participate in planning and discussion of laboratory projects and data with the manager, contribute to experimental design, and provide advice on methodology. Accurately document batch activities in real-time using GMP-compliant systems and controlled documents. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Biology, Biotechnology, Cell Biology, Biochemistry, Bioengineering, or a related field and 4 years’ experience recommended. Hands-on experience in CAR-T or viral vector process development and/or GMP cell manufacturing. Strong understanding of CAR-T operations (e.g., T-cell handling, transduction, expansion, and cryopreservation) and vector production platforms."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194938": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Systems Biology Location: 02 The Judith Sulzberger Genome Center and Single-Cell Core within the Department of Systems Biology are seeking a candidate for the open Staff Associate I position to carry out sophisticated and cutting-edge experiments. Duties include, but are not limited to, the following: Sample processing, experimental design, and planning Provide expert technical guidance to postdoctoral researchers and faculty Manage Aviti and Nanopore user base, inventory, and training of new users Attend the laboratory needs of NextGen and Single-Cell Cores which include lab management, ordering, inventory management, financial management, IT coordination, equiptment repair and maintenance, comliance, updating and maintaing research protocols, training updates, and certifications. Functional Knowledge - 30% Knowledge and understanding of the field of Next-generation sequencing and scientific methods of analysis. Problem Solving - 10% Utilizes analytical  skills to solve moderately complex problems based on guidelines, protocols, and procedures. Decision Making/Autonomy - 10% Works within guidelines to carry out assigned duties in the design of apparatus, the adaptation of relevant technology to the needs of a project, the conduct of specific experiments, and the analysis of data. Technical Expertise - 30% Ability to operate and maintain equipment, computers, software programs, and research tools in support of the project. Communication - 20% Shares information and confirmation of datawith other lab personnel. Bachelor's degree in specific research field required. 4 years of experience preferred. Knowledge of pipetting techniques, running enzymatic reactions, documentation, and sample tracking preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194979": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Stem Cell Initiative Location: 02 The Staff Associate II will support the operations of the Columbia Stem Cell Initiative Flow Cytometry core, which works with over 600 users from 200 labs. The candidate will contribute to research by operating complex equipment for experiments, training users, providing experimental design consultations, maintaining equipment, troubleshooting experiments and equipment, communicating with researchers, performing routine lab maintenance. Responsibilities: Functional Knowledge Operate and maintain complex research instrumentation 10%, Participate in research by assisting users to designe xperiments. 20%, Train users on intricacies of instrumentation to ensure experimental success. 10% Problem Solving Troubleshooting problems with instrumentation. 5% Troubleshooting users' experiments on the instruments. 5% Troubleshooting data analysis. 5% Decision Making/Autonomy Collaboratively make decisions about instrument settings and parameters to ensure experimental success. 5%, Collaboratively make decisions about instrument performance to ensure instrument issues are addressed. 5% Technical Expertise Perform experiments on the instrumentation. 10%, Troubleshoot issues on instrument. 10%, Assist researchers to design and troubleshoot experiments. 5% Communication Communicate with researchers about technical topics;communicating with researchers about group training sessions. 10% Bachelor's degree At least 3 years hands-on flow cytometry experience. Understanding of essential concepts of cell and molecular biology. 4 years of experience are recommended"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194980": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate III",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Division of Pathophysiology & Treatment Research in the Department of Psychiatry is looking for a full time Senior Staff Associate lll to work within Clinical Therapeutics at Columbia University directed by Dr. Yuval Neria. This position requires exceptional strategic and operational expertise to join Professor Yuval Neria's posttraumatic stress disorder research program. The Senior Staff Associate III will assist in the development of innovative solutions to advance the translation of scientific findings into actionable public health solutions. Responsibilities: This role requires expert level knowledge and skills in the public health and mental health areas. The role requires performing ongoing literature reviews to remain current with new procedures and related research. 10%. This role requires the development of innovative solutions to complex research problems in the area of postraumatic stress and mental health. 30%. This role will be delegated the authority to implement new techniques that would have major impact on the implementation and outcomes of research projects. 15%. This role requires the management of substantial portions of complex projects. 30%. This role requires preparation of funding reports and submissions. 10%. Perform additional related duties or special projects as assigned. 5% Bachelor's degree in specific research field and 15+ years experience; Master's degree, or equivalent combination of education and experience preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194983": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Chemistry Location: 01 The Department of Chemistry at Columbia University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in any area of chemistry. We seek candidates who will pursue innovative and pioneering research addressing important problems in modern chemical science, including but not limited to organic, inorganic, physical, analytical, biological, materials, and theoretical/computational chemistry, as well as research at the interfaces of chemistry with biology, physics, engineering, energy, and data science. Exceptional candidates in all subfields are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and sustain an active, independent, and externally funded research group; teach undergraduate and graduate courses in chemistry; mentor graduate, postdoctoral, and undergraduate researchers; participate in committee and service work at the departmental and university levels and in the broader scientific community; be an effective communicator; and exemplify academic and professional leadership. The appointment will begin July 1, 2027. The new faculty member will be joining a collegial department at Columbia with a vibrant research enterprise, lively undergraduate and graduate teaching programs, and a strong record in public outreach in the heart of New York City. Applicants must have a PhD in chemistry or a related field at the time of appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicant materials should include: •       Cover Letter •       Curriculum Vitae •       Research Statement •       Teaching Statement •       Arrange to have three recommendation letters submitted on your behalf For full consideration apply by November 15, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195067": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Computer Science Location: 01 The Department of Computer Science at Columbia University is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Scientist to lead research at the intersection of formal methods, computer security, and artificial intelligence. The position focuses on developing formal verification techniques to enforce safety and security properties of autonomous, agentic AI systems and AI swarms. The successful candidate will work closely with lead faculty to design scalable, agentic verification frameworks capable of leveraging large-scale GPU infrastructure (e.g., Empire AI). The goal of the project is to automate the specification and verification of asafety and security properties of multi-agent AI systems. Key Responsibilities: Research & Development: Formalize and prove security and safety properties for agentic AI architectures and multi-agent swarms using interactive theorem provers. Infrastructure & Tooling: Build and scale automated proof and verification workflows designed to run on high-performance GPU clusters. Collaborative Research: Work closely with co-advisors, PhD/Master’s students, and external collaborators (such as the Lean FRO) to integrate state-of-the-art interactive theorem proving with modern AI systems. Publication & Dissemination: Publish high-impact research in top-tier security, formal methods, and AI conferences/journals (e.g., CAV, PLDI, S&P, CCS, NeurIPS, ICML). Required: Education: Ph.D. in Computer Science, Applied Mathematics, or a related quantitative field (completed or near completion). Domain Expertise: Strong background in formal verification, programming languages, software security, or trustworthy AI. Interactive Theorem Proving: Hands-on experience using Lean, or any similar interactive theorem proving system. Domain Knowledge: Understanding of agentic AI models, multi-agent frameworks, or AI swarms. Systems & Programming: Proficiency in functional programming (e.g., Lean) and Python/C++ for integrating verification tools with AI frameworks. Preferred: Experience with LLM-guided formal reasoning or neuro-symbolic methods (e.g., LLM + Lean integration). Background in multi-agent orchestration, swarm intelligence, or adversarial robustness in security systems. Familiarity with distributed computing or running large-scale automated reasoning workloads on GPU clusters. Interested candidates should submit the following: Cover Letter detailing research interests and relevant experience. Curriculum Vitae (CV) including a complete list of publications. Research Statement (1–2 pages) outlining past contributions and vision at the intersection of Lean, AI swarms, and formal security. Contact Information for 3 References ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195075": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Reproductive Sciences (in Obstetrics and Gynecology) at the Columbia University Medical Center",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: 07 Assistant Professor of Reproductive Sciences (in Obstetrics and Gynecology) at the Columbia University Medical Center Build what reproductive medicine needs next. The Columbia University Fertility Center is seeking an exceptional early-career scientist, engineer, physician-scientist, or technologist to join our research and development team as junior faculty. This is not a conventional laboratory position centered on a single technique, platform, or scientific question. Our laboratory starts somewhere different: with important problems in patient care that should be solvable but are not yet solved. We identify unmet needs in reproductive medicine where a new technology could meaningfully improve patient care, safety, access, efficiency, or outcomes. We then work from first principles to develop a solution, build the initial prototype, establish proof of concept, rigorously validate the technology, and ultimately translate successful innovations into clinical practice. The goal is not simply to study problems. The goal is to solve them. Our work has already led to technologies spanning artificial intelligence, robotics, microfluidics, genomics, diagnostics, laboratory automation, remote monitoring, and novel approaches to patient care. Examples include the STAR System for AI-enabled detection and recovery of extremely rare sperm; STORK, a rapid and lower-cost genetic test for pregnancy loss; APRIL, a robotic system designed to improve the precision of embryo-culture preparation; at-home blood and semen collection technologies; and systems designed to improve monitoring and safety of cryopreserved eggs, sperm, and embryos. We are looking for someone who wants to help invent what comes next. The opportunity The successful candidate will become a core member of a highly collaborative translational R&D program embedded within a major academic fertility center. Rather than being handed a narrowly defined project, the faculty member will have the opportunity to participate across the entire innovation cycle: Identify → Invent → Build → Test → Validate → Translate You will work directly with clinicians, scientists, embryologists, engineers, computational researchers, industry partners, and patients to understand problems deeply and develop practical solutions. A project might begin with a question such as: Why does this part of fertility care still work this way? What currently limits our ability to diagnose or treat this problem? Could a new assay, device, algorithm, robotic system, imaging platform, or workflow solve it? And then: Can we build it? What you will do The faculty member will: Identify high-impact unmet needs in reproductive medicine and fertility care. Translate clinical problems into scientifically and technically tractable research questions. Conceive and develop new technologies, assays, devices, computational approaches, and experimental platforms. Rapidly develop prototypes and proof-of-principle studies. Design rigorous experiments to determine whether promising ideas actually work. Lead validation studies and help move successful technologies toward clinical implementation. Draw upon technologies from fields outside reproductive medicine when they offer a solution to an important clinical problem. Collaborate closely with physicians, embryologists, molecular biologists, engineers, computer scientists, data scientists, and external technology partners. Build multidisciplinary teams around particularly promising ideas. Mentor students, trainees, research staff, and collaborators. Publish meaningful scientific work and present findings nationally and internationally. Pursue extramural funding and participate in development of intellectual property where appropriate. Help create a culture in which ambitious ideas are pursued with scientific rigor, intellectual openness, urgency, and a focus on real-world impact. Who we are looking for There is deliberately no single required scientific specialty for this position. The best candidate may come from reproductive biology, molecular biology, bioengineering, biomedical engineering, computer science, artificial intelligence, robotics, physics, genomics, diagnostics, microfluidics, imaging, or another field entirely. What matters more is how you think. We are looking for someone who: Is deeply curious and intellectually adventurous. Sees problems and instinctively begins thinking about how they might be solved. Is comfortable entering fields outside their formal training and learning rapidly. Can move between conceptual thinking and hands-on experimentation. Enjoys building and testing things rather than stopping at an interesting hypothesis. Is scientifically rigorous but not constrained by traditional disciplinary boundaries. Is comfortable with projects that initially have no obvious solution. Can distinguish an interesting technology from one that addresses an important clinical need. Thrives in highly collaborative, multidisciplinary environments. Has the persistence to take an idea through repeated cycles of failure, redesign, and improvement. Ultimately measures success not only by publications, but by whether the work changes what is possible for patients. We are particularly interested in candidates who bring capabilities or ways of thinking that do not already exist within our field. Why Columbia Fertility? The position offers something uncommon: the ability to develop new technology inside the clinical environment in which it is intended to be used . Our R&D program is closely integrated with physicians, embryologists, patients, clinical laboratories, and the day-to-day practice of reproductive medicine. Problems can therefore be identified directly from clinical care, prototypes can be developed with input from the people who will ultimately use them, and promising technologies can progress from concept toward real-world validation and deployment. The environment is intentionally entrepreneurial, interdisciplinary, and ambitious. The goals of the projects is to develop better ways of caring for patients, improving outcomes, and helping patients have success when it was previously impossible. The person we hope will join us We are looking for someone who, when confronted with a difficult and important problem, does not simply say: “That is an interesting research question.” They think: “There must be a way to solve this.” And then they start figuring out how. A PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant scientific, engineering, computational, or medical discipline. A strong record of scientific achievement appropriate to career stage. Evidence of creativity, independence, and rigorous experimental thinking. Demonstrated ability to develop new methods, technologies, systems, or approaches is highly desirable. Strong communication and collaborative skills. A commitment to translating scientific and technological innovation into meaningful improvements in patient care. Prior experience in reproductive medicine is welcome but not required . We are particularly interested in candidates who bring capabilities or ways of thinking that do not already exist within our field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195076": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Discipline",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic Location: 01 The Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race invites applications for a Lecturer in Discipline beginning July 1, 2027. This is a full-time three-year appointment. The successful candidate will be appointed in any department in the humanities or social sciences. Applicants should have a PhD in hand, a background in training or experience in Ethnic Studies, a commitment to scholarly and teaching excellence, and a specialization in some combination of the following fields: Asian American, African American, Latinx, Native and Indigenous, and/or comparative ethnic studies; thematic specialization is open, with special consideration given to those with expertise in Legal Humanities or Ethnical AI. The Lecturer will be a full member of the Center’s core faculty, with associated benefits and responsibilities. They will teach four courses a year, including courses in the ethnic studies core curriculum. Minimum Degree Requirement: Candidates must have received their doctoral degree. All applications must be made through Columbia University's Academic Search and Recruiting system (ASR/Interfolio). Please upload the following required materials: a cover letter, curriculum vitae, sample syllabus, writing sample (no more than 20 pages) and contact information for three academic references, who may be asked to provide a letter at a later stage of the search. Review of applications will begin February 15, 2027 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195081": {
      "title": "Staff Associate l",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Environmental Health Science Location: 02 Columbia Mailman School of Public Health’s Department of Environmental Health Sciences (EHS) is searching for a full-time Staff Associate I for the research group of Professor Dr. Ami Zota who directs the Toxic Chemicals and Health Research Driving Action and Equity Lab (THRIVE Lab), aiming to advance environmental justice and health equity through research, communication, advocacy, and training next generation leaders. Her nationally and globally recognized environmental health research focuses on social-structural determinants of chemical exposures, the biological responses to environmental chemicals, and resulting health impacts, especially in disadvantaged communities. Dr. Zota is equally committed to developing innovative approaches for science communication so that her research can more effectively be used to inform individual and collective decision-making. Under the supervision of Dr. Zota, the candidate will help coordinate activities for various research projects as well as community engagement efforts. The candidate will also contribute to original research through data collection, database management, data analysis, and manuscript preparation. Finally, the candidate will contribute to translating and communicating research findings to multiple and diverse audiences by developing fact sheets, social media posts, and power point presentations. The candidate will work closely with other members of the THRIVE lab, academic scientists, and community partners. Responsibilities: Coordinate a broad range of activities critical to research including obtaining human subjects approval from the IRB, scheduling team meetings, taking notes during meetings, and helping to prepare progress reports for funders. (20%) Perform quality assurance and quality control measures on raw data through database management and data cleaning. (20%) Conduct literature reviews and data analysis.(10%) Assist in the preparation and dissemination of individual-level and community report back of study results. (10%) Help prepare original research manuscripts for peer review publication. (10%) Develop communications materials to disseminate environmental health information through the THRIVE Lab website and over social media. (10%) Assist in the preparation of presentations and workshops content for community partners and other important audiences. (10%) Help support and co-mentor undergraduate and graduate students. (5%) Performs related duties and responsibilities as assigned/requested. (5%) Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in a related discipline. Degree must be conferred by the start date of the position. The candidate should have enthusiasm for supporting research, training, policy, and communication activities at the nexus of environmental and climate health sciences and environmental/climate/reproductive justice and be interested in working at the science-policy interface. The candidate should have strong writing and communication skills and basic skills in computer programming and data management. Preferred Qualification: Master’s degree in environmental health, climate and society, epidemiology, or related field. Versed in programming languages such as R, SAS, and Stata. Excellent oral, written, and visual communication skills (e.g., evidenced by journal articles, websites, op-eds and social media content) High attention to detail and level of organization, ability to work on multiple projects. Experience working with interdisciplinary teams on research projects. Ability to work and learn independently, including remote work as needed. Ability to work with environmental and/or health-affected communities and/or environmental justice groups from a wide array of cultures, backgrounds and scientific understanding."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195086": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist (Quick Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Department of Psychiatry at Columbia University Irvine Medical Center seeks a highly qualified Associate Research Scientist who will be responsible to establish and lead a research program in the Quick lab that aims to elucidate the function of membrane proteins implicated in psychiatric disorders using advanced molecular, cellular, and protein biochemical approaches. The Quick lab has been actively involved in both basic and applied research in the area of membrane transport processes with special interest in the physiology, bioenergetics, and molecular dynamics of Na+-coupled transport proteins. Using biochemical, biophysical, and molecular biological approaches, a major part of Dr. Quick’s research focuses on the structure and function of neurotransmitter transporters, the molecular targets of psychostimulants. His studies, in collaboration with leading experts in the field of computational biophysics, single-molecule fluorescence spectroscopy electron paramagnetic resonance spectroscopy, and crystallography, led to the discovery of a novel molecular mechanism of Na+-coupled symport and how antidepressant drugs inhibit transport. For further information, please visit: https://www.columbiapsychiatry.org/profile/Matthias-quick-phd The Associate Research Scientist will have the opportunity to interact and collaborate with researchers across Columbia University as well as with researchers at top-tier national research institutions. They will be responsible for establishing and leading a research program in the Quick lab that aims to elucidate the function of membrane proteins implicated in psychiatric disorders using advanced molecular, cellular, and protein biochemical approaches. At CUIMC, we stand together because diverse experiences, perspective, and values enrich every dimension of our work. Join our team and see how your unique skills and experiences can create a real impact by changing lives. Ph.D. neuroscience, pharmacology, biochemistry or related fields Other requirements: -          A minimum of 4 years in an active (wet lab) research setting is required. -         The candidate should have fundamental knowledge in the preparation of bacterial, insect and mammalian cell cultures for the isolation of targeted membrane proteins from these sources. Other required skills include but are not limited to the purification of detergent-solubilized membrane proteins, their reconstitution into artificial membrane systems of defined lipid environment (i.e., proteoliposomes and nanodiscs), and their functional evaluation using methods using electrophysiology, colorimetric and spectroscopic assays as well as radiotracer-based transport and binding studies. Also, the execution of a variety of molecular biology methods, as well as protein-biochemical analytical methods, is considered a required minimum skill set. -        Basic knowledge in the use of general office-based software as well as in the use of specialized scientific software is required. Preferred Qualifications: -  The candidate should have a strong publication record and demonstrated experience working with membrane proteins. Strong computational skills for specialized scientific software (plotting software, DNA sequence analysis software) drive and motivation, and demonstrated aptitude for performing independent research while being receptive to mentorship, as well as the experience and interest in training and mentoring team members, are highly desirable qualifications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195089": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: School of Social Work Location: New York NY The Social Intervention Group (SIG) at the Columbia University School of Social Work is seeking an Associate Research Scientist (ARS) to co-lead the scientific and day-to-day operations of SEE-US (Syphilis Education & Elimination in the US), a newly funded five-year NIH/NIAID R01. SIG develops, tests, and scales evidence-based interventions to address HIV/STIs, substance use, and gender-based violence among marginalized communities in the U.S. and around the world. SEE-US continues that work. It is a two-site hybrid type 2 effectiveness-implementation randomized controlled trial that uses a citizen science approach to engage community members in the creation of digital health messages to promote syphilis and HIV/STI testing uptake, which will then be tested in the trial. The ARS will work closely with Dr. Alissa Davis, the Principal Investigator of the study. They will contribute to the study’s research design, analysis, and publications, and will help oversee study startup and regulatory compliance, participant recruitment and retention, trial operations and participant safety, data management and quality, and the supervision and training of research assistants. The role is highly collaborative. The ARS will also serve as a liaison with partners at the University of Alabama at Birmingham, RTI International, Community Collaborative Research Boards and other organizational partners at each site. This is a chance to shape a Phase III clinical trial from launch through dissemination and to help build community-driven, scalable approaches to reducing syphilis and STI disparities in the US. REAPPOINTMENT IS CONTINGENT ON FUNDING AVAILABILITY . Responsibilities: The Associate Research Scientist’s (ARS) main responsibilities will be focused on co-leading the scientific and day-to-day operations of SEE-US, an NIH-funded study focused on using a citizen science approach to increase uptake of syphilis and HIV/STI testing among reproductive-aged women in New York City and Alabama. The ARS will be responsible for: - Study startup and regulatory compliance, - Coordination across study sites and communication with Community Collaborative Research Boards (CCRBs) and other study partners, - Study operations and trial procedures - Participant recruitment, enrollment, and retention, - Data monitoring, and leading or co-leading statistical analyses - Contributing to refinement of the study protocol, measures, and statistical analysis plan, in collaboration with the PI and the RTI International biostatistics team - Leading and co-authoring peer-reviewed manuscripts and conference presentations - Supervision and training of research assistants - NIH reporting and dissemination of study findings In addition, a small portion of the ARS’s responsibilities will involve assisting with procedures and operations for other studies led by Dr. Alissa Davis, (with effort charged to those studies’ funding sources). Minimum Qualifications : - PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in public health, social work, epidemiology, psychology, or a related field - 3+ years of experience managing NIH-funded clinical trials Preferred Qualifications: -       Postdoctoral research experience -       Experience using citizen science methods and conducting HIV/STI research -       Record of peer-reviewed publications in HIV/STI, sexual and reproductive health, or intervention research"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195096": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Middle Eastern, South Asian, and African Studies Location: 01 SOUTH ASIAN STUDIES The Department of Middle Eastern, South Asian, and African Studies at Columbia University invites applications for a tenured appointment in modern South Asian Studies, broadly conceived. The appointment will be at the rank of Associate or Full Professor.  The department seeks a scholar with a distinguished record of research and publication, whose work is grounded in the languages and cultural forms of the subcontinent. The successful candidate will join a department committed to innovative research and teaching, in which conversations across disciplines, regions, and historical periods are encouraged. Review of applications will begin November 9, 2026; the position will remain open until filled. On-campus interviews will take place in the spring 2027 semester. The appointment will begin on July 1, 2028. Applicants must upload a cover letter, CV, sample publication and the names of 3 referees. PhD Required Application Instructions: All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: • Cover letter explaining research and teaching interests and plans for future research • Curriculum vitae • Contact information for referees (who may be asked to upload letters to the ASR/Interfolio system at a later stage in the search process, after candidates are shortlisted)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195124": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Earth Institute: Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory (LDEO) Location: 04 The Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory of Columbia University located in Palisades, NY, invites applications for an Associate Research Scientist to work in a multidisciplinary team of senior and junior scientists engages in the MINERALS Project develop a mineral properties database for geophysical modeling, particularly for the interpretation of seismic tomography models and geodynamic processes in Earth's deep interior. This position is associated with the project \"Modeling Earth from Atomic to Global Scale (MINERALS),\" funded by the Gordon & Betty Moore Foundation. The primary goal of the MINERALS Project is to develop a mineral properties database for geophysical modeling, particularly for the interpretation of seismic tomography models and their geodynamic implications. The successful candidate will conduct computational simulations of minerals under extreme pressure and temperature conditions and will work in a team comprised of mineral physicists, seismologists, and geodynamicists to advance quantitative links between atomic-scale material behavior and the large-scale structure of the Earth. Duties and Responsibilities will include: Augment database of thermodynamic and thermoelastic properties of phases in the lower mantle, extending the database from properties of silica in various phases of the lower mantle to the NAL and CF phases, as well as additional compositions of bridgmanite and post-perovskite. Use ab initio DFT-based calculations and machine-learning interatomic potentials derived from DFT calculations. Disseminate results with the public via national and international conferences and seminars and with the general public via media outlets and outreach. Prepares research publications and reports and contributes to: patentable inventions, important research techniques and/or theories that significantly impact the science. Receives general supervision from the Professor/PI and interacts with Co-PIs. Applicants should have completed a PhD in mineral physics, materials science, computational physics, geophysics, or a related field and have at least three years of post-PhD research experience that includes software development, ab-initio calculations, and development of machine learning methods. Prior experience with density functional theory simulations, high-performance computing, machine learning, and/or computational modeling of materials is required. The search will remain open for at least 30 days after the ad appears and will continue until the position is filled. Appointments are made on a fiscal year basis and are eligible for renewal each July 1, contingent upon performance and funding. Columbia University benefits accompany this Officer of Research appointment. We accept online applications only."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195145": {
      "title": "Staff Associate - Milstein",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Finance Location: 06 Columbia Business School is seeking to hire one or more Research Staff Associates for the Paul Milstein Center for Real Estate, within the Finance Division.  The work will include quantitative and qualitative research and analysis projects from conception through publication, collection, coordination, and management of information and data, development of research and teaching materials, and work on special projects with all levels of faculty and staff, relating to the research areas within the Center and Finance division.  This position provides an opportunity to gain experience in academic business research with a special focus on real estate finance, securities and mortgage markets, financial intermediation and banking, financial technology, market structure and regulation, housing policy, and the impact of consumer credit markets on the broader economy.  This position is a two-year position with a possible extension to a third-year and would provide ideal preparation for a Ph.D. program or other graduate study.  For example, past associates have left this position to attend Ph.D. programs at Harvard, Stanford, University of Chicago, Kellogg, New York University, Duke, and Columbia University. Specific duties of the Research Staff Associate in the Paul Milstein Center for Real Estate include: Develop models and complete programming with software such as Stata, Matlab, SAS and Access. Perform statistical analysis including running regressions. Maintain computer data sets and databases.  Assist with data entry and ensure data integrity.  Extract and link data from different databases for analysis.  Prepare detailed documentation files. Assist with data analysis, interpretation, write up and dissemination of findings through the preparation of reports, journal articles, presentations, websites, and other research outlets. Perform case-based research, including work with detailed primary documents. Assist in the organization of research programs including conferences, seminars and workshops, and perform other related duties as assigned. Prepare presentations for teaching and research projects. Monitor the status of multiple projects and ensure that interim and final deadlines are met. Applicants for the Staff Associate position are expected to have the following: Bachelor’s degree and/or its equivalent Strong programming experience in one or more of the following languages: STATA, SAS, SPSS, Mathematica, Matlab, perl, Java, C++, SQL. Organizational ability and communications skills Candidates must demonstrate, either through prior work experience or academic course study, an aptitude and enthusiasm for research. Work experience in a research role, preferably in an academic environment. A degree with a major in economics, business, statistics, mathematics, finance, computer science, or engineering strongly preferred. Bachelor’s degree in economics, business, statistics, mathematics, finance, computer science, or engineering strongly preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195173": {
      "title": "Vice Dean of Research and Scholarship",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: School of Nursing Location: Columbia University School of Nursing  The Columbia University School of Nursing (Columbia Nursing or the School), one of the nation's premier schools of nursing and a recognized leader in research, education, clinical practice, and health equity, invites nominations and applications for its next Vice Dean of Research and Scholarship. Guided by its mission of \"Advancing Nursing to Advance Health for All,\" Columbia Nursing combines academic rigor, scientific discovery, clinical excellence, and community engagement to address some of society's most pressing health challenges. With a strong tradition of innovation and an expanding research enterprise, the School is uniquely positioned to lead the future of nursing science, healthcare delivery, and health equity in New York City, across the United States, and around the globe. Reporting directly to the Dean, the Vice Dean serves as the School's chief research officer and a member of the School’s senior leadership team, providing strategic leadership for a dynamic and growing research enterprise that advances scientific discovery, innovation, and scholarly impact. Working closely with faculty, university leadership, and external partners, the Vice Dean will help shape the future of nursing science at Columbia, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration and further elevating the School's influence in research, scholarship, and innovation. As the School's senior research leader, the Vice Dean will champion faculty success, strengthen mentorship and research support systems, and cultivate the next generation of nurse scientists and research leaders. The Vice Dean will possess exceptional communication and relationship-building skills, engaging effectively with faculty, students, staff, university leadership, funding agencies, and national scientific organizations while serving as a visible advocate for Columbia Nursing's research mission. Most importantly, the Vice Dean will build on the School's strong momentum and national prominence to expand its research impact, advance a culture of scientific excellence, and reinforce its position as a global leader in nursing science and scholarship. The successful candidate will be an accomplished and internationally recognized scholar with a distinguished record of scientific achievement, sustained extramural funding, and leadership within complex research environments. They will bring a compelling vision for advancing nursing science and interdisciplinary scholarship, along with a demonstrated ability to foster collaboration across Columbia University, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, and external partners. The Vice Dean will champion innovative research that addresses pressing health challenges, informs policy and practice, and translates scientific discovery into meaningful improvements in health outcomes. COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF NURSING The Columbia University School of Nursing (Columbia Nursing) is one of the nation's leading schools of nursing and a recognized innovator in nursing education, research, clinical practice, and health policy. Founded in 1892, the School is among the oldest nursing schools in the United States and has played a pioneering role in shaping the nursing profession, including establishing the nation's first clinical nursing specialty master's degree program and the first university-based midwifery program. Today, Columbia Nursing advances its mission through world-class education, groundbreaking research, and clinical innovation aimed at improving health outcomes and advancing health equity locally and globally. Situated within Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) in Washington Heights, the School benefits from close collaboration with the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons, the Mailman School of Public Health, the College of Dental Medicine, NewYork-Presbyterian, and a broad network of academic, clinical, and community partners. Columbia Nursing's location within one of the world's premier academic health centers creates exceptional opportunities for interdisciplinary scholarship, education, and clinical care. Columbia Nursing offers a comprehensive portfolio of graduate and professional programs, including the Master's Direct Entry (MDE) program, Master of Science in Advanced Clinical Management and Leadership, Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP), Doctor of Philosophy (PhD), Online Prerequisites for Entry to Nursing (OPEN) program, and subspecialty programs in Oncology and Women's Health. With more than 600 students enrolled across its graduate degree programs, the School prepares future clinicians, scientists, educators, and healthcare leaders through rigorous academic training grounded in scientific inquiry, innovation, and clinical excellence. With 68 full-time faculty members, alongside part-time, attending, and clinical preceptor staff, the School is home to an accomplished faculty of scholars, clinicians, and innovators whose work spans nursing science, health informatics, healthcare delivery, health policy, community and population health, implementation science, and clinical research. Columbia Nursing faculty include members of the National Academy of Medicine, Fellows of the American Academy of Nursing, Fellows of the New York Academy of Medicine, and other nationally and internationally recognized leaders whose scholarship continues to shape healthcare practice, policy, and education. Research Research is a defining strength of the Columbia University School of Nursing and a central pillar of its mission to advance health for all. Columbia Nursing has earned international recognition for innovative, rigorous scholarship and currently ranks among the nation's leading recipients of National Institutes of Health (NIH) funding among schools of nursing, including ranking first nationally in NIH funding for three consecutive years. Faculty, postdoctoral fellows, and students also secure support from a broad range of federal agencies, foundations, and healthcare organizations, reflecting the depth and impact of the School's research enterprise. Integrated within Columbia University and Columbia University Irving Medical Center, Columbia Nursing's research enterprise is distinguished by its interdisciplinary approach and its commitment to translating scientific discovery into meaningful improvements in health and healthcare. Faculty collaborate across academic, clinical, and community settings to address complex challenges facing individuals, families, communities, and health systems in the United States and around the world. The School's research portfolio spans a broad range of scientific disciplines and methodologies, including artificial intelligence, biobehavioral and omics research, data science and informatics, healthcare delivery innovation, primary and comprehensive care, symptom science, patient safety and quality, palliative care, prevention science, urban and global health, and research focused on underserved and vulnerable populations. A hallmark of Columbia Nursing's scholarship is its commitment to understanding and addressing the biological, social, behavioral, and structural factors that shape health outcomes and healthcare access. Research centers and programs serve as hubs for innovation and collaboration across the School. These include the Center for Health Policy, the Center for Community-Engaged Health Informatics and Data Science, the Center for Healthcare Delivery Research and Innovations, the Center for Research on People of Color, and the Center for Sexual and Gender Minority Health Research. Together, these centers advance discovery, support interdisciplinary partnerships, and generate knowledge that informs policy, improves care delivery, and promotes health and well-being across diverse populations. Building on this strong foundation, the next Vice Dean of Research and Scholarship will have the opportunity to further elevate Columbia Nursing's national and global influence in nursing science, healthcare innovation, and research excellence. ROLE OF THE VICE DEAN The next Vice Dean of Research and Scholarship has a remarkable opportunity to shape the future of nursing science at one of the nation's leading schools of nursing. Building on Columbia Nursing's strong foundation of research excellence, the Vice Dean will provide strategic leadership for a vibrant and growing research enterprise while helping advance the School's missions of research, education, clinical practice, and innovation. Working across Columbia University, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, and a broad network of external partners, the Vice Dean will be positioned to expand interdisciplinary collaboration, strengthen the School's research infrastructure, foster faculty and trainee success, and elevate the impact of nursing science on policy, practice, and health outcomes. As the School's chief research officer and senior advocate for its research mission, the Vice Dean will have the opportunity to build on Columbia Nursing's national and global reputation while navigating a rapidly evolving research landscape. KEY PRIORITIES FOR THE NEXT VICE DEAN The successful candidate will cultivate a culture of scientific excellence, mentorship, collaboration, and innovation; support the growth of externally funded research; and prepare the next generation of nurse scientists and research leaders. To realize this vision, the Vice Dean will address the following opportunities and challenges: Lead the next phase of growth for Columbia Nursing’s research enterprise. With a strong foundation of research excellence and a distinguished international reputation, the next Vice Dean will have the opportunity to provide vision and strategy that shapes the future of scholarship at Columbia Nursing, advances the School's scientific influence, expands its research impact, and strengthens its position as a leader in nursing science and healthcare innovation. The Vice Dean will foster and advance a culture of discovery, innovation, and scientific excellence while championing future research in alignment with the School’s mission. Expand interdisciplinary collaboration and research impact across Columbia's ecosystem. The Vice Dean will be uniquely positioned to leverage Columbia University's vast academic and clinical environment to foster new partnerships, catalyze innovative areas of inquiry, and strengthen the School's role in university-wide research initiatives. Working across disciplines and stakeholders, the Vice Dean will represent the School on key university bodies and research groups and serve as the primary advocate for the School’s research mission to advance high-impact scholarship that addresses society's most pressing health challenges. To foster collaboration within the School’s research enterprise, the Vice Dean will serve as the central communications leader and maintain close collaboration with the Dean, Vice Deans, Dean’s Council, Dean’s Leadership Council, Grants Management Office, faculty, postdoctoral fellows, PhD students, and research staff. Grow sponsored research in an increasingly competitive funding landscape. Building on Columbia Nursing's established research strengths, the next Vice Dean will have the opportunity to expand the School's portfolio of externally funded research by supporting faculty competitiveness, fostering collaborative grant activity, and identifying new opportunities for strategic growth and investment. The Vice Dean will maintain a discerning eye on the national landscape and represent the School in partnerships with federal agencies, foundations, and other stakeholders, including identifying opportunities and fostering connections between external stakeholders and Columbia researchers. Strengthen the pipeline of nurse scientists and research leaders. The next Vice Dean will play a central role in developing the next generation of nurse scientists and research leaders. Through leadership of the School’s research centers, doctoral education, postdoctoral training, and faculty development efforts, the Vice Dean will ensure that scholars at every stage of their careers are positioned to thrive and contribute meaningfully to the advancement of nursing science. This important work includes the oversight of faculty promotion processes and annual evaluations for the 23 faculty within the research division, as well as overall mentorship of faculty around their research initiatives. Enhance research infrastructure and operational excellence. As the School's research enterprise continues to evolve, the next Vice Dean will have the opportunity to strengthen the systems, resources, and support structures that enable faculty success. By providing strategic leadership for the Office of Scholarship and Research, the Vice Dean will ensure that research administration, data resources, and faculty support services effectively meet the needs of a dynamic and growing scholarly community. Foster a culture of research excellence, collaboration, and faculty success. The next Vice Dean will help cultivate an environment in which faculty, trainees, and research staff can thrive. By strengthening mentorship, supporting career development and advancement, and promoting a collaborative culture of discovery and innovation, the Vice Dean will build on the School's tradition of excellence while helping to attract, develop, and retain outstanding scholars. The successful candidate for the Vice Dean of Research and Scholarship at Columbia University School of Nursing will possess: •An earned Ph.D, Ed.D, Sc.D, or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field. •Credentials commensurate with appointment as a tenured full professor at Columbia University. •An internationally recognized record of scholarly achievement and scientific leadership, including a sustained history of impactful research and publication. •A strong record of securing and leading externally funded research programs, including significant federal, foundation, and/or industry support. •Demonstrated success leading complex research enterprises, academic units, centers, institutes, or interdisciplinary programs. •The ability to develop and execute a compelling vision for research and scholarship that advances scientific excellence, innovation, and improved health outcomes. •A demonstrated commitment to mentorship, faculty development, and supporting facultysuccess across all career stages while cultivating the next generation of nurse scientists andacademic leaders. •Experience fostering and supporting interdisciplinary collaboration across academic, clinical,community, and research settings. •Success in building collaborative relationships and productive partnerships across diverseinternal and external constituencies, including faculty, students, postdoctoral fellows, researchstaff, university leadership, healthcare organizations, funding agencies, and communitypartners. •The ability to lead and strengthen research infrastructure, including research administration,data resources, and faculty support systems that enable scholarly productivity and growth. •Demonstrated skill in navigating complex organizational environments and achieving resultsthrough collaboration, influence, and consensus-building. •A commitment to advancing research that addresses society's most pressing health challenges,improves health outcomes, and informs practice and policy. •Familiarity with artificial intelligence and its integration into nursing academics and research. •A demonstrated commitment to fostering an inclusive, supportive, and high-performingacademic community where faculty, trainees, and staff can thrive and contribute their bestwork. •Exceptional communication, relationship-building, and leadership skills, with the ability to serveas an effective advocate for the School's research mission within Columbia University andnationally. •Strategic, collaborative, and intellectually curious leadership characterized by integrity, soundjudgment, transparency, and a commitment to excellence. Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search process. Inquiries, nominations, referrals, and CVs with cover letters should be sent via the Isaacson, Miller website: https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/columbia-university-school-nursing/vice-dean-research-and-scholarship. Electronic submission of materials is strongly encouraged."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195248": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor and Chair, Department of Epidemiology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Epidemiology Location: 02 The Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health is seeking applications and nominations for the position of Anna Cheskis Gelman and Murray Charles Gelman Professor and Chair of Epidemiology. The ideal candidate will have a national and global reputation as a top scientist, a leader in epidemiology, a distinguished educator, and a proven administrator and manager. This individual will possess a deep commitment to producing world-class science with real-world impact and training the next generation of epidemiology leaders in research and practice. Reporting directly to the Dean of the School and serving as a key member of the School’s leadership team, the Chair will set a forward-looking and innovative vision for the Department of Epidemiology, leveraging its historic strengths and pioneering research to address contemporary challenges in public health while at the same time working to prepare and adapt to the future of the field and the scientific and practical needs of the public. The Chair will play a primary role in supporting and expanding a dynamic faculty, many of whom are recognized as leaders or emerging leaders in epidemiology and related fields. This role will also involve fostering innovative educational programs that prepare students to navigate and lead in the intricate landscape of science, public and community health, healthcare, government, and industry. The successful candidate will have a proven track record of academic achievement and reputation, substantial extramural grant support, and significant impact within public health. They will bring expertise in addressing both current and emerging challenges in epidemiology, a clear vision for future advancements, and the ability to quickly cultivate new partnerships. Demonstrable leadership and management capabilities also are essential. DEPARTMENT OF EPIDEMIOLOGY Columbia’s Department of Epidemiology is committed to producing world-class science with real-world impact while training the next generation of epidemiology leaders. As one of the oldest and largest departments of its kind, its scientists, teachers, and trainees address major public health challenges including infectious diseases and pandemics, cancer and chronic disease, gun violence and injury, opioid and substance use, mental health, aging, and the social and environmental conditions that shape health and longevity. The Department includes 53 full-time faculty and 52 part-time affiliate and adjunct faculty, for a total of 105 faculty in these categories. It also includes approximately 300 employees across faculty, administrative, and research roles, including primary full-time, primary part-time, secondary, and jointly appointed faculty. Department faculty include elected members of the U.S. National Academy of Medicine, fellows of the Royal Society, Lasker Award recipients, and members of the American Epidemiological Society. The Department has a substantial sponsored-research enterprise, with direct financial responsibility for more than 100 extramurally funded projects. Department faculty publish nearly 1,000 scholarly articles each year and conduct research through a worldwide network spanning more than 60 countries. Their work has informed public policy, clinical and public health practice, national and international guidance, and scientific discourse. A defining feature of the Department is its extensive collaboration across Columbia University. Faculty hold joint appointments and work closely with departments throughout the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons, as well as the New York State Psychiatric Institute, the Gertrude H. Sergievsky Center, the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, Columbia Law School, Teachers College, Columbia Engineering, the School of Social Work, Columbia Global Centers, and other University units. Faculty and graduates also maintain strong connections with the New York City Department of Health and Mental Hygiene, the New York State Department of Health, and public health agencies around the nation and the world. ROLE OF THE CHAIR The next Chair of the Department of Epidemiology will have the opportunity to lead a large, highly accomplished, and globally connected department at a time of rapid change in science, public health, higher education, and research funding. The Chair will provide strategic, academic, and administrative leadership for a department whose faculty, students, staff, and programs contribute to public health knowledge and practice locally, nationally, and globally. As the Department’s academic leader, the Chair will set and advance a compelling vision for the future of epidemiology at Columbia. The Chair will support and develop outstanding faculty, strengthen the research enterprise, promote innovation in education and training, foster a collaborative and inclusive departmental culture, and ensure that students and trainees receive rigorous preparation for leadership in research and practice. The Chair will oversee departmental operations and resources, including budgets, sponsored research, personnel, space, and strategic investments, while ensuring compliance with institutional and funding requirements. The Chair will recruit, retain, mentor, and develop faculty and staff; provide leadership and oversight for faculty advancement and promotion processes, including supporting early-stage investigators and guiding faculty progressing from Associate Professor to Full Professor; support principal investigators in securing competitive funding; strengthen interdisciplinary and interprofessional activities across Columbia; and cultivate partnerships with public agencies, communities, funders, alumni, and external organizations. Given the size and career-stage composition of the Department, the Chair will play an important role in ensuring that faculty receive appropriate mentorship, guidance, and support throughout the promotion process. KEY OPPORTUNITIES AND CHALLENGES FOR THE NEXT CHAIR Define the next chapter of epidemiologic science. The Department’s breadth creates an opportunity for the next Chair to articulate a forward-looking scientific vision that builds on longstanding strengths while anticipating emerging public health needs, new methodologies, evolving data environments, and opportunities for intervention and implementation. The Chair will help connect scientific discovery with application and ensure that the Department remains responsive to changing health challenges locally and globally. Strengthen interdisciplinary and cross-institutional collaboration. Epidemiology faculty are deeply connected across Columbia University, CUIMC, public agencies, research centers, and global partners. The next Chair will have the opportunity to deepen these relationships, create new interdisciplinary partnerships, and strengthen translational research and training that bridge population health, clinical care, policy, community engagement, and implementation. Sustain and diversify the research enterprise. The Department manages a large and complex portfolio of externally funded research. In an evolving funding environment, the next Chair will support faculty success in securing competitive federal funding while identifying opportunities with foundations, philanthropy, industry, and other mission-aligned partners. The Chair will be expected to steward resources carefully, support innovation, and maintain a strong and sustainable operating position. Advance excellence in education and training. With 340 enrolled students across MPH, MS, online MS, DrPH, and PhD programs, as well as numerous doctoral and postdoctoral training initiatives, the Department has a substantial educational mission. The next Chair will support curricular innovation, rigorous methodological training, strong mentorship, and pathways that prepare graduates to lead across academia, public health practice, healthcare, government, industry, and community settings. Recruit, develop, and support an exceptional faculty and staff community. The next Chair will play a central role in recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining outstanding faculty and staff while fostering a culture of collaboration, inclusion, transparency, intellectual curiosity, and academic excellence. The Chair will support faculty across career stages, strengthen leadership development, provide clear feedback and expectations, and ensure that the Department’s people and organizational structures are positioned for continued success. The successful candidate for the Anna Cheskis Gelman and Murray Charles Gelman Professor and Chair of Epidemiology will possess: A PhD, MD, and/or equivalent terminal degree, with scholarly credentials commensurate with appointment as a tenured full professor at Columbia University; National and international stature in epidemiology or in a field with a substantial epidemiologic focus, with an exceptional record of scientific achievement and scholarly impact. A strong record of securing and leading research and supporting the growth of a rigorous and impactful research enterprise; A demonstrated commitment to interdisciplinary and translational public health science and the ability to bridge research, practice, policy, healthcare, government, industry, and communities; The vision and strategic judgment to articulate and implement a compelling future direction for the Department while aligning departmental priorities with those of Columbia Mailman, CUIMC, and Columbia University; Demonstrated administrative and management capability, or clear leadership potential to oversee a large and complex department with substantial faculty, staff, student, financial, and sponsored-research activity; A distinguished record as an educator and mentor, with a commitment to preparing the next generation of epidemiology leaders across master’s, doctoral, and postdoctoral programs; Demonstrated success recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining outstanding faculty and staff and supporting career success across stages; A collaborative, transparent, and communicative leadership style characterized by integrity, sound judgment, self-awareness, decisiveness, and respect; Demonstrated skill in building consensus, navigating complex organizational environments, and addressing challenges constructively and effectively; Strong financial and organizational leadership abilities, including experience with budgets, sponsored research, personnel, space, strategic initiatives, and compliance; Exceptional relationship-building skills and the ability to cultivate productive partnerships across disciplines, departments, centers, universities, public agencies, communities, funders, and other external organizations; A commitment to ethical research and financial practices and to creating an environment that supports excellence, inclusion, mentorship, innovation, and shared purpose; Outstanding written and verbal communication skills and the ability to represent the Department effectively to academic, public, philanthropic, and community audiences. Applicants should submit their application materials through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting website by November 2, 2026. The search will continue until the position is filled. Questions, confidential inquiries, and nominations may be sent to msphexecsearch@cumc.columbia.edu. Nominations should include the nominee’s name, current title and institution, and, when available, contact information and a brief statement regarding the nomination."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195259": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor and Chair,  Department of Health Policy and Management",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Health Policy and Management Location: 02 Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health seeks an accomplished scholar, strategic leader, and collaborative partner to serve as the next Thomas Campbell Jackson and Pamela Brandt Jackson Chair of the Department of Health Policy and Management. The successful candidate will build upon the Department's longstanding reputation for excellence in research, education, and policy impact while helping shape its next chapter of growth and influence. This is an opportunity to lead one of the nation's premier departments of health policy and management at a moment when healthcare systems, public health institutions, and policymakers are confronting increasingly complex challenges that demand rigorous scholarship, innovative thinking, and practical solutions. Reporting directly to the Dean and serving as a key member of the School's leadership team, the Chair will provide strategic, academic, and administrative leadership for the Department. Building on its historic strengths and national reputation, the Chair will articulate a compelling vision for the future, support and expand a distinguished faculty, foster innovative educational programs, and strengthen the Department's contributions to research, policy, and practice. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH POLICY AND MANAGEMENT The Department of Health Policy and Management (HPM) was founded in 1945, at a time when the nation was rapidly expanding and modernizing its hospital infrastructure. Since then, the Department has grown into one of the nation's leading departments of health policy and management, with 25 full-time faculty, approximately 63 affiliated faculty, and nearly 400 students. The Department's mission is to advance better, fairer, and more effective approaches to protecting and improving health, grounded in the belief that policymakers must understand the implementation challenges facing health systems and that managers must be equipped to navigate an increasingly complex policy environment. Through a collaborative and collegial culture, a multidisciplinary faculty that includes many social scientists, and innovative educational programs, HPM seeks to generate prominent scholarship, develop future leaders, and inform policy and practice. HPM is recognized for its leadership in health policy, health services research, health economics, health management, and public health systems. The Department's multidisciplinary faculty examines the structures, incentives, organizations, and policies that shape health and healthcare delivery while advancing practical solutions to complex challenges facing health systems and populations. A defining characteristic of the Department is its longstanding connection to the world of policy and practice. Faculty research frequently informs public decision-making, and many faculty members have held leadership roles in government, chaired studies for the National Academy of Medicine, and participated in influential policy networks. Education has been a defining strength of the Department of Health Policy and Management since its founding in 1945 and remains central to its mission. Today, the Department educates nearly 400 students across its Master of Public Health (MPH) and Master of Health Administration (MHA) programs, supported by 25 full-time faculty and approximately 63 affiliated faculty. ROLE OF THE CHAIR The Chair of the Department of Health Policy and Management serves as the Department's chief academic and administrative officer, providing strategic leadership across its research, educational, and service missions. Reporting directly to the Dean of the Mailman School of Public Health, the Chair is a key member of the School's leadership team and will be responsible for sustaining and advancing one of the nation's most respected departments of health policy and management. The Chair will articulate a compelling vision for the Department's future, building on its longstanding strengths while identifying new opportunities for growth, innovation, and impact in research, education, policy, and practice. Working in close partnership with faculty, students, staff, and School leaders, the Chair will foster a collaborative and inclusive culture that supports scholarly excellence, mentorship, and professional development. The Chair will recruit, develop, and retain outstanding faculty, champion the success of principal investigators and research programs, and ensure that the Department remains an attractive home for leading scholars across the disciplines that shape health policy, health systems, management, and public health. The Chair will also oversee the Department's educational portfolio, strengthening its nationally recognized degree programs while encouraging innovation in teaching and training that prepares future leaders to address complex challenges across the healthcare and public health sectors. The Chair will guide the Department's overall operations and financial stewardship, ensuring alignment of resources with strategic priorities and maintaining a strong foundation for continued growth. This includes overseeing budgeting, workforce planning, space and infrastructure needs, and the effective deployment of resources to support research and educational excellence. KEY OPPORTUNITIES AND CHALLENGES FOR THE NEXT CHAIR Shape the next chapter of a national leader in health policy and management For nearly eight decades, HPM has been a leader in health policy and management scholarship, education, and public engagement. The next Chair will have the opportunity to articulate and execute a compelling agenda that connects scientific inquiry with real-world application while positioning the Department to address emerging challenges in healthcare delivery, health systems, management, financing, and population health. Expand the Department's research enterprise and scholarly impact HPM is home to a distinguished multidisciplinary faculty whose scholarship informs policy, management, and practice at local, national, and global levels. The next Chair will have the opportunity to strengthen and grow the Department's research enterprise by supporting faculty success, fostering interdisciplinary and interprofessional collaboration, advancing innovative areas of inquiry, and expanding extramural funding. Recruit, develop, and support the next generation of faculty leaders The Department's success is rooted in the strength of its faculty, staff, and culture. The next Chair will have the opportunity to recruit exceptional scholars and educators, strengthen mentorship and professional development efforts, and support faculty and staff across all career stages. Enhance educational innovation and leadership development Education is central to HPM's mission and impact. The next Chair will have the opportunity to build upon the Department's highly regarded MPH and MHA programs, experiential learning opportunities, and expanding portfolio of educational offerings. The successful candidate for the Thomas Campbell Jackson and Pamela Brandt Jackson Chair of Health Policy and Management will possess: A PhD, MD-PhD, or equivalent terminal degree, with credentials and accomplishments commensurate with appointment as a tenured professor at Columbia University; A distinguished record of scholarly achievement and intellectual leadership in health policy, health systems, health economics, healthcare financing, health services research, healthcare management, public health, or a closely related field, with demonstrated contributions that have advanced the field through influential scholarship and national or international engagement; A demonstrated ability to foster a thriving research enterprise by leading an independent research program and/or securing external funding from federal or foundation sources. A compelling research vision that combines scholarly rigor with practical relevance, embraces a range of disciplinary perspectives and methodological approaches, and addresses both current and emerging challenges in health policy, healthcare delivery, management, and population health; A demonstrated commitment to educational excellence, including success as a teacher, mentor, and academic leader, and a passion for preparing future leaders in healthcare, public health, policy, and management; Significant leadership experience directing academic departments, centers, institutes, research programs, or other complex organizations; A record of fostering interdisciplinary collaboration and building productive partnerships across departments, schools, sectors, and external organizations; Demonstrated success recruiting, developing, mentoring, and retaining outstanding faculty and staff across career stages; Strong administrative and organizational leadership skills, including experience with strategic planning, budgeting, resource allocation, and stewardship of academic enterprises; A collaborative, transparent, and inclusive leadership style characterized by integrity, sound judgment, accountability, and the ability to build consensus in complex environments; Exceptional interpersonal, communication, and relationship-building skills, along with the political acumen and emotional intelligence to engage diverse stakeholders effectively; and A commitment to advancing the Department's mission and translating scholarship into meaningful impact on policy, practice, and population health. Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search process. Inquiries, nominations, referrals, and CVs with cover letters should be sent via the Isaacson, Miller website: https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/columbia-university-mailman-school-public-health/department-chair-health-policy-and Electronic submission of materials is strongly encouraged. Stephanie Fidel, Partner Jasmine Miller, Senior Associate Maria Peña, Senior Search Coordinator Isaacson, Miller"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195281": {
      "title": "Curator, Avery Classics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: University Libraries Location: 01 The Avery Architectural & Fine Arts Library is eager to recruit a new Curator, Avery Classics. Reporting to the Avery Director, the Curator of Avery Classics provides leadership for the rare book collection, overseeing core collection-related activities at the highest professional level. The incumbent will develop and implement acquisition strategies, support course-integrated instruction, and collaborate with scholars from around the world. The position also manages departmental staff and oversees conservation, cataloging, and digitization initiatives that expand access to and preserve Avery’s collections. Key responsibilities include: Collection Development and Acquisitions Identify and pursue new collecting areas With supervisory support, develop and lead a rare book collecting program focused on architectural design and practice, urban planning, landscape design, and the allied arts. Align collecting activities with Avery’s overall collection policies and priorities. Manage and monitor expenditures within designated collection budgets. Outreach, Instruction, and Research Represent the department within the greater architectural and library communities through presentations, publications, and at professional conferences Develop an instructional program for Classics supporting the teaching and research programs of the Department of Art History and Archaeology and the Graduate School of Architecture, Planning and Preservation (GSAPP) and the In collaboration with the Avery Director, respond to exhibition and loan requests, including review of proposals and selection of works, assignment of insurance values,  and preparation of objects for loans Build partnerships with museums, foundations, libraries, and the international architectural community. In collaboration with Avery and other Columbia University Libraries staff, and faculty develop public programs and events that promote the Classics collections and highlight the contemporary relevance of the collection’s holdings Supervision and Staff Development Supervise and support the professional development of unit staff Collections Management & Patron Services In collaboration with the Avery Director, set priorities for cataloging; care, storage, and conservation of materials; and digital projects Respond to patron inquiries and provide research assistance and support Professional Engagement Participate in Columbia University Libraries and external professional committees and other professional development activities. Minimum Qualifications: Graduate degree in relevant field Expertise in architectural history and theory Thorough knowledge of rare books and preservation practices Strong organizational skills, excellent collaboration and communication skills Preferred: MLIS or PhD preferred Record of professional engagement with the field (publication record, presentations, professional society appointments, etc.) Familiarity with digital project development and management, digitization and web technologies For serious consideration, please apply online with your current CV and a letter of interest at: https://academic.careers.columbia.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195342": {
      "title": "Open Rank - Senior Staff Associate I and/or Senior Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Climate School Location: 01 The Columbia Center on Sustainable Investment (CCSI) in the Columbia Climate School invites applications for Senior Staff Associate I or Senior Staff Associate II. We need better systems: energy, industry, transport, buildings, food, water, and nature that are cheaper, more reliable, more secure, more flexible, and more accessible. Systems that are better in those ways are inherently cleaner, and they last, because their case never rested on climate policy. The technology and the capital to build them largely exist. What is missing is the layer in between: a rigorous picture of what the better system looks like, and the plans, rules, institutions, and financing structures that let it get built. CCSI works at that layer. We start from the outcome, diagnose the specific structural barrier holding a system back, convene the institutions that hold its parts, and design the structure that makes it financeable. We can do this because of who we are: a university-based center with no client and no product to sell; able to hold the legal, technical, and financial faces of a problem together; and trusted across the institutions that build these systems, from prime ministers and finance ministries to development banks, regulators, and the largest companies in energy, industry, and property. As a Climate School center at Columbia University with roots at the Law School, working alongside engineers, economists, and financial practitioners, we hold the legal, technical, and financial faces of a problem together. That is where these problems live and where single-discipline institutions fall between. It also means we have no client, no product, and no position to defend. The position will work closely with the Director and CCSI staff to advance the Center’s applied research agenda in Asia, with a focus on sustainable energy, development and climate finance, emerging-market investment, and systems transformation. The ideal candidate will have a strong applied research background and experience working across energy, economic, financial, and/or food systems. They will be able to analyze complex, interconnected challenges and translate research into actionable strategies, policy recommendations, and practical solutions. Key responsibilities will include: Lead and advance CCSI’s work in Asia, identifying opportunities for impact, shaping the regional research agenda, and developing relationships with key stakeholders. Conduct interdisciplinary research and analysis on sustainable energy finance, emerging-market investment, derisking, and other barriers to systems transformation. Develop actionable strategies and policy recommendations, including on regional initiatives such as ASEAN energy systems integration and mechanisms to reduce financing and policy barriers. Build and maintain relationships with policymakers, multilateral institutions, development banks, credit rating agencies, private-sector stakeholders, and other regional partners. Organize and facilitate bilateral meetings, working groups, workshops, consultations, and other stakeholder engagements. Manage and lead research and advisory projects, including defining research approaches, managing timelines, coordinating collaborators, and delivering high-quality outputs. Produce reports, roadmaps, policy briefs, and other materials for decision-makers and stakeholders. Contribute to capacity-building and knowledge sharing on development and climate finance, including tools and frameworks to strengthen the creditworthiness of emerging-market projects and sovereigns. Collaborate across CCSI to support cross-learning, coordination, and strategic synergies. The position will require a high degree of independent judgment, initiative, and adaptability. The incumbent will work under limited supervision on complex assignments and will be expected to identify opportunities, develop and execute research projects, manage stakeholder relationships, and contribute to CCSI’s strategic priorities in Asia. At the Senior Staff Associate I level, the candidate will independently manage complex assignments, contribute to CCSI’s regional work, develop stakeholder relationships, and provide guidance to junior staff and student researchers. At the Senior Staff Associate II level, the candidate will operate with greater autonomy and strategic responsibility, substantially lead CCSI’s work in Asia, originate and lead complex projects, cultivate senior-level relationships, shape the regional research agenda, and supervise mid-level and junior research staff. Lead and advance CCSI’s work in Asia, identifying opportunities for impact, shaping the regional research agenda, and developing relationships with key stakeholders. Minimum Qualifications: The minimum requirement for Senior Staff Associate I is a Bachelor’s degree and 6 years of experience. The minimum requirement for Senior Staff Associate II is a Bachelor’s degree and 10 years of experience. Good understanding of how sectors, stakeholders, functions, institutions, and disciplines interact to shape a system towards sustainable development outcomes. Ability to grasp the complexity of economic ecosystems and the challenges involved in achieving change, and the ability to think strategically. Experience working with governments, international organizations, financial institutions, private sector actors, and other relevant stakeholders in the field of sustainable finance and sustainable energy Experience convening and facilitating multi-stakeholder consultations and convenings, with a view to advancing a specific agenda related to research or action. A strong ambition to learn new areas and to work in a highly interdisciplinary, flexible, and dynamic environment Proven track record of driving high-level changes in systems, laws, policies, and behaviors through original analysis, applied research, convening, speaking engagements, and media outreach. Experience working in a highly interdisciplinary, flexible, and dynamic environment Excellent interpersonal, organizational, research, and communication skills Preferred Qualification: Additional experience and a Master’s degree are preferred Existing network with relationships of influence in key global, regional, and national institutions, particularly in Asia and with the African Union, European Union, World Trade Organization and/or World Bank Logistical Requirements: Must be willing and able to travel frequently This position requires a physical base in New York City Salary Range or Pay Grade: Salary range for Senior Staff Associate I is $90,000 - $105,000, commensurate with experience. Salary range for Senior Staff Associate II is $105,000 - $120,000, commensurate with experience. The search will remain open for at least 30 days after the ad appears and will continue until the position is filled. Appointments are made on a fiscal year basis and are eligible for renewal each July 1, contingent upon performance and funding. Columbia University benefits accompany this Officer of Research appointment. Application Instructions We accept online applications only Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity Employer / Disability / Veteran Pay Transparency Disclosure The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training.  The above hiring range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195299": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Physiology Location: 02 The laboratory of Dr. Andrew R. Marks, Chair, Department of Physiology and Cellular Biophysics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center, is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Scientist. Projects include: cryo-EM structures of RyR channels (with Nobel Laureate Joachim Frank and Wayne Hendrickson), calcium signaling in diabetes, molecular basis of inherited cardiomyopathies and heart failure, and calcium signaling in neurodegenerative diseases, drug discovery efforts to test novel therapeutics for heart, muscle, metabolic and CNS disorders. We are translating fundamental discoveries into ongoing clinical trials. Recent laboratory members have gone on to leading academic and pharmaceutical industry positions. All applicants should have a PhD or MD/PhD, outstanding publications, oral and written communication skills, organizational skills, work ethic, the ability to work independently, and experience in structural biology, molecular biology, calcium signaling, or electrophysiology."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195300": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Physiology Location: 02 The laboratory of Dr. Andrew R. Marks, Chair, Department of Physiology and Cellular Biophysics at Columbia University Irving Medical Center, is seeking an Associate Research Scientist to lead projects in investigating the role of the ryanodine receptor (RyR), a calcium channel, in normal and pathological physiology. Abnormal calcium handling has been implicated in several diseases, and one of the candidate’s primary duties will be to investigate the role of RyR in pathophysiology using several unique animal models.  The Associate Research Scientist will lead the lab’s effort to study cardiac and brain function and physiology in these animal models. Responsibilities will include performing echocardiography, ECGs, and LTP measurements in animal models, investigating protein biochemistry and purification, analyzing protein-protein interactions, studying intracellular calcium handling in isolated cells, as well as single channel analysis of the ryanodine receptor isolated from normal and diseased tissue.  The Associate Research Scientist position will also be responsible for overseeing the work of more junior research staff and may direct the implementation of laboratory processes/protocols. We are translating fundamental discoveries into ongoing clinical trials. Recent laboratory members have gone on to leading academic and pharmaceutical industry positions. All applicants should have a PhD or MD/PhD, outstanding publications, oral and written communication skills, organizational skills, work ethic, the ability to work independently, and experience in molecular biology, biochemistry, calcium signaling, protein purification, electrophysiology, and/or animal studies including heart failure models."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195477": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Interdisciplinary: Interdisciplinary Center for Innovative Theory and Empirics (INCITE) Location: 01 The Interdisciplinary Center for Innovative Theory and Empirics (Incite) at Columbia University invites applications for a Staff Associate position to assist the Incite Director and other professional officers of research in the conduct of research projects at Incite. This individual will carry out assigned duties in the design of and capture of data, the analysis of data, and will manage workflow and support those projects at Incite that employ statistical analysis of large-scale, individual-level data. The previous Incite SAI was assigned to the Obama Presidency Oral History project. Upcoming projects for the current SAI may include a project focusing on Biden’s economic policies, a review of the war in Afghanistan project and a project on the legacy of George Soros, among other projects. For example, the following projects are included in Incite’s active portfolio: Movements Against Mass Incarceration Lab, a Mellon Foundation-funded project that has partnered with five non-profits to captures stories of justice impacted people throughout the United States; and the Harriman Oral History Project which assesses the impact of Columbia’s Harriman Institute specializes in Eurasia studies; and the Leveraging of Urban AI as a Communal Tool for Connection and Exchange in Harlem Project supported by the National Science Foundation on the structures and dynamics of AI on a community level; and digital workflows and communicating research to the public for Incite. The position will require some independence, with the Staff Associate working both independently and as part of a team. The incumbent will be supervised to the extent required to ensure adequate performance, but typically there will not be consistent daily supervision. Rather, the Staff Associate, the Director, the ARS and the rest of the team will meet weekly and will communicate as needed throughout each week. The incumbents should be able to work with some degree of independence in research activities, applying independent judgment to routine procedures, tracking deadlines, using discretion, and following protocols designed by the Principal Investigator. The incumbent must be able to identify how best to use their skills to complete the day-to-day operations and perform the other responsibilities associated with the position described above. Specific Duties will Include: --Supporting the data collection, processing, cleaning, statistical analysis, and reporting on various large-scale data Incite projects. – 35% --Managing workflow by attending and organizing research team meetings, assisting in setting research agenda, assisting in identifying key research tasks to be performed by team members, communicating and assigning tasks as needed, and ensuring project research deliverables meet their timelines. — 30% --Crafting literature reviews and supporting the development of other research projects at Incite on an as-needed basis. – 20% --Providing other research support as appropriate to meet project deliverables, including supporting a collective of researchers seeking to build a data core on various projects. – 15% BA- Social Science or Computer Science All applications must be submitted through Columbia's online Academic Search and Recruiting portal (ASR) and must include the following materials: CV and cover letter."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195479": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pathology Location: New York, NY The laboratory of Dr. Minah Kim in the Department of Pathology and Cell Biology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites applications for one full-time Postdoctoral Research Scientist position. Candidates with expertise in either neuroscience and neuroimmunology or cancer immunology and tumor vascular biology will be considered, with the research focus determined by the selected candidate’s expertise and interests. The project will investigate either brain endothelial dysfunction, blood–brain barrier disruption, and neuroinflammation in Alzheimer’s disease, or how tumor blood vessels shape immune evasion in the tumor microenvironment. Working with Dr. Kim, lab members, and collaborators, the postdoctoral scientist will lead mechanistic studies and integrate experimental findings with relevant human specimens and molecular datasets. We seek a motivated, rigorous, and collaborative scientist with strong analytical and communication skills. Job Function : Design and conduct independent research on vascular mechanisms in Alzheimer’s disease or cancer, in consultation with Dr. Kim. Investigate how brain endothelial dysfunction affects blood–brain barrier integrity and neuroinflammation, or how tumor endothelial cells regulate immune cell recruitment, localization, function, and immune evasion. Use appropriate experimental models, including mouse models, cell culture systems, and human specimens, to test mechanistic hypotheses. Perform molecular, cellular, imaging, and immunological analyses relevant to the project, including flow cytometry, fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS), and functional immune assays where applicable. Analyze and interpret experimental data and, independently or with collaborators, integrate RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and spatial transcriptomics datasets to characterize vascular and immune cell states and interactions. Lab Mission: The Kim Lab studies how blood vessels regulate tissue function and disease through interactions with immune cells and the surrounding microenvironment. Research in neuroscience focuses on brain endothelial dysfunction, blood–brain barrier disruption, neuroinflammation, and Alzheimer’s disease. Research in cancer focuses on how tumor blood vessels promote immune exclusion and immune evasion, and how vascular pathways may be targeted to improve antitumor immunity. The lab integrates mechanistic experiments with human specimens and transcriptomic approaches to identify disease-relevant vascular and immune pathways. Lab website: www.minahkimlab.com Minimum Education Requirements: A PhD, MD, or equivalent doctoral degree in neuroscience, immunology, cancer biology, vascular biology, biomedical sciences, or a related discipline, completed by the appointment start date. Preferred Qualifications: Candidates should have strengths relevant to their intended research area; expertise in both areas is not required. Neuroscience and neuroimmunology: Experience studying Alzheimer’s disease, neurodegeneration, neuroinflammation, blood–brain barrier function, or cerebrovascular biology. Experience with relevant mouse models, endothelial cell systems, imaging, or human tissue analysis is desirable. Cancer immunology and tumor vascular biology: A strong immunology background with hands-on experience in multiple immunological assays, particularly multiparameter flow cytometry, FACS, and functional immune cell assays. Experience investigating antitumor immunity, immune evasion, or interactions between endothelial and immune cells is desirable. Relevant to either research area: Experience analyzing single-cell RNA-seq, spatial transcriptomics, or bulk RNA-seq data and integrating these analyses with experimental studies. Evidence of research productivity through publications is also preferred. Minimum Skills Required: Demonstrated research experience in neuroscience, neuroimmunology, immunology, cancer biology, vascular biology, or a related field. Ability to design experiments, evaluate results critically, troubleshoot methods, and conduct research with appropriate guidance. Experience with molecular, cellular, or immunological methods relevant to the proposed research area, and competence in quantitative data analysis. Strong scientific writing, communication, organization, and collaborative skills. Hiring Salary Plan: Postdoctoral Research Scientist: $72,116- $72,116"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195502": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist - Burdick Lab",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 The Burdick Lab within the Department of Psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center seeks a highly qualified Postdoctoral Research Scientist wishing to pursue neurocognitive and neuroimaging research in major psychiatric disorders. Our current work is focused on identifying persistent cognitive deficits in major psychiatric disorders, understanding their etiologies, and directly targeting them with treatment. Primary ongoing studies in patients with bipolar disorder and major depressive disorder focus on neurocognition and social cognition and their relationship with everyday functioning. The postdoctoral researcher will analyze existing data and assist with quality control (QC) and the preprocessing of analytic pipelines. Responsibilities will include preparing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals, presenting research findings and engaging in scientific communication at conferences, and assisting with the training and supervision of junior laboratory members. The postdoctoral researcher will also prepare documentation for regulatory review of protocols they lead, contribute to the preparation and submission of grant application materials for projects they will directly lead or for which they will receive direct funding, and conduct diagnostic assessments and clinical ratings with study participants. The postdoctoral researcher will also perform other duties as assigned. At CUIMC, we stand together because diverse experiences, perspective, and values enrich every dimension of our work. Join our team and see how your unique skills and experiences can create a real impact by changing lives. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in psychology, neuropsychology, neuroscience, or a related field Preferred Qualifications: Strong training in statistical and experimental methods A track record of presentation and publication Ability to work independently and collaboratively Strong scientific curiosity, quantitative thinking, and attention to detail Good written and oral communication skills Hiring Salary Range: $72,116.00-$72,116.00."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195549": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Stem Cell Initiative Location: 02 The primary function of this position is to conduct translational biomedical research using genetically engineered mouse models, organoid cultures, histology, and omics analyses. Responsibilities include performing and analyzing experiments, troubleshooting technical challenges, interpreting data, and optimizing research protocols. The Staff Associate II will also support project management and inter-laboratory collaborations, applying scientific judgment and technical expertise to advance research projects. Responsibilities: Functional Knowledge Apply knowledge of regenerative medicine, in vitro organoid and in vivo animal models for translational studies. Integrate experimental findings for mechanistic discoveries and therapeutic evaluations (20%) Problem Solving Troubleshoot and optimize organoid cultures, tissue processing, histologic assays, and related experimental protocols in moderately complex situations. Analyze experimental results to identify technical limitations and recommend protocol modifications or follow-up experiments (20%) Decision Making/Autonomy Conduct assigned research activities independently and collaboratively, including planning experimental workflows, selecting appropriate controls, and adapting protocols based on experimental outcomes. Exercise scientific judgment in prioritizing experiments and coordinating interconnected organoid, animal, and tissuebased studies (20%) Technical Expertise Perform organoid culture, animal tissue collection, histologic processing and examination, IHC/IF, molecular assays, and quantitative and omics-based analyses. Develop and optimize experimental methods for disease modeling, drug screening, and evaluation of therapeutic responses (20%) Communication Summarizes and explains experimental procedures and findings to the PI and research team. Coordinate experimental activities, data, and protocols with collaborating laboratories (20%) Bachelor's degree 4 years of experience is recommended Experience with mouse models, cell and organoid culture, molecular and histologic analyses, and interpretation of experimental data. Strong scientific communication and organizational skills, including experience with research presentations, teaching or mentoring, and collaborative research."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195726": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Neurology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 The Department of Neurology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites applications for a faculty position in the Division of Neurocritical Care. The successful candidate will participate in the Division’s clinical training program and contribute to the Department’s clinical, educational, and research missions. The primary focus of the position will be the evaluation, management, and treatment of patients in the Neurocritical Care Unit. The successful candidate will provide clinical services and participate in clinical activities consistent with the needs of the Division and Department. The position may also include teaching, research, and administrative responsibilities as appropriate to the faculty appointment. The successful candidate will be expected to fulfill all clinical and research-related responsibilities associated with the appointment, including the timely completion of patient charts and other clinical documentation, and to complete all required institutional and hospital training and certifications. The candidate will work in a dynamic academic and clinical environment and have opportunities to collaborate with faculty and multidisciplinary teams across Columbia University Irving Medical Center and NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital. The successful candidate will join a highly dedicated community offering exceptional clinical, educational, and research resources. Compensation and academic rank will vary based on credentials, expertise, and Experience. · Minimum degree required: MD or DO · Candidates should possess a demonstrated record as a clinician and should possess excellent patient care, teamwork, and interpersonal skills. · Board certified in Neurology and Neurocritical care · NYS license · DEA license · Able to meet New York Presbyterian Hospital-Medical Staff Office credentialing requirements · NAbility to provide high-quality clinical care in an academic medical center environment. · Ability to fulfill clinical, educational, research, and administrative responsibilities associated with the faculty appointment. · Ability to complete required clinical documentation in a timely and accurate manner."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195736": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Modern US History",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of History Location: 01 The Department of History, Columbia University, invites applications for an assistant professor specializing in the political, intellectual, and/or economic history of the United States in the late 19th and/or 20 th century. Teaching responsibilities will include graduate and undergraduate courses and involvement in Columbia’s Core Curriculum. The successful applicant will show evidence of innovative scholarship and experience with teaching and advising and have the Ph.D. in hand by 1 July 2027. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action employer. A Ph.D. is required at the time of appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: • Cover letter explaining research and teaching interests and plans for future research • Curriculum vitae • Contact information for three referees (who may be asked to upload letters to the ASR/Interfolio system at a later stage in the search process, after candidates are shortlisted) For further information, please contact Ramy Andil at facultyaffairs-history@columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/176356": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Columbia Aging Center Location: 02 The Columbia University, Robert N. Butler Columbia Aging Center is seeking an Associate Research Scientist to aid in statistical analysis and day-to-day data management associated with multiple concurrent research projects of the Aiello Research Group and Zhang Research Group. The Aiello Research Group seeks to understand how social and biological factors jointly shape long-term health trajectories. Our research examines the influence of socioeconomic position, stress, epigenetic mechanisms, and biomarkers of infection, immune function, and neurodegeneration on aging-related health outcomes, with a focus on Alzheimer’s Disease and related dementias (ADRD). Our ultimate goal is to reduce social disparities in these areas by conducting interdisciplinary, methodologically rigorous research that bridges epidemiology, gerontology, sociology, infectious diseases, immunology, and laboratory science. We strive to produce high-impact, actionable evidence that informs interventions and advances health equity across the life course. Dr. Zhang’s research lies at the intersection of population health, gerontology, biodemography, and economics, focusing on how life course socioeconomic status and participation in social and economic activities contribute to healthy longevity. We use longitudinal cohort studies to investigate how social environments shape life chances for education and work experiences and influence biological health risks, cognitive aging trajectories, dementia risk, and mortality across diverse contexts. Primary Responsibilities Work with the principal investigators, project director, project coordinators, post- and pre-doctoral students, and other collaborators to develop and execute clear, well-documented statistical programs Design and implement statistical analysis and data management plans across data types (e.g. laboratory, genetic, qualitative, and quantitative) Lead data cleaning and dataset creation for projects in various stages of the research process (e.g., proposal/grant writing, pilot testing, data collection, data analysis, data dissemination, and archived) Write statistical-related components (e.g. statistical methods and power analysis sections) for scientific presentations, manuscripts, study protocols, and grant applications; produce tables and figures based on study results Provide statistical consultation and advise on model selection Review and edit statistical programs and results written by colleagues Lead quality assurance and quality control efforts Perform data auditing across multiple projects Maintain understanding of university data security and sharing policies and securely share datasets with collaborators in accordance with established agreements Take initiative to acquire new statistical and/or programming skills as needed -Appointments are made on a fiscal year basis and are eligible for renewal each July 1st, contingent upon performance and funding -The search will remain open for at least 30 days after the ad appears and will continue until the position is filled -Columbia University benefits are offered with this appointment Required Qualifications PhD, ScD or DrPH in social sciences or public health, epidemiology, or related field is preferred Experience in measurement and analysis of biological and social variables in the study of health and aging Strong interpersonal, leadership, and verbal/written communication skills Significant research experience evidenced by peer-reviewed publications 5+ years of experience and expertise in measurement and analysis of biological aging, biomarkers, and/or other areas of epidemiology research Highly proficient in R, SAS, and/or STATA programming software Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated interest in the research topics investigated by the Aiello and Zhang Research Groups"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187004": {
      "title": "General Neurologist - Assistant Professor in Neurology at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 The Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons, Department of Neurology is seeking a full-time General Neurologist to join our Division of General Neurology. Responsibilities include providing outpatient clinical care at one of the ambulatory practice locations, with opportunities for resident and medical student education and clinical research. Subspecialty training is not required but is welcomed. Columbia’s  General Neurology practice is comprised of an integrated team of neurological specialists with particular expertise in the diagnosis and treatment of both common and complex neurological disorders. The successful candidate will join a highly dedicated community offer extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. For more information on the Department of Neurology visit: https://www.neurology.columbia.edu/ MD or MD-PhD Must be board certified in neurology. Candidates should possess a demonstrated record as a clinician and should possess excellent patient care, teamwork, and interpersonal skills. NYS, NJ, and CT medical license Able to meet New York Presbyterian Hospital-Medical Staff Office credentialing requirements Applicants are required to create an applicant profile and upload a CV and Cover Letter in Columbia’s online Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Preferred CV formatting guidelines: https://www.vagelos.columbia.edu/file/41228/download?token=JJuDEmY6"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188232": {
      "title": "Multiple Open-Rank and Open-Track Faculty position(s) - Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, Professor",
      "description": "Posted: Jun 17, 2026 Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Biostatistics Location: 02 The Department of Biostatistics at Columbia University is one of the premier biostatistics departments in the nation. The Department has more than 30 faculty (including 13 ASA Fellows) and 20+ biostatistician research staff members. The Department boasts strong research profiles in clinical trial design, survival analysis, nonparametric and semi-parametric statistics, precision medicine, causal inference, machine learning, functional data analysis, imaging statistics, statistical genetics, statistical methods for mental health, environmental health, aging and many other fields.  The Department has built strong collaborative connections within the Mailman School of Public Health (MSPH), a top-ranked school nationally, and in the world-class Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC).  The Department has thriving education programs leading to M.S., M.P.H., Ph.D., and Dr.P.H. degrees. The Department is at the forefront of advancing health data science in close collaboration with the Columbia Data Science Institute. MSPH is a globally renowned in public health research and education and one of the largest recipients of sponsored research for all schools of public health in the nation. It has six world class departments with over 500 fulltime faculty members, of whom almost 40 percent hold joint or interdisciplinary appointments with other departments of the university, as well as a large network of adjunct faculty. MSPH also features an extensive array of centers and programs, such as ICAP, Center for Infection and Immunity and Robert N. Buter Columbia Aging Center, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration and innovation to advancing public health knowledge and practice. CUIMC includes four schools (Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons, College of Dental Medicine, School of Nursing, and Mailman School of Public Health) and accounts for roughly half of the University's $4 billion annual budget. CUIMC provides world-class leadership in scientific research, health and medical education, and patient care. CUIMC faculty have made profound breakthroughs historically (e.g., the first blood test for cancer, the first medical use of the laser, and the first successful transfer of genes from one cell to another. Responsibilities: Successful candidates will maintain an active research program, both in biostatistical methodology and in collaborative research; teach effectively, both to graduate students in biostatistics and to students from outside the department; mentor graduate students; provide service to the department, the school, the university, and the profession. Competitive candidates will hold a doctoral degree in biostatistics, statistics, or related quantitative field by the start date.  Candidates with statistical and biostatistical expertise in the application of broad biomedical sciences are welcome to apply. Areas of interest include (but not restricted to) deep learning methodologies in health (e.g., medical images analysis, predictive analysis with multimodal data), reinforcement learning for precision intervention development and evaluation, spatio-temporal statistics with environmental applications to extreme weather science, digital and mobile health, network analysis, automated knowledge extraction and transfer, statistical genetics/genomics, methods related to cancer, aging and frailty and precision medicine. Consideration for tenure requires strong teaching, research and publication records in addition to the ability to secure external funding.  Junior faculty candidates should demonstrate clear outstanding potential in research, teaching, and the ability to secure independent funding; senior faculty candidates should be internationally recognized scholars who demonstrate a strong track record of excellence in research, teaching, and mentoring. Assistant Professor Open Track: $140,000 - $160,000 Associate Professor Open Track: $170,00 - $225,000 Professor Open Track: $200,000 - $325,000 Upload a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, statement of research and teaching philosophy, three recommendation letters, and graduate and undergraduate transcripts for expected and recent doctoral graduates (unofficial transcripts acceptable)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188284": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Earth and Environmental Engineering Location: 01 Applications are invited from highly motivated and qualified candidates for the position of Senior Staff Associate II in the research group of Prof. Kartik Chandran, Department of Earth and Environmental Engineering, Columbia University. The successful applicant will ideally have a M.S. (or equivalent) or Ph.D. (or equivalent) on directions closely related to sustainable global sanitation.  The successful applicant will oversee and manage the Global Sanitation Program in the Kartik Chandran Laboratories at Columbia University. Prior experience in a leadership or managerial role in a research laboratory is highly desirable but not required. Specific activities include but are not limited to the following: • Working as the principal point of contact for the Global Sanitation program and ensure the efficient technical growth and progress of the Global Sanitation program through close interactions with the Principal Investigator, Prof. Kartik Chandran, program researchers and administrative and logistics staff at Columbia University • Lead the development and preparation of scheduled research deliverables and reports, in conjunction with undergraduate, graduate and post-doctoral researchers • Organize and coordinate periodic internal meetings within the research group and external meetings with collaborators and program sponsors • Assist the PI, Prof. Chandran towards the development and submission of proposals, applications and publications pertinent to the growth and sustained productivity of the Global Sanitation Program The successful applicant will ideally have a M.S. (or equivalent in education + experience) or Ph.D. (or equivalent in education + experience) on directions closely related to sustainable global sanitation. Desirable qualifications and skills include the following: • Reliable and able to work in a self-guided manner, and care for multiple priorities while meeting demanding timelines • Detail-oriented and able to perform work accurately and thoroughly • Excellent organizational, oral, and written communication skills. • Able to work effectively and with sensitivity across barriers such as language, culture, and distance. • Excellent software skills (Microsoft Suite, organizational and scheduling software related to research productivity, among others), including a demonstrated ability to convey complex information using supporting tables, graphs, and other visual representations. • Ability to travel up to 25% domestically and internationally The position is available immediately for an initial one-year term.  Renewal will be contingent on performance and funding. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Columbia University benefits are offered with this Officer of Research appointment.  Columbia University values diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from members of underrepresented minority groups.  Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity Employer / Disability / Veteran."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190258": {
      "title": "Academic Hospitalist: Instructor/ Assistant Professor/ Associate Professor at the CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - General Medicine Location: 02 The Department of Medicine, Division of General Medicine at the Columbia University Medical Center in New York City is looking to expand our academic hospitalist group at the Milstein Hospital. Duties include teaching of residents and medical students, providing direct patient care with the assistance of physician assistants, and involvement in quality improvements, patient safety initiatives and other academic activities. Successful applicants must have clinical teaching experience in comparable setting and substantive interest in hospital medicine, either as a career path or gap year before subspecialty fellowship. We are recruiting for daytime, nighttime, and hybrid positions; please indicate your interest in one or more of these schedules in your cover letter. We are considering applicants at the Instructor, Assistant and Associate Professor levels. Learn more at https://www.genmed.columbia.edu/hospital-medicine Minimum Degree: M.D. or equivalent medical professional degree Minimum Qualifications: Must be board certified or board eligible in Internal Medicine and must be eligible to practice in the State of New York. Preferred: Spanish proficiency Instructor in Medicine at CUMC: $225,630 Assistant Professor of Medicine at CUMC: $243,225 Associate Professor of Medicine at CUMC: $274,275"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190596": {
      "title": "All Ranks: Assistant/ Associate Professor of Radiology (Breast Imaging)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Radiology Location: 02 The Department of Radiology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC)/NewYork-Presbyterian (NYP) is seeking a full-time faculty radiologist with fellowship training in Breast Imaging to join our growing and highly regarded division. This is an outstanding opportunity to join a nationally recognized academic radiology department known for excellence in clinical care, education, and research. Faculty will provide high-quality breast imaging interpretation and diagnostic intervention across a broad spectrum of cases and modalities while contributing to the department’s academic mission. Compensation and academic rank will vary based on credentials, expertise, and experience. Salary range starts is $480,000-$570,000. The successful candidate will serve several Westchester locations, including comprehensive breast imaging centers at NewYork-Presbyterian Westchester and NewYork-Presbyterian The One , a new multispecialty outpatient location in White Plains that offers breast cancer care and other women’s health specialties. Our ideal candidate has strong interpretative skills in all breast imaging modalities. We value skilled communicators, as our breast imaging faculty collaborate closely with a world-class team of surgeons and oncologists to provide comprehensive care to our patients. Many faculty members also participate in didactic and hands-on training of radiology residents and breast imaging fellows and participate in a range of research projects to advance the field. The Department of Radiology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center has more than 130 full time faculty and performs over one million imaging exams each year in state-of-the-art facilities. A very active and growing research program provides support and guidance for those interested in participating. Our hospital, NewYork-Presbyterian, is ranked as one of the best in the country and New York City. Columbia University is an exciting hub of innovation, with many opportunities to collaborate with other departments and schools. Columbia University offers a highly competitive benefits package, including college tuition and childcare reimbursement, tuition exemption for faculty members who wish to pursue graduate programs, generous retirement plans, and excellent health and dental coverage. Continuing Medical Education/conference days are apportioned by academic rank and are negotiable to fit candidate needs. Position requires M.D. or D.O. Candidate must be board certified or board eligible in Diagnostic Radiology and must be eligible to practice in the State of New York. Fellowship training and experience in breast imaging required. Candidate must be MQSA certifiable. Training and experience in breast imaging required. More information about the department can be found on the Columbia Radiology website . To apply, please email a curriculum vitae and statement of interest to: Katja Pinker-Domenig, MD, PhD Chief, Division of Breast Imaging Columbia University Irving Medical Center NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital kp3124@cumc.columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194785": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/ Full Professor in African Studies",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Middle Eastern, South Asian, and African Studies Location: 01 Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Columbia University Arts & Sciences seeks to fill a position in African Studies, as part of a hiring cluster, to begin July 1 2027, at any rank (Assistant Professor, Associate Professor without tenure, Associate Professor with tenure, or Full Professor). This appointment will be in the Department of Middle Eastern, South Asian, and African Studies, with a possible joint appointment in the Departments of Anthropology, History, or English and Comparative Literature, or a possible affiliation with the Institute for Comparative Literature and Society. We seek candidates with a distinguished record of scholarly inquiry in the humanities or the humanistic social sciences, who will contribute to the broader intellectual community and mission for the study of Africa in and across disciplines. We are particularly interested in scholars whose research draws upon primary sources in African languages other than French, English, and Arabic. Candidates will be expected to sustain an active research and publication profile and teach in the relevant undergraduate and graduate programs. Review of applications will begin on November 21, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, an article-length writing sample, and the names and contact information of three references. Within your cover letter, please discuss your teaching philosophy. Columbia University is an Equal Opportunity Employer / Disability / Veteran. Please visit our online application site at https://dossier.interfolio.com/apply/194785 for further information about this position and to submit your application. PhD Required Application Instructions: All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Please upload the following required materials: A cover letter, curriculum vitae, an article-length writing sample, and the names and contact information of three references. Within your cover letter, please discuss your teaching philosophy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194987": {
      "title": "Open Rank: Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor_Pediatric Emergency Medicine at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Emergency Medicine Location: 02 Posted: Oct 21, 2026 The Department of Emergency Medicine at Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons and NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital is seeking highly skilled, full-time BC/BE Pediatric Emergency Medicine faculty at the Assistant, Associate, and Professor levels to support our divisions of general and pediatric emergency medicine. The division delivers comprehensive emergency care to over 60,000 children a year at the NewYork-Presbyterian Children’s Hospital of New York at Morgan Stanley in Manhattan and at NewYork-Presbyterian Westchester in Bronxville. Candidates with a strong interest in clinical care and academic excellence are encouraged to apply. Candidates with an interest in nocturnist coverage are also encouraged to apply. NewYork-Presbyterian | Columbia is a premier academic institution with world-class clinical facilities and programs committed to excellence in patient care, research, education, and community service. NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital is ranked #1 in the NY metropolitan area and repeatedly named to the Honor Roll of “America’s Best Hospitals”; Columbia University College of Physicians & Surgeons is a top ten medical school in the nation with a superb, collaborative research environment. EM faculty enjoy the academic benefits of working in one of the country’s premiere academic health centers. Columbia University Medical Center is an internationally recognized leader in the creation of new knowledge and therapies to improve health in individuals and populations. We seek candidates with strong interpersonal and communication skills to work collaboratively within the department. Applicants must possess: MD, MD/PhD, DO degree NY state medical license NY state registered DEA Board certified or board eligible in Pediatric Emergency Medicine The annual salary range based on rank is: Assistant Professor-$281,000 - $321,000 Associate Professor- $310,080 - $350,080 Professor- $368,411 - $408,411 *Salary ranges are inclusive of a potential $40,000 nocturnist stipend.*"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195317": {
      "title": "Staff Associate (Accounting)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia Business School: Accounting Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 21, 2026 The Accounting Division at Columbia Business School welcomes applications for one or more positions at the Staff Associate I rank. Candidates are expected to contribute to the School's mission by supporting faculty-led research initiatives through research development, management, analysis, and output preparation. Individuals in this role are responsible for: Assisting in design of research models, systems, and experiments. Collecting, cleaning, and managing quantitative and qualitative data, and establishing research reproducibility Conducting literature reviews, background research, and data analysis and visualization using appropriate statistical or programming tools. Supporting preparation of research outputs such as summaries, reports, academic papers, presentation materials, and multimedia. Additional relevant duties as assigned. Highest Earned Degree Required: BA Experience or Other Special Requirements: Applicants should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in research and to institution-building contributions that advance the mission of the School. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrate strong potential for, research design, data collection and management, statistical/programming skills, and scholarly research material output. Preferred Qualifications: Applicants with two years of research experience, in college or beyond, preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195573": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Art History and Archaeology Location: 01 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Department of Art History and Archaeology at Columbia University seeks a part-time lecturer to teach one undergraduate course in the spring of 2027 with an emphasis on African art history within the Global Core program of Columbia college. This course is organized around discussion of a set of primary materials produced in these traditions and may draw from texts or other forms of media, as well as from oral sources or performance, broadly defined. The course is also expected to take a comparative or interdisciplinary approach. For more, see: https://www.college.columbia.edu/GlobalCore. PhD required. Review of applications will begin November 10. PhD must be completed by the start of the appointment. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting system (ASR/Interfolio)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195601": {
      "title": "Mary Griggs Burke Professorship in East Asian Buddhist Art History--Assistant Professor, Associate Professor (tenure-track or tenured), or Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Art History and Archaeology Location: 01 Posted: Oct 21, 2026 The Department of Art History and Archaeology at Columbia University invites applications for the Mary Griggs Burke Professorship of East Asian Buddhist Art History at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor (tenure-track or tenured), or Full Professor to begin July 1, 2027. The committee welcomes applications from candidates whose research centers on the Buddhist art of Japan, China, or Korea, and who demonstrate interests in interregional perspectives within East Asia. The department is interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to the excellence of the academic community and to sustaining and building a robust curriculum of undergraduate survey courses in the art of East Asia, graduate seminars, and to Ph.D. advising. The incumbent will also contribute to the administration of the Mary Griggs Burke Center for Japanese Art. The Ph.D. must be completed and deposited by June 30, 2027. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting system (ASR/Interfolio).Applications should include a cover-letter, CV, sample publication, sample syllabus, and names and contact information of three referees who may be asked to provide a letter at a later stage in the search. Application review will begin on November 16, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195688": {
      "title": "NSCORE Postdoctoral Research Scientist in Computational Organismal Biology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Arts and Sciences Core - Academic: Department of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology Location: 01 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The National Synthesis Center for Organismal Resilience (NSCORE) is hiring its inaugural cohort of Postdoctoral Research Scientists. Based in the Department of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology at Columbia University, these three scientists will help further NSCORE’s mission of merging organismal biology with computer science to build the field of computational organismal biology, and developing the AI tools and expertise needed to synthesize data—from molecules to individuals to ecosystems—and predict how organisms remain resilient in the face of change. Postdocs will spend half of their time working directly with one of NSCORE’s first three working groups (described below), and half of their time conducting independent synthesis science using existing organismal and environmental data under the mentorship of affiliated faculty. Applicants should demonstrate excellence in science with a strong publication record, evidence of independent scholarship, and experience working with and leading interdisciplinary teams of scientists. Postdocs will receive a generous salary, office space at NSCORE, a modest research and travel allowance, and access to compute resources. The first three working groups will explore key dimensions of organismal resilience and plasticity: 1. Behavioral flexibility . Animal movement, and the fine-scale behavior embedded within it, is one of the most sensitive and immediate expressions of organismal response to a changing environment, yet it remains one of the hardest dimensions of that response to measure consistently across species, ecosystems, and continents. Accelerometers are now deployed as a near-default complement to GPS tracking on thousands of individual organisms worldwide, and they already record fine-scale kinematic information continuously. Yet, the resulting archives have accumulated under a patchwork of sampling regimes, tag designs, and single-study aims. We also have growing archives of remote video of animal behavior, which can be analyzed in similar ways. Using billions of accelerometer records, combined with video analysis and advances in machine learning models, this group will ask whether the fine-scale behavioral signals encoded in acceleration, together with concurrent GPS records, can improve models of animal movement and provide biodiversity metrics useful for conservation and policy. This working group will be co-led by Roland Kays (North Carolina State University) and Alex Jordan (Max Planck Institute of Animal Behavior). 2. Molecular plasticity . Alternative splicing expands transcriptome diversity and gives organisms a flexible layer of gene regulation, letting genotype translate into phenotype in response to a changing environment. By refining the training of genome language models with a large public compendium of 20,000+ Arabidopsis thaliana transcriptomes that cover a range of experimentally applied perturbations, alongside several existing collections of transcriptomic variation from wild A. thaliana populations, this group aims to distinguish mechanisms of molecular plasticity that are shared across many kinds of stress from those unique to a specific pressure. In addition, the group will test how well lab-induced responses actually reflect what happens in wild populations. The result will generate a more integrative, mechanistic picture of how plants build organismal resilience, and demonstrate the application of genome language models to a broader range of organisms. This working group will be co-led by Joy Bergelson (New York University) and Itsik Pe’er (Columbia University). 3. Morphological Plasticity . Why do some forests withstand climatic extremes while others undergo lasting structural change? Part of the answer may lie in the trees themselves. Species differ in maximum attainable height, crown architecture, wood density, shade tolerance, and hydraulic strategy, and in how far they can adjust their growth and form under stress. This group will use deep learning to test whether the traits of individual trees, species identity, and community composition predict how the three-dimensional structure of a forest resists and recovers after droughts, heat waves, and storms worldwide. Global vegetation surveys and long-term forest inventories will be paired with repeat airborne laser scanning and spaceborne lidar, as well as with trait data from existing databases and herbarium collections. AI-based image analysis will extract morphological traits directly from digitized specimens. AI models trained on these data will link traits and composition to canopy architecture to track structural change responses in forests to documented climate extreme events. The group will produce a global data synthesis and a predictive framework of forest resilience that scales from single trees to whole forests. This working group will be co-led by Sheng-Feng Shen (Academia Sinica), Mukund Rao (Columbia University), Emily Sessa (New York Botanical Garden), and Brad Oberle (New York Botanical Garden). Anticipated start date between 2/2027-7/2027. Required • Ph.D. (completed by the start date of the appointment) in biology, computer science, data science, or a related field. • Experience with computational, statistical, machine learning, or AI methods applied to biological questions, or with organismal, ecological, or evolutionary data that lends itself to computational synthesis. Preferred • A strong record of productivity relative to career stage, such as first-author publications, preprints, software, datasets, or grants and fellowships. • Experience integrating large or heterogeneous datasets, for example across levels of biological organization (molecules to ecosystems), taxa, or data types. • Familiarity with tools common in comparative biology (e.g., phylogenetic comparative methods, genomics or trait databases) and/or modern ML/AI frameworks (e.g., PyTorch, foundation models, and reproducible coding practices in Python or R). • A demonstrated ability to work in, or lead, interdisciplinary and collaborative teams, particularly those that bridge biology and computer science. • Strong written and oral communication skills, including explaining technical work to audiences across disciplines. All applications must be made through Columbia University’s Academic Search and Recruiting (ASR) system. Applicants should include a 1-2 page statement of past research accomplishments that indicates how they would be a good fit for one of the three working groups detailed above. Applicants should also include a 1-2 page statement of the independent synthesis science project that they would undertake at NSCORE as a Postdoctoral Research Scientist. Finally, applicants should include a CV with publication list, as well as names and contact information for three referees who may be asked to provide letters later in the search process. Application review begins November 13th and will continue until positions are filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195890": {
      "title": "Staff Associate III",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - General Medicine Location: 02 Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Columbia University Center for Patient Safety Science (CPSS) is seeking a full-time Staff Associate III with a focus on Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Advanced Analytics. CPSS was established by Columbia University Irving Medical Center (CUIMC) to advance the science and tools necessary to achieve zero patient harm. The Center brings together multidisciplinary teams of clinicians, researchers, data scientists, statisticians, and other experts to solve critical patient safety challenges and improve the quality and safety of care for patients at CUIMC, NewYork-Presbyterian, and beyond. The successful candidate will be proactive, collaborative, and able to work effectively with minimal supervision on complex assignments. This individual will play a central role in developing and implementing machine learning and large language model (LLM)-based approaches, providing technical leadership on collaborative projects, mentoring trainees, and contributing to high-impact research initiatives. Responsibilities Lead and contribute to data analysis and AI-driven initiatives across CPSS projects. Collect, clean, preprocess, and normalize large, complex datasets from multiple sources to ensure data quality and integrity for analysis and model training. Organize, integrate, and manage data across various systems and formats. Design, build, test, and implement machine learning algorithms and predictive models using programming languages such as Python or R. Conduct exploratory data analysis and independently generate actionable insights. Coordinate with team members to ensure the accuracy and efficiency of data collection and data entry tools and processes. Prepare summary statistics, tables, figures, and reports for internal and external stakeholders. Conduct advanced data analyses and contribute to study design in collaboration with data scientists and clinician investigators. Stay current with advancements in AI, machine learning, and data analytics tools, exploring innovative methodologies to enhance workflows and drive continuous improvement. Design, implement, and lead evaluation frameworks for large language models (LLMs), including prompt engineering, benchmarking, and iterative optimization. Lead the development and implementation of AI/ML and LLM-based applications in patient safety research and serve as the technical lead on collaborative projects with internal and external partners (e.g., MCIC). Translate research methodologies into scalable tools, workflows, and operational applications. Contribute to abstracts, manuscripts, and conference presentations. Supervise and mentor medical students, data science trainees, and junior analysts. Support the onboarding and training of new data analysts and team members. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree or higher in Data Science, Computer Science, Biostatistics, or a related field, and 4-6 years of relevant experience. Ability to work on-site at Columbia University Irving Medical Center. Demonstrated experience in data analysis, machine learning, or applied AI. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Data Science, Computer Science, Biostatistics, or a related field. Proficiency in SQL and Python or R. Strong background in statistics. Demonstrated ability to independently perform exploratory and advanced data analyses. Excellent communication skills and the ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary teams. Ability to establish and maintain strong working relationships. Experience working with healthcare data. Experience with large language models (LLMs), prompt engineering, and evaluation frameworks. Experience applying AI/ML methods in healthcare or clinical research settings. Demonstrated ability to lead technical projects. Experience mentoring or supervising students, trainees, or junior staff. Track record of research dissemination through publications, abstracts, and conference presentations. Additional Requirements Strong project management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and lead workstreams. Demonstrated initiative, problem-solving abilities, and leadership in complex projects. Ability to quickly learn new skills, technologies, and data models."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195904": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Medicine: Medicine - Nephrology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Department of Medicine, Division of Nephrology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center seeks a highly motivated and experienced Senior Staff Associate I (Lab Manager) to support research focused on the genetic and molecular mechanisms of kidney disease and kidney development. The successful candidate will serve as a key member of a translational research laboratory, performing advanced molecular and tissue-based laboratory studies while coordinating day-to-day laboratory operations. This position requires a strong technical background in molecular biology techniques, laboratory workflow coordination, quality control, regulatory compliance, and research documentation. The incumbent will work under the scientific direction of the Principal Investigator while exercising considerable independence in the execution of approved experimental protocols and laboratory management activities. Responsibilities Perform molecular and tissue-based laboratory techniques, including DNA/RNA extraction, PCR-based genotyping, quantitative PCR (qPCR), gel electrophoresis, immunoblotting, histology, immunohistochemistry, immunofluorescence, and microscopy in support of kidney genetics and development research. Process, label, track, store, and maintain biological specimens while ensuring sample integrity, traceability, and accurate documentation. Maintain complete experimental records, laboratory notebooks, protocols, raw data files, and research documentation to support data integrity and reproducibility. Coordinate experimental workflows, schedule laboratory activities, allocate samples and resources, and support the execution of multiple concurrent research projects. Troubleshoot technical issues involving genotyping, nucleic acid extraction, PCR assays, immunostaining procedures, microscopy, and other laboratory methods; identify root causes and implement corrective actions. Optimize assay conditions, evaluate quality control measures, assess experimental performance, and determine when procedures should be repeated to ensure reliable and reproducible results. Independently execute approved experimental protocols, select appropriate controls, assess assay quality, and exercise technical judgment while working under the scientific direction of the Principal Investigator. Maintain laboratory inventory, prepare reagents, coordinate purchasing activities, and ensure adequate supplies are available to support ongoing research operations. Oversee routine laboratory equipment maintenance and coordinate repairs as needed to ensure operational readiness. Ensure compliance with laboratory safety requirements, institutional policies, and applicable research regulations involving biological materials and laboratory chemicals. Train and provide technical guidance to students, research personnel, and laboratory staff on laboratory procedures, equipment use, documentation standards, sample handling, and safe laboratory practices. Communicate experimental results, technical issues, and laboratory updates to the Principal Investigator and research collaborators. Support ongoing studies investigating the genetic and molecular mechanisms of kidney disease and kidney development. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Biology, Molecular Biology, Biochemistry, Genetics, Cell Biology, Biomedical Sciences, or a related scientific discipline. Minimum of 6 years of related research experience in an academic, biomedical, or laboratory research environment. Experience performing molecular biology techniques, including DNA/RNA extraction, PCR-based genotyping, quantitative PCR (qPCR), and gel electrophoresis. Experience with tissue-based laboratory techniques, including histology, immunohistochemistry, immunofluorescence, and microscopy. Demonstrated ability to work independently on complex assignments requiring initiative, analytical thinking, and sound technical judgment. Experience maintaining laboratory records, managing samples, and ensuring data quality and reproducibility. Strong organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills. Knowledge of laboratory safety practices and research compliance requirements. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Biology, Molecular Biology, Genetics, Biomedical Sciences, or a related field. 8-10 years of related laboratory research experience. Experience in kidney biology, nephrology, developmental biology, genetics, or translational research. Experience troubleshooting and optimizing molecular and tissue-based laboratory assays. Experience managing laboratory operations, including inventory, equipment maintenance, and purchasing activities. Experience training or mentoring students, technicians, or research staff. Experience assisting with research publications, grant applications, manuscripts, or other scientific documentation"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195910": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Industrial Engineering and Operations Research",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Industrial Engineering and Operations Research Location: 01 Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Columbia Engineering invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor in the Department of Industrial Engineering and Operations Research, starting July 1, 2027. Appointments will be made at the rank of Associate Professor without tenure, Associate Professor with tenure, or Professor with tenure, depending on qualifications. Exceptional candidates currently at the Assistant Professor level with a strong independent research program are also encouraged to apply. We seek candidates whose research advances the foundations, methods, and applications of modeling and decision-making under uncertainty. We particularly encourage applications from those who will expand the department's footprint in AI research, including reinforcement learning and work relevant to supply chains, service platforms, finance, and the design and operation of AI systems. Candidates must have a Ph.D. (or equivalent degree) in operations research, engineering, management science, applied mathematics, statistics, computer science, or in a closely related area by the start date of the appointment. Successful candidates are expected to build a strong research record, to contribute to the department’s educational programs, and be committed to service to the department and school. Columbia encourages multi-disciplinary research and collaborations across academic units on the campus. The candidate will benefit from interactions with various faculty from the School of Engineering and Applied Science, the Data Science Institute, Columbia Business School, Columbia Climate School, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, and the School of Arts and Science. Applicants for this position at the Associate Professor without tenure level must have engaged in pioneering research and have a history of effective teaching. Applicants for this position at the tenured level (Associate or Full Professor) must have a demonstrated record of outstanding research accomplishments, excellent teaching credentials, and established leadership in the field. For additional information and to apply, please see: https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-resources/faculty-positions . Applications should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/195910 and include the following: a curriculum vitae including a list of publications, a research statement (description of research accomplishments and planned research), a statement of teaching interests and plans, at least three letters of recommendation, and up to three pre/reprints of scholarly work. All applications received by November 27, 2026, will receive full consideration, though we encourage early application, and the position will remain open until filled. Inquiries should be directed to facultysearch@ieor.columbia.edu. Applicants can consult https://ieor.columbia.edu for more information about the department. Hiring Salary Range: Associate Professor (without tenure): $120,000-$172,000; Associate Professor (with tenure): $124,000-$183,000; Full Professor (with tenure): $135,000-$315,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195931": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medical Psychology (in Psychiatry) at CUMC - ARIA",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 Posted: Oct 22, 2026 This is an opportunity to join the Department of Psychiatry at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Assistant Professor. The Columbia University Department of Psychiatry is one of the largest in the country in terms of faculty size as well as state, federal, and foundation research support. We are currently among the top-ranked in the nation for Psychiatry in the US News & World Report Best Hospital rankings, as well as in psychiatric research funding from the National Institutes of Health. We have extraordinary clinical, educational, and research resources. Our faculty includes over 400 psychiatrists, psychologists, social workers, nurses, and neurobehavioral scientists. Clinical facilities and laboratories of the Psychiatry Department are located in a large number of institutions and healthcare systems. These include NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, the New York State Psychiatric Institute, the New York State Office of Mental Health, and the Washington Heights Community Mental Health Center. The Department of Psychiatry also houses the Center for Neurobiology and Behavior, the Mind/Brain Institute, a Howard Hughes Research Institute and the Stanley Center for Applied Neuroscience of Bipolar Disorders. Key Responsibilities: ·         Participate in research studies focused on autism spectrum disorder and related genetic conditions ·         Conduct and score neurodevelopmental and cognitive assessments for research participants and clinical patients at the Center for Autism and the Developing Brain ·         Oversee and supervise neurodevelopmental and cognitive assessments conducted by research staff ·         Train and maintain fidelity on research assessments, in collaboration with colleagues within the Columbia University Irving Medical Center, Weill Cornell Medicine, and NewYork-Presbyterian systems to maintain cross-site consistency ·         Provide evidence-based psychotherapy for anxiety, disruptive behavior, or other mental health conditions, including in the context of a primary diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder ·         Supervise and mentor clinical trainees, as well as research trainees and research staff · Minimum Qualifications: PsyD or PhD in Psychology or equivalent degree ·         Eligibility for licensure in clinical psychology (may occur after one year of employment) ·         Demonstrated experience with children with a diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder and related neurodevelopmental conditions ·         Demonstrated experience with evidence-based psychotherapies, such as Cognitive Behavioral Therapy and Parent Child Interaction Therapy ·         Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines ·         The ability to create a high-energy, results-oriented work environment that encourages collaboration and shared success"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195937": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II (Mufson Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Psychiatry Location: 02 Posted: Oct 21, 2026 The Division of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center/New York State Psychiatric Institute is dedicated to advancing the understanding, prevention, and treatment of psychiatric and neurodevelopmental disorders in children, adolescents, and young adults. The division integrates clinical care, research, and education, with a strong emphasis on interdisciplinary collaboration and evidence-based practice. DCAP supports a diverse portfolio of federally funded research, innovative clinical programs, and training initiatives aimed at improving mental health outcomes and promoting resilience across development. This full-time position is part of a PCORI-funded study comparing brief interventions (Safety Planning Intervention plus follow-up contact vs. ultra-brief Interpersonal Psychotherapy for Adolescents, crisis version) with youth aged 12-19 presenting to the emergency department for suicidal ideation and behavior. The candidate must be comfortable interacting with diverse suicidal youth and their families in a fast-paced emergency setting (with training and supervision); the ideal candidate will be a licensed clinician who has clinical experience working with adolescents in crisis settings. Candidates must be highly organized, thorough, and detail-oriented, as they will be responsible for assessing and documenting suicide risk in a complex, multi-site longitudinal clinical trial. The ideal candidate will have prior experience providing emergency assessment, clinical services, and/or crisis stabilization to at-risk adolescents from diverse ethnic, linguistic, and socioeconomic backgrounds as well as participating in the conduct of research. The licensed study clinician will be part of a research team assisting the Principal Investigator, Laura Mufson, PhD, and Co-investigator, Christa Labouliere, Ph.D., with many aspects of study implementation including: recruitment; obtaining informed consent/assent; conducting baseline and follow-up assessments; maintaining ongoing contact with participants and their families and providing support to them throughout the study period; facilitating connection to ongoing services; and facilitating engagement/information exchange between participants and the study team. The licensed clinician will assist the research team with providing psychosocial support to and maintaining continual good communication with participants and their families, as well as facilitating communication with the clinical sites at NewYork Presbyterian Hospital and external collaborators/sites. Responsibilities: Assist in identifying and assessing participant eligibility and assisting in conduct of consent/ assent meetings. Conducting clinical risk assessments of the participants at baseline and follow-up assessments. Administer the C-SSRS to participants during assessment and as needed, along with supportive safety planning and clinical risk management activities. Providing clinical risk management coverage for research staff for assessments and participant communication. Assist in implementing study interventions for participants and provide clinical care for participants post-study as needed. Assist in case management for participants to ensure connection with outpatient services after study interventions are completed. Work collaboratively with the clinical services providing the study interventions and support care of participants. Performs related duties & responsibilities as assigned/requested. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in counseling, psychology, or social work Bilingual in Spanish Excellent interpersonal, communication, and problem-solving skills based on assessment At least 1-2 years of experience (could be during training or on the job) providing clinical care to adolescents with mood and anxiety disorders Experience conducting suicide risk assessments and clinical experience with diverse adolescents Ability to effectively use Microsoft computer software (Word/Excel/Powerpoint), and REDCap Preferred Qualifications: Licensure or license eligible as a mental health clinician, such as a licensed clinical social worker (LCSW) or licensed mental health clinician (LMHC) Master’s degree in counseling, clinical psychology, or social work 1 year in a crisis setting with experience conducting suicide risk assessments using standardized measures. At least 4 years of experience preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195948": {
      "title": "Senior Faculty Position in Aerospace Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science Location: 01 Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Columbia University's Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS) invites applications for a tenured, chaired, full professor position in Aerospace Engineering. This is a senior faculty leadership role representing SEAS’ strategic commitment to expanding research and education programs in Aerospace Engineering. We seek an exceptional scholar and leader with established expertise in aerospace engineering. Areas of emphasis include, but are not limited to: space systems, materials and structures, computation, sensing, communications, space-based computing and networking, fluid dynamics, propulsion and sustainable aviation, autonomy and AI. Aerospace systems of interest range from spacecraft and satellites to commercial transportation, EVTOLS, drones, and novel flight systems. Applicants must have a demonstrated record of outstanding research accomplishments, excellent teaching credentials, and translation of fundamental research to real-world impact through collaborations with government agencies and industry. Candidates should also have demonstrated leadership capabilities for guiding the development and expansion of Aerospace Engineering programs and faculty within the school. This position is intentionally structured to maximize flexibility and attract an exceptional candidate. The appointment will be in the School of Engineering and Applied Science, with departmental affiliation(s) determined in consultation with the candidate. For additional information, please visit: http://engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-job-opportunities Applications should be submitted electronically at https://apply.interfolio.com/195948 and include the following materials: Curriculum vitae, including a comprehensive publication list Research statement describing research accomplishments and a vision for future research Statement of teaching interests, educational philosophy, and plans for mentorship Contact information for four to five references who can provide letters of recommendation Up to three preprints/reprints of scholarly work that are most representative of your research Applications received by December 15, 2026, will receive full consideration. The position will remain open until filled. Hiring 9-month Academic Year Salary Range: $167,000 - $333,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195954": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Rehabilitation and Regenerative Medicine (Physical Therapy) at CUIMC and Clinical Physical Therapist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Rehabilitation and Regenerative Medicine Location: 02 Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Columbia University Program in Physical Therapy is seeking a collaborative individual with a strong work ethic for a staff physical therapist position in a vibrant Academic Clinical Faculty Physical Therapy Practice.  This individual strives for clinical excellence and patient-centered, compassionate care. The mission of this faculty practice is to provide the best clinical practice, facilitate professional development among the physical therapy clinical and academic faculty of the future, and foster a collegial learning environment for clinicians, residents, and physical therapy students. This experienced, energetic, and team-oriented individual will: Provide clinical care to patients with a broad range of orthopedic diagnoses Efficiently execute all professional duties Remain current with emerging evidence and skills in the pursuit of lifelong learning Invest in collaborative clinical practice Communicate with respect and compassion Opportunities include: Professional growth through co-treatments and collaborations with other health professions Involvement in the Columbia University academic community, including teaching within the entry-level DPT program, mentoring an Orthopedic Physical Therapy Resident, and providing clinical instruction to physical therapy program students Participation in clinical research Faculty Practice Location(s) Columbia Physical Therapy, Tarrytown 155 White Plains Road East Building, Suite 102 Tarrytown, NY 10951 Columbia Physical Therapy, CUIMC 51 Audubon Avenue, 1 st Floor New York, NY 10032 Minimum Qualifications: Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) Must currently have or be eligible for PT licensure in New York Stat e Preferred Qualifications Clinical specialty preferred (ie, Board-certified Orthopedic Specialist) Graduate of an accredited physical therapy program BCLS Certified Member of the American Physical Therapy Association"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195990": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Pediatrics at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 Posted: Oct 21, 2026 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center’s working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. RESPONSIBLITIES Columbia University College of Physicians and Surgeons and New York-Presbyterian Hospital are recruiting for a full-time academic pediatric faculty member in the Division of Pediatric Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor  (commensurate with credentials) on the non-tenure track.  This position will be focused on the clinical care of pediatric patients undergoing stem cell transplantation. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to collaborate with the outstanding clinical and research faculty within the division and at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center, the NCI-designated Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, and the Mailman School of Public Health. Founded in 1959, The Division of Pediatric Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplant at Columbia Children’s Health, New York-Presbyterian Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital (MSCH) is one of the oldest and most comprehensive centers in the country focused on treating children with cancer and blood disorders. MSCH has 202 inpatient pediatric beds, including a 54-bed Pediatric Intensive Care Unit and 10 operating rooms. Our division is currently composed of 3 sections (Hematology, Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation) with 23 full-time faculty, 6 research RN/NP’s, 9 clinical research associates, and 3 regulatory specialists and includes an ACGME-accredited Fellowship Program and a FACT-accredited stem cell transplant program. The primary responsibility of this position is to provide excellent clinical care for children and adolescents undergoing stem cell transplantation and/or cellular therapies (such as CAR T-cells and gene therapies) for malignant and non-malignant disorders.  The ideal candidate will have experience in caring for pediatric stem cell transplant patients, with a focus on the care of patients with high risk/relapsed leukemia, as well as interest and experience in conducting clinical trials and leading other clinical research initiatives related to pediatric stem cell transplantation. Clinical responsibilities will include serving as attending physician on the inpatient HSCT service and providing outpatient care to children, adolescents and young adults for pre-HSCT intake/consultation and post-HSCT care as part of a multidisciplinary team. Research opportunities include leading and expanding clinical research efforts in pediatric stem cell transplantation, including clinical trials (PI-initiated, consortium, industry-sponsored), as well as research related to acute and late complications, patient-reported outcomes, and impact of health-care disparities. Educational responsibilities in clude training of fellows and residents, including clinical oversight, research mentorship and didactic teaching sessions. Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License Must be Board certification in Pediatric Hematology/Oncology or an equivalent board certification with experience in Stem Cell Transplantation Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. Salary Range Assistant Professor: $223,912 - $230,000 Associate Professor: $223,912 - $254,000"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196081": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 Posted: Oct 22, 2026 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Assistant Professor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers is working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. The Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons is seeking a pediatric palliative care physician to join its well-established pediatric palliative care team in the service of Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science centers, working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, NewYork-Presbyterian, at Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital. The successful candidate will join the Division of Pediatric Critical Care, Hospital, and Palliative Medicine and collaborate closely with distinguished clinical and research faculty within the division and across the medical center, contributing to a highly integrated academic and clinical environment. The palliative care team at Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital is a busy and growing consult service providing advanced pain and symptom management, psychosocial support, goals of care navigation, and advance care planning in all inpatient settings for infants, children, and young adults with medical complexity, chronic, and/or life-limiting diagnoses. Our current team is comprised of two attending physicians, one nurse practitioner, two social workers, and one chaplain. Palliative Care works closely with social work, spiritual care, child life, rehab services, creative arts, integrative medicine, and other complementary services within the Children’s Hospital to meet the needs of our patients. We also have strong relationships with hospice services in the region. We are actively seeking a board-eligible/board-certified physician in pediatrics and hospice and palliative medicine who is interested in a clinically focused academic career for a 1.0 FTE position on our team. Our mission includes clinical care, research, education, advocacy, community outreach, and coordinated care with local home services. The Department and University have a robust infrastructure to support a variety of career interests. This role provides inpatient consultative service as a member of the interdisciplinary pediatric palliative care team with a combination of in-person and remote on-call coverage. This role reports directly to the section chief of pediatric palliative care. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Direct patient care for infants, children, and young adults for whom palliative care has been consulted in our inpatient (floor, NICU, PICU) or pediatric emergency department settings within Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital; Collaboration, as indicated, in the provision of pediatric palliative care with our interdisciplinary palliative care team members; Service time shared among attending physicians in proportion to FTEs assigned; Home call to address urgent inpatient concerns shared among attending physicians in proportion to FTEs assigned; Providing education to local and visiting medical students, pediatric residents, and fellows from pediatric hematology/oncology, pediatric hospital medicine, and hospice and palliative medicine fellowship programs; Maintain strong professional communication and education among patients and families, staff, trainees, primary attendings and teams, and collaborating services; As Assistant Professor of Pediatrics you will also be expected to pursue your academic interests, publish in the peer-reviewed literature, and obtain grant funding as appropriate or necessary to continue your investigations; Academic pursuits based on candidate interests with protected time provided, which may include research studies, quality improvement, program development, educational projects, and other collaborative scholarly activities in the Department of Pediatrics and the Vagelos College of Physicians & Surgeons. Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License. Must be Board certification in in hospice and palliative medicine. Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines; The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff. Ability to work on and lead a team. Respect for diversity. Professional competence, ethical standards, and the ability to serve as a role model for other faculty, staff, and trainees."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196091": {
      "title": "Staff Associate I",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Physiology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Department of Physiology and Cellular Biophysics has an outstanding opportunity for a Staff Associate I in the Kousteni lab. In the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center and Edward P. Evans MDS Center and the Hematological Malignancies Initiative, the Kousteni lab investigates the fundamental mechanisms by which myeloid malignancies arise and develop from healthy hematopoietic stem cells. Laboratory responsibilities of this individual include receiving and processing primary samples, managing the mouse colony. The incumbent will be working with the PI and staff to contribute to research projects and publications. This person should have strong previous laboratory research experience, preferably related to hematopoiesis, cancer, and/or stem cells, some evidence of prior publications, excellent technical, communication, and organizational skills. Duties and responsibilities: o process primary blood samples, manage mouse colonies. o working closely with the PI, staff, and collaborators to contribute and support ongoing research projects, employing the following methodologies: · Human hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) isolation, genetic modification, cell culture, and molecular and functional analysis (by flow cytometry, microscopy, etc). · iPSC culture, hematopoietic differentiation. · Cloning and molecular biology, transfection, and generation of lentivirus. · Mouse colony maintenance and bone marrow transplantation. · Other lab tasks assigned as needed for individual research projects. Bachelor’s degree with at least 4 years of relevant research experience, or Master’s degree with equivalent experience. Strong technical skills in cell culture, preferably primary cell culture; flow cytometry; and animal experiments. Technical excellence and ability to follow as well as optimize protocols. Excellent oral and writing communication skills; computational skills a plus. Desire and ambition to contribute to scientific discoveries and publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196238": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Earth Institute: Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory (LDEO) Location: 04 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Dec 31, 2026 The Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory of Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for the Lamont-Brinson Postdoctoral Fellowship in the field of earthquake physics, fault structure and mechanics, and related fields. We are interested in candidates with expertise in observational and/or computational seismology, geodesy, and/or geology. Researchers that utilize AI/ML approaches are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate is expected to perform research broadly related to LDEO’s “Anticipating Earthquakes” Initiative ( https://lamont.columbia.edu/anticipating-earthquakes ) within the Seismology, Geology, and Tectonophysics Division (SGT) . The initiative aims at improving our understanding of the relationship between active faults and the earthquakes they generate. The principal selection criteria for Lamont-Brinson Postdoctoral Fellowship are scientific excellence and a clearly expressed proposal to perform research under the mentorship of one or more faculty within SGT ( https://lamont.columbia.edu/research-divisions/seismology-geology-tectonophysics ). Candidates that work on interests of multiple SGT research or teaching faculty are preferred, and are encouraged to contact potential mentors. Lamont-Brinson Postdoctoral Fellowships are supported for 12 months, include a $7,500 research allowance, and carry an annual salary of $72,116. Candidates should have recently completed their Ph.D. or should expect to complete their degree requirements by December 2026. The principal selection criteria for Lamont-Brinson Postdoctoral Fellows are scientific excellence and a clearly expressed proposal to perform research under the mentorship of one or more faculty within SGT ( https://lamont.columbia.edu/research-divisions/seismology-geology-tectonophysics ). Candidates that work on interests of multiple SGT research or teaching faculty are preferred, and are encouraged to contact potential mentors. The search will remain open for at least 30 days after the ad appears and will continue until the position is filled. The deadline for applications for the Lamont-Brinson Postdoctoral Fellowship is December 31, 2026. The appointment will start on or about July 1, 2027, and will be for one year, renewable for up to a total of two years. Columbia University benefits accompany this Officer of Research appointment. We accept online applications only."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196253": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Audiology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Otolaryngology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Department of Otolaryngology/ Head & Neck Surgery at Columbia University College of Physicians & Surgeons is seeking an Audiologist at the Assistant Professor level. The Clinical Audiologist provides comprehensive audiologic services to pediatric and adult patients in an academic medical center, with a focus on audiometric diagnostic testing, hearing aids, implantable hearing technologies, and vestibular diagnostic testing. Diagnostics will include complete audiometric evaluations, vestibular testing and electrophysiological testing. Hearing aid evaluation, dispensing and management within the center will also be expected. Focus will be with adult patients, but not limited to this population. Applicant will become part of a team of physicians and audiologists that work closely together to diagnose and treat their patients. Individuals will practice in the University hospital setting. Educational opportunities include teaching medical students, ENT residents, and, supervision of audiology student clinicians. Research opportunities in clinical setting will be available. Minimum Qualifications: Doctor of Audiology (AuD) degree. Current New York State Hearing Aid dispensing license or eligibility for licensure. Current New York State Audiology license or eligibility for licensure. Demonstrated competency (ASHA Certificate in Clinical Competency) in audiology. Strong clinical, communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary healthcare environment. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with pediatric and adult cochlear implant populations. Experience with cochlear implant evaluation, programming, and outcome assessment. Experience with hearing aids and implantable hearing technologies. Experience supervising AuD students. Experience in an academic medical center or tertiary-care setting. Participation in clinical research, quality improvement, or other scholarly activities."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196315": {
      "title": "Instructor in Pediatrics at CUMC",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Pediatrics Location: 02 Posted: Oct 24, 2026 This opportunity to join the Department of Pediatrics at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons at the rank of Instructor is an exciting one. Columbia University Irving Medical Center, one of the nation’s foremost academic health science center is working together with one of the nation’s premier health systems, New York-Presbyterian Hospital. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES The Department of Pediatrics at Columbia University Medical Center is seeking two board-certified or board-eligible (BC/BE) Pediatricians, Pediatric Intensivists, Pediatric Cardiologists, and Pediatric Cardiac Intensivists for full-time faculty positions as Pediatric Cardiac Intensive Care Unit (PCICU) Hospitalists. These positions are at the Instructor level, with appointments to Columbia University and NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital, effective July 1, 2027. The successful candidates will join a highly dedicated team providing 24/7 coverage of the PCICU at NewYork-Presbyterian Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital and Columbia Irving Medical Center, home to one of the premier pediatric cardiac programs in New York and the nation. We deliver the highest-quality care to infants, children, and adolescents with complex congenital heart disease, heart failure, and pulmonary hypertension. Key highlights of the pediatric cardiac program at NYP Morgan Stanley Children’s Hospital include: Over 500 bypass cases annually, performed by four congenital cardiothoracic surgeons. More than 1,000 cardiac catheterizations per year. One of the largest Pediatric Heart Failure/Transplant programs in the country. A world-renowned Pulmonary Hypertension Center. Expertise in electrophysiology and a variety of non-invasive cardiac imaging modalities. Our institution is also a high-volume ELSO Platinum Center of Excellence for ECMO and has extensive experience managing all types of pediatric ventricular assist devices, including the Berlin EXCOR, HeartMate 3, Total Artificial Heart, and Impella. As a PCICU Hospitalist, you will provide first-line care for critically ill patients in collaboration with a multidisciplinary team of intensivists, cardiologists, surgeons, and other specialists. Must have a MD or DO degree and an active NYS Medical License. Must be Board certification / or board Eligible in Pediatrics, Pediatric Critical Care, or Pediatric Cardiology Consistently strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build and sustain effective working relationships with professionals in other disciplines. The ability to create a high-energy results-oriented work environment that encourages colleagues and staff to value each other’s contributions and the ability to share and celebrate success with colleagues and staff."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195818": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Dermatology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 27, 2026 Position Description The Department of Dermatology at Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons of Columbia University invites applications for a postdoctoral research scientist position in the laboratory of Dr. Annemieke de Jong. The Department of Dermatology is an exciting multidisciplinary workplace with excellent resources. The de Jong lab is seeking a creative and highly motivated individual with an interest in human T cell biology and skin immunology. The successful applicant will be working in a dynamic research environment with opportunity to gain research experience and receive excellent mentoring. The position is for an immunology project that aims to better understand the functions and antigen specificities of human unconventional T cell subsets. The lab is particularly focused on CD1-restricted T cells that respond to lipid antigens rather than peptide antigens. These T cells can be activated by changes in the lipid environment that occur in inflammation, infection, and cancer, but their exact functions and regulation in homeostasis and disease remain incompletely understood. The ideal candidate will have a strong background in immunology and T cell biology. Roles and Responsibilites · Design and perform immunological assays, collect and analyze data · Isolate and phenotype human T cell populations and maintain in culture · Visualize and interpret datasets, and use to formulate new hypotheses · Work collaboratively with other members of the lab and the department · Present data in weekly lab meetings and at conferences · Contribute to manuscript preparation and fellowship applications Qualifications · Ph.D., M.D./Ph.D., M.D. in Immunology or a related field · Strong laboratory, analytical, interpersonal and communication skills. Preferred Qualifications · Competence with flow cytometry (including spectral) · Sterile cell culture methods and cell isolation techniques · Immunoassays (Elispot / ELISA) and T cell activation assays · Single cell RNA-seq/TCR-seq using platforms such as 10x Genomics · Familiarity with computational analysis of single cell GEX/TCR data · Background in T cell biology is an asset · Contribute to manuscript preparation and fellowship applications APPLICATION Applicants are requested to submit the following materials as a single PDF: · Cover letter describing research interests, experience, relevant skills, and career goals · Curriculum Vitae · Contact information for 3 references familiar with the applicant’s research and qualifications Please send complete applications to ad2952@cumc.columbia.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195165": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Electrical Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Electrical Engineering Location: 01 Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Columbia Engineering is pleased to invite applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor (without tenure, but on tenure-track), in the Department of Electrical Engineering at Columbia University in the City of New York, starting on July 1, 2027. The department welcomes applications in all areas of electrical and computer engineering. Candidates must have a Ph.D. or its professional equivalent by the starting date of the appointment. Applicants for this position must demonstrate the potential to do pioneering research and to teach effectively. The successful candidate is expected to contribute to the advancement of their field and the department by developing an original and leading externally funded research program, and by contributing to the undergraduate and graduate educational mission of the Department. Columbia fosters multidisciplinary research and encourages collaborations with academic departments and units across Columbia University. For additional information and to apply, please see: http://engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-job-opportunities . Applications should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/195165 and include the following: a curriculum vitae including a publication list, a research statement including a description of research accomplishments, a statement of teaching interests and plans, three letters of recommendation, and up to three pre/reprints of scholarly work. All applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive priority consideration, and applications received by January 15, 2027 will receive full consideration. Applicants can consult http://www.ee.columbia.edu for more information about the department. Hiring Salary Range: Assistant Professor: 113K-148K; Associate Professor (without tenure): 120K-172K"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196265": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/ Associate Professor (Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist) (Non-Tenured) (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: School of Nursing Location: 02 Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The faculty & staff of Columbia University School of Nursing welcome you to a tradition of excellence in nursing education, clinical practice & research that spans more than a century. We represent the very best in quality & innovation within an academic environment. Our focus is on building evidence for effective practice and translating that evidence-based knowledge to practice & policy environments. Located in New York City, Columbia University offers rich opportunities with an infrastructure that provides the foundation for scholarly success. Columbia Nursing is a close-knit & collaborative team that partners on research, education & clinical experiences, and shares a vision for the future of healthcare in this country. We are internationally known & clinically unrivaled with recognition for excellence in research & curricular advancements as part of one of the world’s most renowned medical centers. Our faculty thrive in our new state-of-the-art building that includes a two-story simulation center. A career with Columbia Nursing has never been so inviting! Columbia University School of Nursing seeks a dynamic Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) to join the faculty of our DNP Nurse Anesthesia Program. This open-rank, non-tenured position offers the opportunity to shape the next generation of nurse anesthetists through a combination of didactic teaching, clinical supervision, and an active clinical practice. The faculty member will teach didactic and clinical courses within the DNP Nurse Anesthesia Program and maintain an active clinical anesthesia practice, with responsibilities aligned to program needs and faculty expertise. The position includes advising and mentoring students throughout their clinical education and scholarly projects, supporting academic achievement and certification success. The faculty member will contribute to curriculum development, program evaluation, and continuous quality improvement efforts, while engaging in scholarship, professional service, and committee activities appropriate to rank. The role also requires collaboration with faculty and clinicians across the School of Nursing and Columbia University Irving Medical Center to advance the program's educational, clinical, and scholarly mission. Columbia University offers a competitive salary. This position is not benefits eligible. The selected individual will receive an academic appointment commensurate with credentials & qualifications. (This is a non-tenured faculty positions.) The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training. The above hiring range represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimated of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Successful candidates will possess: Doctoral degree (DNAP, PhD, DNP, DNSc, DrPH, or EdD) from an accredited institution Minimum of two (2) years of clinical practice experience as a CRNA Active CRNA license and national certification Active membership in AANA Exemplary written and verbal communication skills Preferred: Prior experience as a clinical or didactic instructor Experience with curriculum development or program administration Salary Range: Assistant /Associate Professor of Clincal Nursing - $101,500 - $101,500"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196384": {
      "title": "Staff Associate II",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Mailman School of Public Health: Health Policy and Management Location: Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health / Health Policy and Management Department Posted: Oct 28, 2026 The Department of Health Policy and Management at the Mailman School of Public Health is recruiting a full-time Staff Associate II with an exceptional background and skillset in behavioral sciences (ideally social psychology). This position is limited based on grant funding. The focus of the research is quantitative methods for predicting future human behavior and decision-making in major public emergencies, primarily focused on New York City. Essential Functions The Staff Associate II will be tasked with critical work on NIH (and potentially other) grants that will complex modelling of human behavior based on mathematical modeling, survey data, cell phone/foot traffic data, and LLM methods. The Staff Associate II must have excellent quantitative skills (R, Python) and general knowledge of data management in research contexts. A strong track record of exceptional and detailed interactive data dashboards for public and research management is absolutely necessary (ideally using Shiny and/or Tableau) – examples are required for the application submission. The candidate must have also completed policy training at graduate level, ideally as part of a master (or equivalent) program in behavioral, decision, or social sciences. The ideal candidate should be beginning a career in academic research. Research activities (data management, analysis, writing, survey development, etc.). -95% Other related duties assigned. - 5% Columbia University is dedicated to the goal of building a culturally diverse and pluralistic faculty and staff committed to teaching and working in a diverse environment and strongly encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Bachelor's degree in psychology, economics, or other behavioral science discipline and 4 years recommended component of training in quantitative analysis and behavioral theory (ideally linking topics such as economic policy, decision-making, and population well-being). Exceptional statistical skills that apply directly to behavioral science and public policy. Strong data visualization skills that apply directly to behavioral science and public policy, including independent development of interactive data dashboards for both analysis and research project management. Ability to work independently but also within a collaboration. Must be able to write and analyze large datasets without the need for constant supervision but also work on feedback and input from Principal Investigator. Cover Letter – maximum one page Names and contact information for three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196434": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Neurology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 29, 2026 The Department of Neurology at the Columbia University Medical Center seeks a highly qualified individual with experience with murine disease models and gene editing systems to work on projects related to Parkinson’s disease and spinocerebellar ataxia.  The candidate will be expected to manage research projects, supervise technicians and placement students, design, implement and validate research techniques, to present findings both internally and externally, to draft primary research publications. Job Responsibilities: -          Manage research projects. -          Supervise technicians and placement students. -          Design, implement and validate research techniques. -          Present findings both internally and externally. -          To draft primary research publications. Minimum Qualifications: ●        PhD in Biological Sciences, neruoscience, neurodegeneration, or related fields . ●        2 years of research activities following PhD graduation. Preferred Qualifications: ●        Research experience with mouse models of synucleinopathies and spinocerebellar ataxias ●        Use of gene modification systems using CRISPR-Cas9 or other approaches, ideally in vivo. ●        Molecular biology skills needed for the creation of custom genetic research tools ●        Record of publication of multiple first-author primary research paper in internationally circulated journals"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196436": {
      "title": "All Ranks: Assistant/Associate/Professor Neuroradiology",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Radiology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 28, 2026 The Department of Radiology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NewYork-Presbyterian is seeking a full-time faculty member to join our growing Division of Neuroradiology. Join a dynamic team at one of the top clinical, research, and teaching institutions in the country and enjoy the flexibility to work near where you live! Compensation and academic rank will vary based on credentials, expertise, and experience. Salary range starts at $475,000 - $550,000. Our radiology department offers hybrid and onsite options extending from Westchester to New York City. Our ideal candidate has experience in interpretation of all imaging modalities and familiar with vascular /stroke imaging, advanced MR imaging, routine ENT and pediatric studies. We value skilled communicators, as our neuroradiology faculty collaborate closely with world-class clinical teams to provide comprehensive care to our patients. Specific clinical and research interest in ENT, spine imaging, pediatric neuroimaging , advanced MR imaging techniques, cancer, stroke, neurodegenerative disorders, and MS are welcomed. Clinical responsibilities include coverage of inpatient and outpatient diagnostic neuroradiology CT and MRI studies and routine image-guided procedures. Although there is weekend call responsibility, there is evening and overnight neuroradiology on-call coverage by the ER division at all times. The neuroradiology division consists of 22 faculty members with a wide range of subspecialty expertise and interests. We participate in daily didactic and hands-on training of excellent radiology residents and neuroradiology fellows, engage in numerous research projects to advance the field, and hold leadership positions in local and national radiology societies. The Department of Radiology at Columbia University Irving Medical Center has more than 100 full time faculty and performs over 800,000 imaging exams each year in state-of-the-art facilities. Our hospital, New York-Presbyterian, is a tertiary and quaternary care center ranked as one of the best in the country and New York City, treating a wide range of patients from the NYC tristate area and across the world. Columbia University is an exciting hub of innovation and technology, with many opportunities to collaborate with other departments and schools. Columbia University offers a highly competitive benefits package, including college tuition and childcare reimbursement, tuition exemption for faculty members who wish to pursue graduate programs, generous retirement plans, and excellent health and dental coverage. Continuing Medical Education/conference days are apportioned by academic rank and are negotiable to fit candidate needs. More information about the department can be found on the Columbia Radiology website . To apply, please email a curriculum vitae and statement of interest to: Noor Ahmad, MD Vice Chair of Clinical Operations Department of Radiology Columbia University Irving Medical Center NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital na2884@cumc.columbia.edu Position requires M.D. or D.O. Candidate must be board certified or board eligible in Diagnostic Radiology and must be eligible to practice in the State of New York. Training and experience in neuroradiology required."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196439": {
      "title": "Medical Physicist (MS level) – Junior Diagnostic Medical Physics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Radiology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 28, 2026 The Department of Radiology at the Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons, part of Columbia University Irving Medical Center, is seeking a junior clinical diagnostic medical physicist to join our clinical medical physics group, which provides diagnostic imaging physics services to NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital and offsite locations. Join a collegial team and play a significant role in advancing medical imaging at one of the nation's top academic medical centers. The successful candidate will report directly to the Chief of Medical Physics. The salary range is $120,000 - $140,000. Compensation and academic rank will vary based on credentials, expertise, and experience. Columbia offers a generous benefits package, including college tuition and childcare reimbursement, generous retirement plans, and excellent health and dental coverage. Columbia University Irving Medical Center consists of a main campus with a 1300-bed hospital located in Manhattan, a community hospital, and several satellite medical offices. The imaging equipment consists of 23 MRI units, 21 CT scanners including 6 PET/CTs and 3 SPECT/CTs, 24 special procedure suites, 38 fixed radiography and R/F rooms, 11 digital mammography units, and a large number of mobile radiographic, mobile fluoroscopy, and ultrasound units. In total, there are more than 225 X-ray tubes. The diagnostic radiology physics group is comprised of three PhDs and three MS-level physicists, as well as three medical physics assistants. ACR accreditation is maintained for mammography, MRI, ultrasound, nuclear medicine, PET, and CT. Most of the equipment and facilities have recently been modernized. Job Responsibilities: Please note that since this opening is for a junior position, modality specific training will be provided as needed to ensure competency to perform surveys independently and successfully. The following responsibilities are listed in this context. Conduct acceptance testing, periodic diagnostic x-ray equipment testing, and quality assurance surveys across a wide range of modalities, including CT, mammography, general radiography, fluoroscopy, MRI, and ultrasound. Oversee radiographic technologist QA programs, PDM QA review, and contribute to ACR accreditation for CT, mammography, MRI, and ultrasound. Prepare and maintain equipment testing records to comply with regulatory standards from NYC-DOH, NYS-DOH, MQSA, FDA, TJC, ACR, and other pertinent bodies. Provide assistance on CT and MRI imaging protocol optimization, CT dose tracking, and MRI safety practices, including evaluation of MR-conditional devices and site safety audits. Engage in educational activities, teaching radiology residents, and training healthcare staff on, e.g., phantom scans related to equipment quality assurance. Collaborate with other departments and liaise with inspectors and regulators to maintain compliance and excellence in diagnostic imaging services. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Good understanding of the rules, guidelines, and standards of regulatory and accrediting bodies. Exceptional communication skills in all means of communication, capable of effective interaction at all levels of hospital staff across a multitude of departments. Strong interpersonal skills, essential for teamwork and problem-solving. More information about the department can be found on the Columbia Radiology website . To apply, please email a curriculum vitae and statement of interest to: Klaus A. Hamacher, PhD Associate Professor of Radiology (Physics) at CUIMC Columbia University Irving Medical Center NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital kah2212@cumc.columbia.edu Education and Experience: MS or PhD in medical physics, radiological physics, or a related field. Preferred Mammography MQSA certification. Having, or being eligible for, NYSED licensure in diagnostic radiological medical physics. Clinical experience in diagnostic imaging quality assurance, preferably in a university hospital setting. Comprehensive experience with a wide range of radiology imaging equipment, including CT, mammography, general radiography (CR/DR), fluoroscopy (II/FPD), MRI, and ultrasound. Must be fluent in MS Office, with additional programming skills preferred. Licenses and Certifications: Board certification in diagnostic medical physics by the American Board of Radiology (ABR) or at least already eligible to take part II of the ABR exam in diagnostic medical physics. If not already NYS licensed, must obtain NYS licensure in diagnostic medical physics within one year of starting. MQSA certification is preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196462": {
      "title": "Term-Limited Assistant Professor in Applied Physics",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics Location: 01 Posted: Oct 29, 2026 The Department of Applied Physics and Applied Mathematics (APAM) at Columbia University in the City of New York invites applications for a 2-year, non-tenure-track assistant professor faculty position in Applied Physics. APAM is a highly multidisciplinary department investigating fundamental math, physics, and materials science problems underlying critical applications in science and engineering, ranging from Plasma/Fusion physics to Climate dynamics. For this search, the Department is primarily interested in Candidates who can advance the goals of the recently announced Columbia-Max Planck Transatlantic Center, Optimizing Symmetry in Fusion Plasmas. The scope of the center includes research in the areas of power exhaust, edge stability, energetic particles, the simulation of transients, hybrid tokamak-stellarators, and superconducting magnets. Scope outside of these areas can be considered in exceptional circumstances. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Physics, Applied Physics, Nuclear, Aero/Astro, or related areas by the start date of the appointment. Positions at the Assistant Professor rank require a demonstrated potential for scholarly success and teaching contributions. Candidates at all levels must be able to demonstrate the ability to develop a highly successful and internationally recognized independent research program and to participate effectively in the teaching of our core applied physics, plasma physics, and/or related applied science curriculum at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Columbia encourages multidisciplinary research and collaborations across academic units on the campus. The Department is particularly interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to the diversity and excellence of the university community. Applications should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/196462 and include the following: curriculum vitae (including a list of publications), a research statement (description of research accomplishments and planned research), a statement of teaching interests and plans, three letters of recommendation from experts in the candidate's field, and up to three pre/reprints of scholarly work. All applications received by December 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. Inquiries should be directed to apam@columbia.edu. Applicants are encouraged to consult https://apam.columbia.edu/ for more information about the Department, and https://fusion.columbia.edu/ for more information about the Fusion Research Center."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196543": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor / Associate Professor / Professor of Reproductive Sciences - Women's Mental Health",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: Columbia University Irving Medical Center: Vagelos College of Physicians and Surgeons: Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: 02 Posted: Oct 29, 2026 The research-to-practice Center for the Transition to Parenthood, Dept of Obstetrics & Gynecology, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, invites applicants to apply for tenure-track with flexible start dates in 2027. For this position, we seek outstanding and innovative scientist with established or emerging research programs and success in garnering extramural funding. Candidate’s research must encompass one of the following research themes: (1) neuroscience and/or mechanistic studies in the perinatal period focused on either generation, specifically, the prenatal influences on children’s brain-behavior development or brain-behavior changes in parents-to-be or perinatal mental health; (2) implementation science that includes expertise or interest in women’s mental health, father and/or partner’s mental health, or broadly parent and child well-being in the transition to parenthood. The Center, funded by the Bezos Family Foundation, aims to generate and leverage research to transform the prenatal ecosystem towards whole-person, two-generation, equitable, prenatal care (The transition to parenthood in obstetrics: enhancing prenatal care for 2-generation impact. American Journal of Obstetrics & Gynecology MFM, 4(5), 100678). Candidate must have a Ph.D. or equivalent and will be expected to maintain an active, extramurally funded research program, as well as their independent research lab, and to mentor post-docs. There are potential opportunities to teach as adjunct faculty at Columbia or Barnard College, and, for those with a clinical degree, to work in the Center’s Women’s Mental Health @Ob/Gyn clinical service integrated in the Department’s clinical practices. Salary, start-up funds, and other considerations will be consistent with Columbia’s commitment to recruit and retain exceptional individuals. Currently, hybrid-work arrangements (2-days minimum in the office) are in effect. Applicants should apply online https://academic.careers.columbia.edu/ and submit a CV, a one-two page cover letter describing their research program, three representative publications, and three references letters. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Applications are open to US citizens, permanent residents, and non-residents eligible for appropriate work or exchange visas. Review of applications will be on a rolling basis and continue until the position are filled. Minimum Qualification: • PhD, MD or equivalent in Neuroscience, Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Developmental Psychology, Epidemiology, Public Health, Implementation Science, Maternal & Child Health, Behavioral Science, Human Development, or a closely related field. • Active extramurally funded research program"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196554": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Computer Science",
      "description": "Department: Columbia University in the City of New York: The Fu Foundation School of Engineering and Applied Science: Computer Science Location: 01 Posted: Oct 29, 2026 Columbia Engineering invites applications for one or more faculty positions in the Department of Computer Science at Columbia University in the City of New York at the tenure-track assistant professor, associate professor, or full professor level, to begin July 1, 2027. Applications are sought in all areas of computer science. Candidates must have a Ph.D. or its professional equivalent by the starting date of the appointment. Applicants at the ranks of Assistant Professor and Associate Professor (untenured, but on tenure-track) must have the potential to do pioneering research and to teach effectively. Applications at the tenured level (Associate or Full Professor) must have a demonstrated record of outstanding research accomplishments, excellent teaching credentials, and established leadership in the field. The successful candidate is expected to contribute to the advancement of their field and the department by developing an original and leading externally funded research program, and by contributing to the undergraduate and graduate educational mission of the Department. Candidates are also expected to be committed to service to the Department and to the School. Applications should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/196554 and include the following: a curriculum vitae including a publication list, a research statement including a description of research accomplishments and plans, a statement of teaching interests and plans, at least three letters of recommendation, and up to three pre/reprints of scholarly work. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled . Hiring Salary Range: Assistant Professor: 113K - 148K; Associate Professor (without tenure): 120K - 172K; Associate Professor (tenured): 124K - 183K; Professor (tenured): 135K - 315K"
    }
  },
